window.B6COURSE = {"title": "Block 6 Academy — Airframe Electrical, Comm & Nav", "subtitle": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 (Electrical) · Ch.11 (Comm/Nav) · Ch.10 (Autopilot/EFIS)", "passMark": 0.7, "storeKey": "block6-academy-v1", "modules": [{"n": 1, "day": "Unit 1", "title": "Electrical Theory & AC Fundamentals", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 (pp. 9-1 to 9-27) · Ohm's Law · AC values · reactance · impedance · power", "sections": [{"id": "b6m1s1", "title": "Ohm's Law, current, EMF, resistance & induction", "lesson": "\n<p><b>The whole block starts here.</b> Electricity in a wire behaves like <b>water\nin a pipe</b>: <b>voltage is the pressure</b> that pushes, <b>current is the flow</b>\nthat actually moves, and <b>resistance is a pinch</b> in the pipe that fights the\nflow. Ohm's Law is just the math that ties those three together.</p>\n\n<h3>Ohm's Law</h3>\n<p><b>Ohm's Law</b> describes the relationship between voltage, current, and\nresistance. It says <b>current is directly proportional to the applied voltage</b>\n(more push → more flow) and <b>inversely proportional to the resistance</b>\n(more pinch → less flow) (p. 9-1).</p>\n<p>The three forms — memorize the wheel:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>I = E &divide; R</b> &nbsp;(current = volts &divide; ohms)</li>\n<li><b>E = I &times; R</b> &nbsp;(volts = amps &times; ohms)</li>\n<li><b>R = E &divide; I</b> &nbsp;(ohms = volts &divide; amps)</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide004_1.jpeg\" alt=\"Ohm's Law wheel\"><figcaption>The Ohm's Law / power \"wheel\" — cover the value you want and the wheel shows the math.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked examples</span>\n<ul>\n<li>E = 10 V, R = 20 &Omega; &rarr; I = 10 &divide; 20 = <b>0.5 A</b>.</li>\n<li>E = 100 V, R = 20 &Omega; &rarr; I = 100 &divide; 20 = <b>5 A</b>.</li>\n<li>E = 12 V, R = 30 &Omega; &rarr; I = 12 &divide; 30 = <b>0.4 A</b>.</li>\n<li>E = 12 V, R = 1 &Omega; &rarr; I = 12 &divide; 1 = <b>12 A</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n\n<h3>Current, EMF & resistance defined</h3>\n<p><b>Current</b> is the <b>movement of electrons</b>. Its symbol is <b>I</b>; it is\nmeasured in <b>amperes (amps)</b>, symbol <b>A</b> (p. 9-1). One important pair of\nconventions: <b>electron flow</b> moves from <b>negative to positive</b>, while\n<b>conventional flow</b> is assumed from <b>positive to negative</b> — both describe\nthe same movement of mass (p. 9-1).</p>\n<p><b>Electromotive force (EMF)</b> is the electrical <b>pressure/potential</b> that\nmoves electrons. Symbol <b>E</b>, measured in <b>volts</b>. EMF is a <i>potential\ndifference (PD)</i> — it always exists <b>between two points</b> (p. 9-1).</p>\n<p><b>Resistance</b> opposes current. If the resistance of a circuit is\n<b>doubled</b> while voltage is held constant, the current is <b>cut in half</b>\n(p. 9-2). <b>Conductors</b> are materials that offer <b>low resistance</b> to\ncurrent flow.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide022_1.jpeg\" alt=\"factors affecting resistance\"><figcaption>Factors that set a conductor's resistance.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Four things set a conductor's resistance</b> (p. 9-2):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>type of conductor material</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Length</b> — resistance is <b>directly proportional</b> to length (longer wire = more R).</li>\n<li><b>Cross-sectional area</b> — resistance is <b>inversely proportional</b> to area (fatter wire = less R).</li>\n<li><b>Temperature</b> — as temperature <b>increases</b>, resistance <b>increases</b> in most materials.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Power (Watt's Law)</h3>\n<p><b>Power</b> is the rate of doing electrical work, measured in <b>watts (W)</b>.\nThe power forms: <b>P = E &times; I</b>, and by substitution <b>P = I&sup2; &times; R</b>\nand <b>P = E&sup2; &divide; R</b> (p. 9-3).</p>\n\n<h3>Electromagnetic generation of power</h3>\n<p><b>Electromagnetic induction</b> is the process of producing a voltage (EMF) by\n<b>moving a magnetic field in relationship to a conductor</b> (or moving the\nconductor through the field). If a <b>complete circuit</b> is connected to that\nconductor, the induced voltage also produces a <b>current flow</b> (p. 9-4). This is\nhow every generator and alternator makes electricity.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide023_1.jpeg\" alt=\"electromagnetic induction\"><figcaption>Moving a magnetic field past a conductor induces an EMF.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>I = E/R</b>, <b>E = I&times;R</b>, <b>R = E/I</b>. Current &prop; voltage, &prop; 1/resistance.</li>\n<li>Current <b>I</b> in amps (A) = electron movement. Electron flow <b>&minus;&rarr;+</b>; conventional flow <b>+&rarr;&minus;</b>.</li>\n<li>EMF <b>E</b> in volts = pressure/potential, measured between two points.</li>\n<li>Double R at constant E &rarr; current <b>halves</b>. Resistance &prop; length, &prop; 1/area, rises with temperature.</li>\n<li>Power <b>P = E&times;I = I&sup2;R = E&sup2;/R</b>, in watts.</li>\n<li>Electromagnetic induction = making EMF by relative motion between a magnetic field and a conductor.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Ohm's Law states that current flow in a circuit is:", "options": ["Independent of both voltage and resistance", "Inversely proportional to voltage and directly proportional to resistance", "Directly proportional to both voltage and resistance", "Directly proportional to voltage and inversely proportional to resistance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Directly proportional to voltage, inversely proportional to resistance.", "expl": "More applied voltage pushes more current; more resistance restricts it. I = E/R captures both.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Which equation correctly solves for current (I)?", "options": ["I = E ÷ R", "I = E × R", "I = R ÷ E", "I = E + R"], "correct": 0, "short": "I = E ÷ R.", "expl": "Current equals voltage divided by resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "A circuit has 10 V applied across 20 Ω. The current is:", "options": ["0.5 A", "2 A", "200 A", "5 A"], "correct": 0, "short": "0.5 A.", "expl": "I = E/R = 10 ÷ 20 = 0.5 A.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "With 100 V applied across 20 Ω, the current is:", "options": ["2,000 A", "0.2 A", "5 A", "50 A"], "correct": 2, "short": "5 A.", "expl": "I = E/R = 100 ÷ 20 = 5 A.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "12 V is applied across a 30 Ω resistor. Current equals:", "options": ["360 A", "2.5 A", "4 A", "0.4 A"], "correct": 3, "short": "0.4 A.", "expl": "I = 12 ÷ 30 = 0.4 A.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "To find resistance when voltage and current are known, use:", "options": ["R = E ÷ I", "R = I ÷ E", "R = E × I", "R = E + I"], "correct": 0, "short": "R = E ÷ I.", "expl": "Resistance = voltage divided by current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The symbol for current is I; the unit (ampere) is abbreviated:", "options": ["C", "W", "V", "A"], "correct": 3, "short": "A (ampere).", "expl": "Current uses symbol I and is measured in amperes, abbreviated A.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Electron flow is defined as movement from:", "options": ["High resistance to low resistance", "Positive to negative", "Ground to positive only", "Negative to positive"], "correct": 3, "short": "Negative to positive.", "expl": "Electron flow goes − to +; conventional flow is assumed + to −. Both describe the same movement of mass.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Conventional current flow is assumed to move from:", "options": ["Neutral to negative", "Negative to positive", "Both terminals inward", "Positive to negative"], "correct": 3, "short": "Positive to negative.", "expl": "Conventional flow: + to − (opposite of true electron flow).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Electromotive force (EMF) is measured in ____ and always exists ____.", "options": ["amps; through one point", "ohms; across a switch", "watts; in a magnetic field", "volts; between two points"], "correct": 3, "short": "Volts; between two points.", "expl": "EMF is electrical pressure/potential, in volts, and is a potential difference between two points.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "If the resistance of a circuit is doubled while voltage is held constant, the current:", "options": ["Quadruples", "Stays the same", "Doubles", "Is cut in half"], "correct": 3, "short": "Is cut in half.", "expl": "I = E/R; doubling R halves I when E is constant.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Resistance of a conductor is:", "options": ["Unaffected by length or area", "Directly proportional to length and inversely proportional to cross-sectional area", "Directly proportional to both length and area", "Inversely proportional to length and directly proportional to area"], "correct": 1, "short": "Directly proportional to length; inversely to area.", "expl": "Longer wire = more R; fatter wire (more area) = less R. Material and temperature also matter.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "As temperature increases, the resistance of most conductor materials:", "options": ["Decreases", "Increases", "Stays constant", "Drops to zero"], "correct": 1, "short": "Increases.", "expl": "In most materials resistance rises with temperature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "A conductor is best described as a material that:", "options": ["Offers high resistance to current", "Offers low resistance to current", "Stores electrostatic charge", "Blocks all current flow"], "correct": 1, "short": "Offers low resistance to current flow.", "expl": "Conductors (copper, aluminum) let current flow easily.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Electromagnetic induction is the process of:", "options": ["Opposing a change in current with back voltage", "Producing a voltage by moving a magnetic field relative to a conductor", "Storing charge on two plates separated by a dielectric", "Converting AC into DC with a diode"], "correct": 1, "short": "Producing voltage by relative motion of a magnetic field and a conductor.", "expl": "This is how generators/alternators work; a complete circuit then carries the induced current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Electrical power can be found with which expression?", "options": ["P = E × I", "P = E ÷ I", "P = I ÷ R", "P = R ÷ E"], "correct": 0, "short": "P = E × I.", "expl": "Power (watts) = volts × amps; also I²R and E²/R.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}]}, {"id": "b6m1s2", "title": "AC theory & values (cycle, frequency, wavelength)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>Direct current (DC)</b> flows one direction only. <b>Alternating current (AC)</b>\nconstantly reverses: it rises from zero to a maximum in the positive direction,\nfalls back to zero, then rises to a maximum in the negative direction and returns\nto zero — over and over, tracing a <b>sine wave</b> (p. 9-5).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide031_1.png\" alt=\"AC sine wave\"><figcaption>One AC sine wave: zero &rarr; +peak &rarr; zero &rarr; &minus;peak &rarr; zero.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>The three AC values</h3>\n<p>Three values apply to both AC voltage and current and describe the sine wave\n(p. 9-5):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Instantaneous</b> — the value at any one instant (changes constantly).</li>\n<li><b>Peak</b> — the maximum value reached (the top of the wave).</li>\n<li><b>Effective (RMS)</b> — the AC value that does the same work as an equal DC\nvalue. <b>Effective = 0.707 &times; peak</b>, and <b>peak = 1.414 &times; effective</b>\n(p. 9-6). Household and aircraft AC voltages are given as effective (RMS) values.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Cycle, alternation, period, frequency, wavelength</h3>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide033_1.png\" alt=\"AC cycle\"><figcaption>A cycle is one complete set of changes back to the start; an alternation is one half of that.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Cycle</b> — when a voltage or current goes through a full series of changes,\nreturns to the starting point, and begins again, that series is one <b>cycle</b> (p. 9-5).</li>\n<li><b>Alternation</b> — simply <b>one-half of a cycle</b> (p. 9-5).</li>\n<li><b>Period</b> — the <b>time required for one full cycle</b>. Period is\n<b>inversely proportional to frequency</b>: the higher the frequency, the\n<b>shorter</b> the period (p. 9-6).</li>\n<li><b>Frequency</b> — the <b>number of cycles per second</b>, measured in\n<b>hertz (Hz)</b>. 10 cycles per second = <b>10 Hz</b> (p. 9-6).</li>\n<li><b>Wavelength</b> — the <b>distance a waveform travels during one period</b>,\nindicated by the Greek letter <b>lambda (&lambda;)</b> (p. 9-6).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide036_1.png\" alt=\"frequency\"><figcaption>Frequency = cycles per second (Hz).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>AC values: <b>instantaneous, peak, effective</b>. Effective (RMS) = <b>0.707 &times; peak</b>; peak = <b>1.414 &times; effective</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Cycle</b> = one full set of changes; <b>alternation</b> = one-half cycle.</li>\n<li><b>Period</b> = time for one cycle, <b>inversely</b> proportional to frequency.</li>\n<li><b>Frequency</b> = cycles per second in <b>hertz (Hz)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Wavelength (&lambda;)</b> = distance the waveform travels in one period.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Alternating current is best described as current that:", "options": ["Changes direction at regular intervals, rising and falling through a sine wave", "Flows in one direction only", "Never returns to zero", "Has a constant, unchanging value"], "correct": 0, "short": "Reverses direction at regular intervals (a sine wave).", "expl": "AC rises 0→+peak→0→−peak→0 repeatedly. DC flows one way only.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The three values of AC that apply to both voltage and current are:", "options": ["Resistive, inductive, and capacitive", "Series, parallel, and total", "Positive, negative, and neutral", "Instantaneous, peak, and effective"], "correct": 3, "short": "Instantaneous, peak, and effective.", "expl": "These three describe the sine wave.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The effective (RMS) value of AC equals:", "options": ["2 × peak", "0.5 × peak", "0.707 × peak", "1.414 × peak"], "correct": 2, "short": "0.707 × peak.", "expl": "RMS = 0.707 × peak; conversely peak = 1.414 × RMS. RMS does the same work as equal DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Peak voltage equals effective (RMS) voltage multiplied by:", "options": ["0.707", "3.1416", "1.0", "1.414"], "correct": 3, "short": "1.414.", "expl": "Peak = 1.414 × RMS.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "One complete set of AC changes that returns to the starting point is called a:", "options": ["Wavelength", "Period", "Cycle", "Alternation"], "correct": 2, "short": "Cycle.", "expl": "A full series of changes returning to start = one cycle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "An alternation is:", "options": ["Two cycles", "One-half of a cycle", "The peak value", "One full cycle"], "correct": 1, "short": "One-half of a cycle.", "expl": "An alternation = half a cycle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The period of a sine wave is:", "options": ["The distance the wave travels", "The maximum value reached", "The number of cycles per second", "The time required for one full cycle"], "correct": 3, "short": "The time for one full cycle.", "expl": "Period is time-per-cycle and is inversely proportional to frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Period and frequency are related how?", "options": ["Always equal", "Unrelated", "Directly proportional", "Inversely proportional"], "correct": 3, "short": "Inversely proportional.", "expl": "Higher frequency → shorter period.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Frequency is defined as:", "options": ["The peak-to-peak voltage", "The distance between two waves", "The number of cycles in one second", "The time for one cycle"], "correct": 2, "short": "Number of cycles per second.", "expl": "Measured in hertz (Hz).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Frequency is measured in:", "options": ["Volts", "Hertz", "Ohms", "Farads"], "correct": 1, "short": "Hertz (Hz).", "expl": "10 cycles per second = 10 Hz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "If a waveform completes 10 cycles per second, its frequency is:", "options": ["1 Hz", "0.1 Hz", "10 Hz", "100 Hz"], "correct": 2, "short": "10 Hz.", "expl": "Cycles per second = hertz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Wavelength is:", "options": ["The number of cycles per second", "The distance a waveform travels during one period", "The time for one cycle", "The peak value of the wave"], "correct": 1, "short": "Distance the waveform travels in one period.", "expl": "Indicated by lambda (λ).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Wavelength is indicated by which Greek letter?", "options": ["Sigma (Σ)", "Pi (π)", "Omega (Ω)", "Lambda (λ)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Lambda (λ).", "expl": "λ symbolizes wavelength; Ω is ohms, π is pi.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The 'instantaneous' value of AC is:", "options": ["Always zero", "The value at any single instant in time", "The maximum the wave reaches", "The same as the RMS value"], "correct": 1, "short": "The value at one instant.", "expl": "It changes continuously along the sine wave.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}]}, {"id": "b6m1s3", "title": "Inductance & inductive reactance (Xₗ)", "lesson": "\n<p>In an AC circuit, opposition to current comes from more than plain resistance.\nTwo AC-only oppositions appear: <b>inductive reactance</b> (from inductors/coils)\nand <b>capacitive reactance</b> (from capacitors). This section covers inductance.</p>\n\n<h3>What an inductor does</h3>\n<p>An <b>inductor</b> is a coil of wire. When the <b>current through a conductor\nchanges</b>, its <b>magnetic field expands and collapses</b> accordingly (p. 9-7).\nThat moving field induces a voltage back into the coil.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide040_1.png\" alt=\"inductor\"><figcaption>An inductor: a coil whose changing current builds and collapses a magnetic field.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Lenz's Law and back voltage (CEMF)</h3>\n<p><b>Lenz's Law</b>: the voltage induced into the conductor has a polarity that\n<b>opposes the change that caused it</b> (p. 9-7). This induced opposing voltage is\ncalled <b>counter-electromotive force (CEMF)</b>, commonly called <b>back\nvoltage</b>. It is this back voltage that is the <b>resistive force in an inductive\ncircuit</b> (p. 9-7).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide043_1.jpeg\" alt=\"Lenz's law\"><figcaption>Lenz's Law: the induced voltage opposes the change that produced it.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Inductors in series and parallel</h3>\n<p>The <b>unit of inductance is the henry (H)</b>. Inductors combine like resistors\n(p. 9-7):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series</b> — total inductance = <b>sum</b> of the individual inductors\n(L<sub>T</sub> = L1 + L2 + ...), assuming their fields don't interact.</li>\n<li><b>Parallel</b> — computed like <b>resistors in parallel</b> (reciprocal method).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Inductive reactance (X<sub>L</sub>)</h3>\n<p><b>Inductive reactance</b> is the coil's opposition to changing current, measured\nin <b>ohms</b>. The formula:</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Formula</span>\n<b>X<sub>L</sub> = 2&pi;fL</b> &nbsp; where &pi; = 3.1416, f = frequency in Hz,\nL = inductance in henrys (p. 9-8).</div>\n<p>Because f is on top, <b>higher frequency &rarr; more inductive reactance</b>. A\ncoil fights fast changes hard and slow changes easily.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Changing current &rarr; expanding/collapsing magnetic field &rarr; induced voltage.</li>\n<li><b>Lenz's Law</b>: induced voltage opposes the change causing it &rarr; <b>CEMF / back voltage</b> = the resistive force in an inductive circuit.</li>\n<li>Inductance unit = <b>henry (H)</b>. Series adds; parallel like resistors in parallel.</li>\n<li><b>X<sub>L</sub> = 2&pi;fL</b>, in ohms. Frequency up &rarr; X<sub>L</sub> up.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "When the current through a conductor changes, its magnetic field:", "options": ["Expands and collapses", "Disappears permanently", "Stays fixed", "Reverses the current"], "correct": 0, "short": "Expands and collapses.", "expl": "A changing current makes a changing field, which induces voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Lenz's Law states that the induced voltage has a polarity that:", "options": ["Aids the change that caused it", "Doubles the source voltage", "Is always zero", "Opposes the change that caused it"], "correct": 3, "short": "Opposes the change that caused it.", "expl": "This opposing induced voltage is CEMF (back voltage).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The counter-EMF (CEMF) in an inductor is commonly called:", "options": ["Back voltage", "Forward voltage", "Peak voltage", "Ripple voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Back voltage.", "expl": "CEMF opposes the source; it is the resistive force in an inductive circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "In an inductive circuit, the resistive (opposing) force is provided by:", "options": ["The slip rings", "The commutator", "The back voltage (CEMF)", "Dielectric stress"], "correct": 2, "short": "The back voltage (CEMF).", "expl": "CEMF from the collapsing/expanding field opposes current change.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The unit of inductance is the:", "options": ["Farad", "Ohm", "Henry", "Hertz"], "correct": 2, "short": "Henry (H).", "expl": "Inductance is measured in henrys.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Inductors connected in series have a total inductance equal to:", "options": ["The largest inductor only", "The reciprocal of the sum", "The sum of the individual inductors", "The product over the sum"], "correct": 2, "short": "The sum of the individual inductors.", "expl": "Series inductors add, like resistors in series.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Inductors connected in parallel are calculated:", "options": ["By simple addition", "Like resistors in parallel", "By subtraction", "Like resistors in series"], "correct": 1, "short": "Like resistors in parallel.", "expl": "Use the reciprocal method, just like parallel resistors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The formula for inductive reactance is:", "options": ["Xₗ = 1 ÷ (2πfL)", "Xₗ = 2πfL", "Xₗ = √(R² + L²)", "Xₗ = 2πfC"], "correct": 1, "short": "Xₗ = 2πfL.", "expl": "f in Hz, L in henrys, π = 3.1416; result in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "In Xₗ = 2πfL, the term L is expressed in:", "options": ["Ohms", "Henrys", "Hertz", "Farads"], "correct": 1, "short": "Henrys.", "expl": "L = inductance in henrys; f = frequency in Hz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "As frequency increases, inductive reactance (Xₗ):", "options": ["Decreases", "Increases", "Stays the same", "Becomes negative"], "correct": 1, "short": "Increases.", "expl": "f is in the numerator of 2πfL, so Xₗ rises with frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Inductive reactance is measured in:", "options": ["Farads", "Watts", "Ohms", "Henrys"], "correct": 2, "short": "Ohms.", "expl": "Like resistance, reactance is an opposition measured in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "What component provides inductance in a circuit?", "options": ["An inductor (coil)", "A resistor", "A capacitor", "A diode"], "correct": 0, "short": "An inductor (coil of wire).", "expl": "Inductance comes from a coil's magnetic field.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}]}, {"id": "b6m1s4", "title": "Capacitance & capacitive reactance (X_C)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>Capacitance</b> is an AC property besides resistance and inductance. It is the\nability to <b>store an electric charge</b>, and the component that provides it is the\n<b>capacitor</b> (p. 9-9).</p>\n\n<h3>Units of capacitance</h3>\n<p>The basic unit is the <b>farad (F)</b>. The two common smaller units are the\n<b>microfarad (&micro;F) = 10<sup>&minus;6</sup> F</b> and the <b>picofarad (pF) =\n10<sup>&minus;12</sup> F</b> (p. 9-9).</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Slide vs. handbook</span> Matthew's class slide states the\nmicrofarad is \"10 to the negative fifth\" — that is a typo. The correct value is\n<b>10<sup>&minus;6</sup></b> (one-millionth). The picofarad (10<sup>&minus;12</sup>)\nis correct. Learn the FAA values.</div>\n\n<h3>How a capacitor works</h3>\n<p>A capacitor is two conductive <b>plates</b> separated by an insulator called the\n<b>dielectric</b>. One plate connects to the source's <b>positive</b> terminal, the\nother to the <b>negative</b>. Electrons are drawn to the plate at the positive\nterminal and flow away from the plate at the negative terminal. <b>No current flows\nacross the dielectric</b>, yet the plates become <b>electrostatically charged</b>\n(p. 9-9).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide051_1.png\" alt=\"capacitor operation\"><figcaption>Plates charge electrostatically; no current crosses the dielectric.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>A capacitor stores energy in <b>two ways</b> (p. 9-10):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Through the <b>electrostatic attraction across the dielectric</b>.</li>\n<li>Through the <b>distortion of the electron orbits</b> of the atoms in the\ndielectric material. This distortion is called <b>dielectric stress</b>, which\nitself stores electrostatic charge.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Capacitors in series and parallel</h3>\n<p>Capacitors combine <b>opposite</b> to resistors (p. 9-10):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series</b> — calculated like <b>resistors in parallel</b> (reciprocal); total\ncapacitance is <b>reduced</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Parallel</b> — like <b>adding plate area</b>, so total capacitance is the\n<b>sum</b> and the circuit's total capacity is <b>increased</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Capacitive reactance (X<sub>C</sub>)</h3>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Formula</span>\n<b>X<sub>C</sub> = 1 &divide; (2&pi;fC)</b> &nbsp; where &pi; = 3.1416, f = frequency\nin Hz, C = capacitance in farads. It is a resistive force (opposition), measured in\n<b>ohms (&Omega;)</b> (p. 9-11).</div>\n<p>Because f and C are on the bottom, <b>higher frequency &rarr; less capacitive\nreactance</b> — the opposite behavior of an inductor.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Capacitance unit = <b>farad (F)</b>; &micro;F = 10<sup>&minus;6</sup>, pF = 10<sup>&minus;12</sup>.</li>\n<li>Plates charge <b>electrostatically</b>; no current crosses the <b>dielectric</b>. Distortion of dielectric orbits = <b>dielectric stress</b>.</li>\n<li>Capacitors in <b>series</b> = like resistors in parallel; <b>parallel</b> = add (increases total capacitance).</li>\n<li><b>X<sub>C</sub> = 1/(2&pi;fC)</b>, in ohms. Frequency up &rarr; X<sub>C</sub> down.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Capacitance is the ability to:", "options": ["Generate a magnetic field", "Convert AC to DC", "Store an electric charge", "Oppose a change in current"], "correct": 2, "short": "Store an electric charge.", "expl": "The capacitor provides capacitance by storing charge on its plates.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The basic unit of capacitance is the:", "options": ["Ohm", "Farad", "Coulomb", "Henry"], "correct": 1, "short": "Farad (F).", "expl": "Given the symbol F.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "A picofarad (pF) equals:", "options": ["10⁶ F", "10⁻⁶ F", "10⁻³ F", "10⁻¹² F"], "correct": 3, "short": "10⁻¹² F.", "expl": "pF = one-trillionth of a farad; µF = 10⁻⁶.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "A microfarad (µF) correctly equals:", "options": ["10⁻¹² F", "10⁻⁵ F", "10⁻⁶ F", "10⁶ F"], "correct": 2, "short": "10⁻⁶ F.", "expl": "The handbook value is 10⁻⁶ (one-millionth). A class slide's '10⁻⁵' is a typo.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Inside a charging capacitor, current flow across the dielectric is:", "options": ["Zero — no current crosses the dielectric", "Reversed", "Equal to the source current", "Very large"], "correct": 0, "short": "Zero — no current crosses the dielectric.", "expl": "The plates charge electrostatically; the dielectric is an insulator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The insulating material between a capacitor's plates is the:", "options": ["Armature", "Commutator", "Dielectric", "Electrolyte"], "correct": 2, "short": "Dielectric.", "expl": "It separates the two plates and undergoes dielectric stress when charged.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The distortion of the electron orbits within a capacitor's dielectric is called:", "options": ["Dielectric stress", "Impedance", "Hysteresis", "Back voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Dielectric stress.", "expl": "This distortion also stores electrostatic charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Capacitors connected in series are calculated:", "options": ["Like resistors in parallel", "By multiplying only", "By simple addition", "Like resistors in series"], "correct": 0, "short": "Like resistors in parallel.", "expl": "Series capacitance uses the reciprocal method; total capacitance is reduced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Connecting capacitors in parallel:", "options": ["Has no effect on capacitance", "Reduces total capacitance", "Increases total capacitance (like adding plate area)", "Converts them to inductors"], "correct": 2, "short": "Increases total capacitance.", "expl": "Parallel is like adding plate area, so the total capacity goes up.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The formula for capacitive reactance is:", "options": ["X_C = √(R² + C²)", "X_C = 1 ÷ (2πfC)", "X_C = 2πfL", "X_C = 2πfC"], "correct": 1, "short": "X_C = 1 ÷ (2πfC).", "expl": "f in Hz, C in farads; result in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "As frequency increases, capacitive reactance (X_C):", "options": ["Increases", "Becomes infinite", "Stays the same", "Decreases"], "correct": 3, "short": "Decreases.", "expl": "f is in the denominator of 1/(2πfC), so X_C falls as frequency rises — opposite of an inductor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Capacitive reactance is measured in:", "options": ["Ohms", "Farads", "Hertz", "Henrys"], "correct": 0, "short": "Ohms.", "expl": "It is an opposition (resistive force) to current, measured in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}]}, {"id": "b6m1s5", "title": "Impedance, power factor & AC generation", "lesson": "\n<p>Put resistance and both reactances together and you get <b>impedance</b> — plus a\nnew way of measuring power in AC.</p>\n\n<h3>Impedance (Z)</h3>\n<p>An AC circuit is opposed by <b>resistance (R)</b>, <b>inductance (L)</b>, and\n<b>capacitance (C)</b>. A circuit containing all three is called an <b>RCL\ncircuit</b> (p. 9-12). The <b>combined effect</b> of resistance, inductive\nreactance, and capacitive reactance is <b>impedance</b>, symbol <b>Z</b>, measured\nin <b>ohms (&Omega;)</b> (p. 9-12).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide058_1.png\" alt=\"RCL impedance\"><figcaption>An RCL circuit combines R, X<sub>L</sub>, and X<sub>C</sub>.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>You <b>cannot</b> simply add the oppositions, because <b>X<sub>L</sub> and\nX<sub>C</sub> act in opposite directions</b>. First find the <b>net reactance</b>\n(X<sub>L</sub> &minus; X<sub>C</sub>), then use a derivative of the <b>Pythagorean\ntheorem</b> (p. 9-12):</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Formula</span>\n<b>Z = &radic;(R&sup2; + (X<sub>L</sub> &minus; X<sub>C</sub>)&sup2;)</b> for a series\nAC circuit. Parallel AC circuits use a related derivative of the Pythagorean\ntheorem.</div>\n<div class=\"box ex\"><span class=\"bt\">Worked example</span>\nX<sub>C</sub> = 7 &Omega;, X<sub>L</sub> = 10 &Omega;, R = 4 &Omega;. Net reactance =\n10 &minus; 7 = 3 &Omega;. Z = &radic;(4&sup2; + 3&sup2;) = &radic;(16 + 9) =\n&radic;25 = <b>5 &Omega;</b>.</div>\n\n<h3>Power in an AC circuit</h3>\n<p>AC power depends on the applied voltage <b>and</b> both the circuit's resistance\nand reactance. Three terms (p. 9-13):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Apparent Power (AP)</b> = <b>E &times; I</b>, measured in <b>volt-amps (VA)</b>.\nIt is the power consumed by the <b>entire circuit</b>, including resistance and\nreactance.</li>\n<li><b>True Power (TP)</b> = the <b>actual</b> power when phase is taken into\naccount = <b>E &times; I &times; power factor</b>, measured in <b>watts</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Power Factor (PF)</b> = the <b>ratio of True Power to Apparent Power</b> =\nwatts &divide; (volts &times; amps). As a percent: <b>PF = 100 &times; TP &divide; AP</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide065_1.jpeg\" alt=\"power in AC circuit\"><figcaption>True vs. apparent power and the power factor.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>In a <b>purely resistive circuit</b> (no reactance), the <b>phase angle is 0&deg;</b>,\nthe <b>power factor is 1</b>, and <b>True Power equals Apparent Power</b> (p. 9-13).</p>\n\n<h3>Generating AC</h3>\n<p>In a basic AC generator the ends of the rotating loop connect to <b>two slip\n(collector) rings</b> (C1 and C2), and current is taken from those rings by\n<b>brushes</b> (p. 9-14).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide070_1.png\" alt=\"AC generation slip rings\"><figcaption>AC generator loop ends terminate at two slip rings; brushes pick off the current.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Impedance <b>Z</b> (ohms) = combined R + X<sub>L</sub> + X<sub>C</sub>; <b>RCL</b> circuit has all three.</li>\n<li>Net reactance = <b>X<sub>L</sub> &minus; X<sub>C</sub></b>; series <b>Z = &radic;(R&sup2; + (X<sub>L</sub>&minus;X<sub>C</sub>)&sup2;)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>AP = E&times;I</b> (volt-amps); <b>TP = E&times;I&times;PF</b> (watts); <b>PF = TP/AP</b>.</li>\n<li>Purely resistive circuit: phase angle <b>0&deg;</b>, PF <b>= 1</b>, TP = AP.</li>\n<li>AC generator uses <b>two slip (collector) rings</b> and <b>brushes</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A circuit containing resistance, inductance, and capacitance together is called a(n):", "options": ["Open circuit", "RCL circuit", "Series-only circuit", "Purely resistive circuit"], "correct": 1, "short": "RCL circuit.", "expl": "It combines R, L, and C oppositions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Impedance (Z) is:", "options": ["Resistance only", "The number of cycles per second", "The same as apparent power", "The combined effect of resistance, inductive reactance, and capacitive reactance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Combined R, Xₗ, and X_C.", "expl": "Z is the total opposition in an AC circuit, measured in ohms.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Impedance is represented by the symbol ____ and measured in ____.", "options": ["P; watts", "E; volts", "I; amps", "Z; ohms"], "correct": 3, "short": "Z; ohms.", "expl": "Impedance symbol Z, unit ohm.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Why can't you simply add R, Xₗ, and X_C to get impedance?", "options": ["Impedance ignores resistance", "Resistance is always zero in AC", "They are measured in different units", "Xₗ and X_C have opposite effects, so you must find the net reactance first"], "correct": 3, "short": "Xₗ and X_C oppose each other — find net reactance first.", "expl": "Net reactance = Xₗ − X_C, then combine with R using the Pythagorean derivative.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The series-circuit impedance formula is:", "options": ["Z = 2πfL", "Z = R + Xₗ + X_C", "Z = E × I", "Z = √(R² + (Xₗ − X_C)²)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Z = √(R² + (Xₗ − X_C)²).", "expl": "A derivative of the Pythagorean theorem.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "For X_C = 7 Ω, Xₗ = 10 Ω, R = 4 Ω, the impedance Z equals:", "options": ["21 Ω", "11 Ω", "5 Ω", "3 Ω"], "correct": 2, "short": "5 Ω.", "expl": "Net X = 10−7 = 3; Z = √(4²+3²) = √25 = 5 Ω.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC", "fig": "img/slide063_1.png"}, {"q": "Apparent power (AP) in an AC circuit is found by:", "options": ["E ÷ I", "E × I × power factor", "E × I", "I² × R only"], "correct": 2, "short": "E × I.", "expl": "Apparent power = volts × amps, measured in volt-amps (VA).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Apparent power is measured in:", "options": ["Volt-amps", "Watts", "Ohms", "Farads"], "correct": 0, "short": "Volt-amps (VA).", "expl": "AP includes both resistance and reactance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "True power (TP) is calculated as:", "options": ["I × R", "E × I × power factor", "E × I", "E ÷ I"], "correct": 1, "short": "E × I × power factor.", "expl": "True power is the actual power with phase considered, in watts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "True power is expressed in:", "options": ["Watts", "Volt-amps", "Ohms", "Hertz"], "correct": 0, "short": "Watts.", "expl": "True power = the real power doing work, in watts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Power factor is defined as the ratio of:", "options": ["Resistance to reactance", "Apparent power to true power", "Voltage to current", "True power to apparent power"], "correct": 3, "short": "True power to apparent power.", "expl": "PF = TP ÷ AP = watts ÷ (volts × amps).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "In a purely resistive AC circuit (no reactance), the power factor is:", "options": ["1", "1.414", "0.707", "0"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.", "expl": "Phase angle is 0°, so true power equals apparent power and PF = 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "In a purely resistive AC circuit, the phase angle is:", "options": ["45°", "0°", "180°", "90°"], "correct": 1, "short": "0°.", "expl": "No reactance means voltage and current are in phase (0°), PF = 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "In a basic AC generator, the ends of the rotating loop connect to:", "options": ["A carbon pile", "The field frame", "Two slip (collector) rings", "A single commutator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Two slip (collector) rings.", "expl": "Brushes ride the slip rings (C1, C2) to take off the AC. A commutator is for DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC", "fig": "img/slide070_1.png"}]}]}, {"n": 2, "day": "Unit 2", "title": "Aircraft Batteries", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · primary/secondary cells · lead-acid · NiCd · charging · maintenance", "sections": [{"id": "b6m2s1", "title": "Battery fundamentals & capacity", "lesson": "\n<p>A <b>battery</b> is a group of <b>cells</b> connected together that store chemical\nenergy and release it as electricity. The <b>cell is the basic unit</b> of any\nbattery.</p>\n\n<h3>Primary vs. secondary cells</h3>\n<p>There are two types of cells (p. 9-15):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Primary cell</b> — its active material <b>cannot be restored (recharged)</b>.\nWhen it is used up, the cell is discarded (like a flashlight battery).</li>\n<li><b>Secondary cell</b> — using electricity from an <b>external source</b>, the\nactive material <b>can be restored</b> to its original (charged) condition. These\nare <b>rechargeable</b>, and are what aircraft use.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The two main secondary types on aircraft are <b>lead-acid</b> and\n<b>nickel-cadmium (NiCd)</b> (p. 9-15).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide072_1.jpeg\" alt=\"aircraft battery\"><figcaption>An aircraft storage battery — a group of secondary (rechargeable) cells.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Battery capacity (ampere-hours)</h3>\n<p><b>Capacity</b> is measured quantitatively in <b>ampere-hours</b> — the current a\nbattery can deliver, over time, at a <b>specified discharge rate</b> down to a\n<b>specified cut-off voltage</b>, at <b>room temperature</b> (p. 9-16).</p>\n<p>The <b>one-hour rate</b> is the discharge rate a battery can sustain for\n<b>1 hour</b> while its voltage stays <b>at or above</b> (p. 9-16):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>1.67 volts per cell</b>, or</li>\n<li><b>20 volts</b> for a 24-volt lead-acid battery, or</li>\n<li><b>10 volts</b> for a 12-volt lead-acid battery.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Nominal cell voltages</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>lead-acid</b> cell produces approximately <b>2.1 volts</b>.</li>\n<li>A <b>nickel-cadmium</b> cell delivers approximately <b>1.2 volts</b> during discharge.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Primary</b> cell = not rechargeable; <b>secondary</b> cell = rechargeable (aircraft use secondary).</li>\n<li>Aircraft secondary types: <b>lead-acid</b> and <b>nickel-cadmium (NiCd)</b>.</li>\n<li>Capacity = <b>ampere-hours</b> at a specified discharge rate/cut-off voltage at room temperature.</li>\n<li><b>One-hour rate</b>: 1 hour at &ge; <b>1.67 V/cell</b> (20 V for a 24-V, 10 V for a 12-V lead-acid).</li>\n<li>Cell voltage: lead-acid <b>2.1 V</b>, NiCd <b>1.2 V</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A primary cell is one whose active material:", "options": ["Is made of nickel and cadmium", "Can be recharged from an external source", "Cannot be restored (recharged)", "Delivers 2.1 volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "Cannot be restored/recharged.", "expl": "Primary cells are single-use; secondary cells are rechargeable.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A secondary cell is defined by the fact that:", "options": ["Its active material cannot be restored", "It produces only 1.2 volts", "It contains no electrolyte", "Electricity from an external source can restore its active material"], "correct": 3, "short": "External electricity can restore (recharge) it.", "expl": "Secondary = rechargeable; aircraft batteries are secondary.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The two main types of secondary (rechargeable) aircraft batteries are:", "options": ["Carbon-zinc and alkaline", "Lithium and mercury", "Lead-acid and nickel-cadmium", "Primary and dry"], "correct": 2, "short": "Lead-acid and nickel-cadmium.", "expl": "These are the two secondary types used on aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The basic unit of any battery is the:", "options": ["Terminal", "Plate", "Separator", "Cell"], "correct": 3, "short": "Cell.", "expl": "Cells are connected together to form a battery.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Battery capacity is measured quantitatively in:", "options": ["Volts", "Ampere-hours", "Watts", "Ohms"], "correct": 1, "short": "Ampere-hours.", "expl": "Ampere-hours at a specified discharge rate/cut-off voltage at room temperature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Ampere-hour capacity is specified at a given discharge rate down to a specified cut-off voltage at:", "options": ["Maximum charge temperature", "Room temperature", "Freezing temperature", "Any temperature"], "correct": 1, "short": "Room temperature.", "expl": "Capacity ratings assume room temperature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The one-hour rate is the discharge a battery can sustain for 1 hour while cell voltage stays at or above:", "options": ["2.1 volts per cell", "1.67 volts per cell", "2.35 volts per cell", "1.2 volts per cell"], "correct": 1, "short": "1.67 volts per cell.", "expl": "Or 20 V for a 24-V, or 10 V for a 12-V lead-acid battery.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "At the one-hour rate, a 24-volt lead-acid battery voltage must stay at or above:", "options": ["24 volts", "12 volts", "20 volts", "10 volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "20 volts.", "expl": "24-V battery → 20 V floor; 12-V battery → 10 V floor; also 1.67 V/cell.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "At the one-hour rate, a 12-volt lead-acid battery must stay at or above:", "options": ["10 volts", "5 volts", "20 volts", "12 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "10 volts.", "expl": "12-V lead-acid floor is 10 V at the one-hour rate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A single lead-acid cell produces approximately:", "options": ["2.1 volts", "2.35 volts", "1.2 volts", "1.5 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "2.1 volts.", "expl": "Lead-acid ≈ 2.1 V/cell; NiCd ≈ 1.2 V/cell.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A single nickel-cadmium cell delivers a nominal discharge voltage of about:", "options": ["1.67 volts", "1.2 volts", "2.35 volts", "2.1 volts"], "correct": 1, "short": "1.2 volts.", "expl": "NiCd nominal cell voltage is 1.2 V.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Which battery type is used for emergency power and engine starting in a small aircraft?", "options": ["A secondary (rechargeable) storage battery", "A dry cell only", "A primary battery", "No battery is used"], "correct": 0, "short": "A secondary storage battery.", "expl": "Aircraft use rechargeable secondary batteries.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}]}, {"id": "b6m2s2", "title": "Lead-acid batteries", "lesson": "\n<p>The <b>lead-acid</b> battery is the <b>most commonly used</b> storage battery in\naircraft (p. 9-15).</p>\n\n<h3>Construction & voltage</h3>\n<p>A lead-acid battery is typically built with <b>6 or 12 cells</b>, and each cell\nproduces approximately <b>2.1 volts</b> (so ~12.6 V for a 6-cell, ~25 V nominal\n\"24-volt\" for a 12-cell). The <b>individual cells cannot be removed</b> from a\nlead-acid battery (p. 9-15, 9-18).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide076_1.jpeg\" alt=\"lead-acid battery\"><figcaption>Lead-acid cells: lead plates in a sulfuric-acid electrolyte.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Specific gravity & freezing protection</h3>\n<p>The state of charge of a lead-acid cell is read with a <b>hydrometer</b> that\nmeasures the electrolyte's <b>specific gravity</b>. To <b>prevent freezing\ndamage</b>, keep each cell's specific gravity <b>at 1.275</b> (for sealed lead-acid\nbatteries, check the <b>open-circuit voltage</b> instead) (p. 9-16).</p>\n\n<h3>Temperature correction (hydrometer readings at 80&deg;F)</h3>\n<p>U.S.-manufactured lead-acid batteries are considered <b>fully charged</b> when\nthe specific gravity reads between <b>1.275 and 1.300</b>. Approximate readings\n(taken at an electrolyte temperature of 80&deg;F) (p. 9-16):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Fully charged: <b>1.275 &ndash; 1.300</b></li>\n<li>1/3 discharged: about <b>1.240</b></li>\n<li>2/3 discharged: about <b>1.200</b></li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide080_1.jpeg\" alt=\"specific gravity table\"><figcaption>Specific-gravity readings vs. state of charge (hydrometer at 80&deg;F).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Charging limit</h3>\n<p>When charging a lead-acid battery, the voltage <b>per cell must not exceed\n2.35 volts</b> (p. 9-17).</p>\n\n<h3>Discharge characteristics</h3>\n<p>A lead-acid battery is capable of a <b>high rate of discharge</b> and good\n<b>low-temperature performance</b>. However, holding a high discharge rate for a\nperiod of time usually <b>warps the cell plates and shorts out the battery</b>. Also,\na lead-acid battery that becomes <b>fully discharged may not accept a recharge</b>\n(p. 9-18).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Lead-acid = <b>most common</b> storage battery; <b>6 or 12 cells</b>; <b>2.1 V/cell</b>; cells <b>cannot</b> be removed.</li>\n<li>Keep specific gravity at <b>1.275</b> to prevent <b>freezing</b>; sealed types &rarr; check open-circuit voltage.</li>\n<li>Fully charged SG = <b>1.275&ndash;1.300</b>; 1/3 discharged &asymp; <b>1.240</b>; 2/3 discharged &asymp; <b>1.200</b> (at 80&deg;F).</li>\n<li>Charging voltage <b>per cell must not exceed 2.35 V</b>.</li>\n<li>Sustained high discharge <b>warps plates / shorts</b> the battery; a fully discharged one may not accept recharge.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The most commonly used storage battery in aircraft is the:", "options": ["Nickel-cadmium battery", "Lead-acid battery", "Dry cell", "Lithium-ion battery"], "correct": 1, "short": "Lead-acid battery.", "expl": "Lead-acid is the most common aircraft storage battery.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A lead-acid aircraft battery is typically constructed with:", "options": ["6 or 12 cells", "18 or 20 cells", "24 cells always", "1 or 2 cells"], "correct": 0, "short": "6 or 12 cells.", "expl": "Each cell ≈ 2.1 V; 6 cells ≈ 12.6 V, 12 cells ≈ a nominal 24-V battery.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The individual cells of a lead-acid battery:", "options": ["Can be individually removed and replaced", "Are removable only when discharged", "Are made of nickel and cadmium", "Cannot be removed"], "correct": 3, "short": "Cannot be removed.", "expl": "Unlike NiCd, lead-acid cells are not individually removable.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The state of charge of a lead-acid cell is measured with a hydrometer, which reads:", "options": ["Cell voltage", "Internal resistance", "Specific gravity of the electrolyte", "Cell temperature"], "correct": 2, "short": "Specific gravity of the electrolyte.", "expl": "Higher specific gravity = more charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "To prevent freezing damage, each lead-acid cell's specific gravity should be maintained at:", "options": ["1.200", "1.275", "1.240", "1.100"], "correct": 1, "short": "1.275.", "expl": "Keeping SG at 1.275 prevents freezing; sealed types check open-circuit voltage instead.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A U.S.-made lead-acid battery is considered fully charged when its specific gravity reads between:", "options": ["1.200 and 1.240", "1.275 and 1.300", "1.100 and 1.150", "1.400 and 1.500"], "correct": 1, "short": "1.275 and 1.300.", "expl": "Full-charge SG range for U.S. lead-acid batteries.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A hydrometer reading of about 1.240 at 80°F indicates a lead-acid battery that is:", "options": ["Completely dead", "About 2/3 discharged", "About 1/3 discharged", "Fully charged"], "correct": 2, "short": "About 1/3 discharged.", "expl": "1.240 ≈ 1/3 discharged; 1.200 ≈ 2/3 discharged.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A specific-gravity reading of about 1.200 at 80°F indicates the battery is:", "options": ["Fully charged", "2/3 discharged", "1/3 discharged", "Overcharged"], "correct": 1, "short": "About 2/3 discharged.", "expl": "1.200 ≈ 2/3 discharged.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Specific-gravity readings for temperature correction are referenced to an electrolyte temperature of:", "options": ["80°F", "32°F", "100°F", "50°F"], "correct": 0, "short": "80°F.", "expl": "The standard reference temperature for hydrometer readings is 80°F.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "When charging a lead-acid battery, the voltage per cell must not exceed:", "options": ["2.1 volts", "2.75 volts", "2.35 volts", "1.67 volts"], "correct": 2, "short": "2.35 volts per cell.", "expl": "Exceeding 2.35 V/cell during charge can damage the battery.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Maintaining a high rate of discharge on a lead-acid battery for a period of time usually:", "options": ["Has no effect", "Increases its voltage", "Warps the cell plates and shorts out the battery", "Improves its capacity"], "correct": 2, "short": "Warps the plates and shorts the battery.", "expl": "Sustained high discharge damages the plates.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A lead-acid battery that becomes fully discharged:", "options": ["Will always recharge normally", "Gains capacity", "May not accept a recharge", "Reverses polarity"], "correct": 2, "short": "May not accept a recharge.", "expl": "Deep discharge can leave a lead-acid battery unable to take a charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "For a sealed lead-acid battery, state of charge is checked by measuring:", "options": ["Open-circuit voltage", "Specific gravity with a hydrometer", "Electrolyte level", "Plate color"], "correct": 0, "short": "Open-circuit voltage.", "expl": "Sealed batteries can't be hydrometer-tested; use open-circuit voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}]}, {"id": "b6m2s3", "title": "Nickel-cadmium (NiCd) batteries", "lesson": "\n<p>The <b>nickel-cadmium (NiCd)</b> battery is the other main secondary type used on\naircraft, favored on turbine aircraft for its high, steady output.</p>\n\n<h3>Construction & voltage</h3>\n<p>The <b>cell is the basic unit</b> of a NiCd battery. Each cell consists of\n<b>positive and negative plates, separators, electrolyte, a cell vent, and a cell\ncontainer</b> (p. 9-15). NiCd cells are rechargeable and deliver a <b>nominal\n1.2 volts</b> during discharge (p. 9-15, 9-18).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide078_1.jpeg\" alt=\"nickel-cadmium battery\"><figcaption>A nickel-cadmium cell: positive/negative plates, separators, electrolyte, vent, container.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Servicing characteristics</h3>\n<p>Unlike lead-acid, NiCd batteries have <b>removable cells</b>. After replacing a\nbad cell with a good one, the battery's life can be <b>extended 5 or more years</b>\n(p. 9-18).</p>\n<p>The state of charge of a NiCd battery <b>cannot</b> be judged by a hydrometer or\nby voltage alone. The <b>only accurate way to determine the state of charge is by a\nmeasured discharge with a NiCd battery charger</b> (p. 9-18).</p>\n\n<h3>Thermal runaway</h3>\n<p>NiCd batteries can suffer <b>thermal runaway</b> — heat lowers internal\nresistance, which increases current, which makes more heat. It can lead to violent\nventing or fire, so charging temperature and current must be watched carefully.\n(This is why the state of charge is verified by measured discharge, and why\nmanufacturer procedures are mandatory.)</p>\n\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Neutralizing agents (do not mix them up)</span>\nLead-acid electrolyte (sulfuric acid) is neutralized with a <b>baking-soda</b>\n(sodium bicarbonate) solution. NiCd electrolyte (potassium hydroxide, an alkaline)\nis neutralized with <b>boric acid or vinegar</b>. Servicing tools and areas for the\ntwo chemistries must be kept <b>separate</b>.</div>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>NiCd cell = positive/negative plates, separators, electrolyte, cell vent, cell container; nominal <b>1.2 V</b>.</li>\n<li>NiCd cells are <b>removable</b>; replacing a bad cell can extend life <b>5+ years</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>only accurate</b> way to know a NiCd's state of charge = a <b>measured discharge</b> with a NiCd charger.</li>\n<li>Lead-acid spill &rarr; neutralize with <b>baking soda</b>; NiCd spill &rarr; neutralize with <b>boric acid/vinegar</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A nickel-cadmium cell consists of positive and negative plates, separators, electrolyte, and:", "options": ["A commutator and brushes", "A cell vent and cell container", "Slip rings", "A carbon pile"], "correct": 1, "short": "A cell vent and cell container.", "expl": "These plus plates, separators, and electrolyte make up a NiCd cell.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A NiCd cell delivers a nominal voltage during discharge of about:", "options": ["1.2 volts", "2.1 volts", "1.67 volts", "2.35 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.2 volts.", "expl": "NiCd nominal cell voltage = 1.2 V.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Compared with lead-acid, the cells of a NiCd battery are:", "options": ["Rated at 2.1 volts each", "Filled with sulfuric acid", "Individually removable/replaceable", "Permanently sealed in and non-removable"], "correct": 2, "short": "Individually removable/replaceable.", "expl": "This lets you replace a single bad cell.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "After replacing a bad cell with a good cell, a NiCd battery's life can be extended:", "options": ["It cannot be extended", "A few days", "About 1 year", "5 or more years"], "correct": 3, "short": "5 or more years.", "expl": "Cell replacement can add 5+ years of service.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The only accurate way to determine the state of charge of a NiCd battery is:", "options": ["Reading specific gravity with a hydrometer", "Feeling the case temperature", "A measured discharge with a NiCd battery charger", "Measuring open-circuit voltage"], "correct": 2, "short": "A measured discharge with a NiCd charger.", "expl": "NiCd state of charge can't be read by hydrometer or voltage alone.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The dangerous condition where a NiCd battery's heat lowers its resistance, raising current and heat further, is:", "options": ["Reverse polarity", "Thermal runaway", "Dielectric stress", "Sulfation"], "correct": 1, "short": "Thermal runaway.", "expl": "A self-feeding heat/current loop that can cause venting or fire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Spilled lead-acid electrolyte should be neutralized with:", "options": ["Plain water only", "A baking-soda (sodium bicarbonate) solution", "Vinegar", "Boric acid"], "correct": 1, "short": "Baking soda (sodium bicarbonate).", "expl": "Lead-acid = sulfuric acid, neutralized by baking soda.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Spilled NiCd electrolyte (an alkaline) should be neutralized with:", "options": ["Boric acid or vinegar", "Ammonia", "Sulfuric acid", "Baking soda"], "correct": 0, "short": "Boric acid or vinegar.", "expl": "NiCd = potassium hydroxide (alkaline), neutralized by a mild acid.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Servicing areas and tools for lead-acid and NiCd batteries should be:", "options": ["Cleaned with the same neutralizer", "Used interchangeably", "Shared to save space", "Kept completely separate"], "correct": 3, "short": "Kept completely separate.", "expl": "Cross-contaminating the two chemistries damages the batteries.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Which battery type is favored on turbine aircraft for high, steady current output?", "options": ["Lead-acid", "Primary dry cell", "Nickel-cadmium", "Carbon-zinc"], "correct": 2, "short": "Nickel-cadmium.", "expl": "NiCd holds a steady output and tolerates high discharge, common on turbine aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}]}, {"id": "b6m2s4", "title": "Charging, maintenance & installation", "lesson": "\n<p>Battery inspection, charging, and installation errors are common test items — and\ncommon ways to ruin a battery or start a fire. Always <b>follow the battery\nmanufacturer's approved procedures</b>, because they vary with chemistry and\nconstruction (p. 9-17).</p>\n\n<h3>Two ways to charge</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Constant-voltage charging (CV)</b> — the <b>aircraft's on-board charging\nsystem</b> is the constant-voltage type. The regulator holds a fixed voltage and the\nbattery draws less current as it charges (p. 9-17).</li>\n<li><b>Constant-current charging</b> — most convenient for charging batteries\n<b>outside the airplane</b>, because several batteries of <b>varying voltages</b> can\nbe charged at once on the same system (p. 9-17).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Inspection</h3>\n<p>An aircraft battery inspection includes (p. 9-19):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inspect the <b>battery sump jar and lines</b> for condition and security.</li>\n<li>Inspect <b>battery terminals and quick-disconnect plugs and pins</b>.</li>\n<li>Inspect <b>drain and vent lines</b> for restriction, deterioration, and security.</li>\n<li>Look for <b>physical damage, loose connections, and electrolyte loss</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Ventilation</h3>\n<p>A battery <b>ventilating system removes gasses and acid fumes</b> from the\nbattery to <b>reduce fire hazards</b> and to <b>eliminate damage to airframe\nparts</b> (p. 9-19).</p>\n\n<h3>Installation — connect/disconnect order (safety-critical)</h3>\n<p>The order you connect and remove battery leads matters, to avoid shorting the hot\nterminal to structure (p. 9-19):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Removal</b>: remove the <b>grounding (ground) lead FIRST</b>, then the\npositive lead.</li>\n<li><b>Connection</b>: connect the <b>grounding lead LAST</b>, to minimize the risk\nof shorting the hot terminal during installation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Other installation points: <b>clean</b> the area; <b>neutralize</b> if changing to\na different battery type; verify <b>venting</b> and the <b>sump jar</b>; secure the\n<b>hold-down devices</b>; and <b>ensure the aircraft wiring goes to the proper\nterminal</b> — <b>reverse polarity can seriously damage</b> the battery and other\nelectrical components (p. 9-19).</p>\n\n<h3>Troubleshooting</h3>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide086_1.jpeg\" alt=\"battery troubleshooting chart\"><figcaption>Typical battery troubleshooting chart — symptom, probable cause, corrective action.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Aircraft on-board charging = <b>constant-voltage</b>; shop charging of several batteries at once = <b>constant-current</b>.</li>\n<li>Ventilation <b>removes gasses/acid fumes</b> to reduce fire hazard and airframe damage.</li>\n<li>Removal: <b>ground lead first</b>. Connection: <b>ground lead last</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Reverse polarity</b> can seriously damage the battery and electrical components.</li>\n<li>Always follow the <b>manufacturer's approved procedures</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The battery charging system built into an airplane is of the:", "options": ["Constant-current type", "Trickle-pulse type", "Constant-voltage type", "Rapid-cycle type"], "correct": 2, "short": "Constant-voltage type.", "expl": "On-board aircraft charging is constant-voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Constant-current charging is most convenient for:", "options": ["Charging several batteries of varying voltages at once outside the airplane", "Charging the battery while installed in the aircraft", "Preventing thermal runaway only", "Emergency starting"], "correct": 0, "short": "Charging several batteries outside the airplane at once.", "expl": "Constant-current lets you series-charge several batteries of different voltages.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The purpose of a battery ventilating system is to:", "options": ["Increase battery voltage", "Charge the battery faster", "Cool the electrolyte only", "Remove gasses and acid fumes to reduce fire hazard and airframe damage"], "correct": 3, "short": "Remove gasses/acid fumes (fire and corrosion protection).", "expl": "Ventilation reduces explosion/fire hazard and prevents fumes from damaging structure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "When removing a battery, which lead is removed first?", "options": ["The grounding (ground) lead", "The positive (hot) lead", "Both simultaneously", "Either lead, order does not matter"], "correct": 0, "short": "The grounding lead first.", "expl": "Remove ground first so a slipped tool can't short the hot terminal to structure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "When installing a battery, which lead is connected last?", "options": ["The sump jar", "The vent line", "The positive lead", "The grounding (ground) lead"], "correct": 3, "short": "The grounding lead last.", "expl": "Connect ground last to minimize risk of shorting the hot terminal.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Connecting a battery with reversed polarity can:", "options": ["Seriously damage the battery and other electrical components", "Have no effect", "Improve charging speed", "Only trip a circuit breaker harmlessly"], "correct": 0, "short": "Seriously damage the battery and components.", "expl": "Always verify wiring goes to the proper terminal.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A battery inspection should include checking the sump jar, terminals, quick-disconnect plugs/pins, and:", "options": ["The exciter field", "The commutator", "Drain and vent lines for restriction, deterioration, and security", "The slip rings"], "correct": 2, "short": "Drain and vent lines.", "expl": "Also look for physical damage, loose connections, and electrolyte loss.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Because inspection and maintenance procedures vary with battery chemistry and construction, you should always:", "options": ["Charge at maximum current", "Skip the manufacturer's data", "Use one generic procedure for all batteries", "Follow the battery manufacturer's approved procedures"], "correct": 3, "short": "Follow the manufacturer's approved procedures.", "expl": "Procedures differ by chemistry/construction.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "When installing a different type of battery than was previously used, the technician should first:", "options": ["Clean and neutralize the battery area/box", "Skip cleaning the area", "Remove the vent system", "Increase the charging voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Clean and neutralize the area.", "expl": "Neutralize residue from the old chemistry before installing the new type.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Battery hold-down devices are used to:", "options": ["Increase battery capacity", "Regulate charging voltage", "Neutralize acid", "Secure the battery against vibration and movement"], "correct": 3, "short": "Secure the battery against vibration/movement.", "expl": "Hold-downs keep the battery firmly in place.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}]}]}, {"n": 3, "day": "Unit 3", "title": "DC Generators & Controls", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · construction · series/shunt/compound · ratings · regulators (carbon-pile, 3-unit)", "sections": [{"id": "b6m3s1", "title": "DC generator construction", "lesson": "\n<p>A <b>DC generator</b> turns mechanical energy (from the engine) into DC\nelectricity using electromagnetic induction. To understand it you need its three\nconstruction features: the <b>field frame</b>, the <b>armature</b>, and the\n<b>commutator</b>.</p>\n\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide088_1.jpeg\" alt=\"DC generator construction\"><figcaption>Main construction features of a DC generator.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Field frame</h3>\n<p>The <b>field frame</b> (also called the yoke) is the stationary outer housing. It\nholds the <b>field poles and field windings</b> and provides the magnetic circuit\n(the path for the magnetic field the armature spins through) (p. 9-20).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide090_1.jpeg\" alt=\"field frame\"><figcaption>The field frame houses the field poles and windings.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Armature</h3>\n<p>The <b>armature</b> is the <b>rotating</b> part — the coils of wire that spin\nthrough the magnetic field. It is where the <b>voltage is induced</b> (p. 9-20). The\nvoltage induced in the spinning armature is <b>alternating current (AC)</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide091_1.jpeg\" alt=\"armature\"><figcaption>The rotating armature is where voltage is induced.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Commutator</h3>\n<p>The <b>commutator</b> is the key to making DC. A basic DC generator uses\n<b>two half-cylinders (segments)</b> — the commutator — with <b>brushes</b> riding\non them. As the armature turns, the commutator <b>reverses the connection to the\nexternal circuit at the right instant</b>, so the AC generated in the armature comes\nout as <b>DC</b> at the brushes. In other words, the commutator <b>converts the AC\nin the armature into DC output</b> (p. 9-20/21).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide092_1.jpeg\" alt=\"commutator\"><figcaption>The commutator + brushes convert the armature's AC into DC.</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Generator vs. AC generator</span> A DC generator uses a\n<b>commutator</b>. An AC generator uses <b>slip (collector) rings</b>. That single\npart is the difference between a DC output and an AC output.</div>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Field frame</b> = stationary housing; holds field poles/windings and the magnetic circuit.</li>\n<li><b>Armature</b> = the rotating coils where voltage is induced (as AC).</li>\n<li><b>Commutator</b> = split segments + brushes that <b>convert the armature's AC into DC</b>.</li>\n<li>DC generator = <b>commutator</b>; AC generator = <b>slip rings</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The three main construction features of a DC generator are the:", "options": ["Rotor, dielectric, and rectifier", "Field frame, armature, and commutator", "Exciter, pilot exciter, and main generator", "Slip rings, brushes, and stator"], "correct": 1, "short": "Field frame, armature, and commutator.", "expl": "These are the DC generator's core parts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The stationary outer housing that holds the field poles and windings is the:", "options": ["Brush rigging", "Commutator", "Field frame (yoke)", "Armature"], "correct": 2, "short": "Field frame (yoke).", "expl": "It provides the magnetic circuit and houses the field windings.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The rotating part of a DC generator, where voltage is induced, is the:", "options": ["Brush", "Armature", "Commutator", "Field frame"], "correct": 1, "short": "Armature.", "expl": "The spinning armature coils have voltage induced in them.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The voltage actually generated within the spinning armature is:", "options": ["Direct current (DC)", "Alternating current (AC)", "Zero until rectified by the field frame", "Constant with no polarity"], "correct": 1, "short": "Alternating current (AC).", "expl": "The armature generates AC; the commutator converts it to DC output.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The purpose of the commutator in a DC generator is to:", "options": ["Convert the AC generated in the armature into DC output", "Regulate the field current", "Store electrostatic charge", "Provide the magnetic field"], "correct": 0, "short": "Convert the armature's AC into DC output.", "expl": "Commutator segments + brushes rectify the AC by commutation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A basic DC generator commutator is made of:", "options": ["Two slip rings", "A permanent magnet", "Two half-cylinders (segments)", "A carbon pile"], "correct": 2, "short": "Two half-cylinders (segments).", "expl": "The split segments reverse the connection at the right instant to make DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The main difference between a DC generator and an AC generator is that the DC generator uses a ____ while the AC generator uses ____.", "options": ["commutator; slip rings", "carbon pile; a field frame", "diode; a rectifier", "slip rings; a commutator"], "correct": 0, "short": "Commutator vs. slip rings.", "expl": "Commutator = DC output; slip rings = AC output.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Brushes in a DC generator:", "options": ["Rotate with the armature", "Provide the magnetic field", "Ride on the commutator to carry current to/from the external circuit", "Store charge like a capacitor"], "correct": 2, "short": "Ride on the commutator to carry current.", "expl": "Brushes are the sliding contact between rotating and fixed parts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}]}, {"id": "b6m3s2", "title": "Generator types, ratings & maintenance", "lesson": "\n<h3>Types of DC generators</h3>\n<p>DC generators are classified by <b>how the field winding is connected</b> relative\nto the armature (p. 9-22):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series-wound</b> — the field winding is in <b>series</b> with the armature and\nthe load. The field current is the load current, so output voltage <b>varies greatly\nwith load</b> — a poor choice where steady voltage is needed.</li>\n<li><b>Parallel- (shunt-) wound</b> — the field winding is in <b>parallel (shunt)</b>\nwith the armature. It gives a <b>relatively constant voltage</b> over a range of\nloads.</li>\n<li><b>Series-parallel (compound) wound</b> — has <b>both</b> a series field and a\nshunt field, combining the characteristics of the two.</li>\n</ul>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap\">\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:150px\"><img src=\"img/slide096_1.jpeg\" alt=\"series wound\"><figcaption>Series-wound</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:150px\"><img src=\"img/slide097_1.jpeg\" alt=\"shunt wound\"><figcaption>Parallel (shunt)-wound</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:150px\"><img src=\"img/slide098_1.jpeg\" alt=\"compound wound\"><figcaption>Compound-wound</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n\n<h3>DC generator ratings</h3>\n<p>Generators are typically rated for <b>voltage and power (amps)</b>, usually\n<b>14- or 28-volt DC</b> systems (p. 9-23). Key rating facts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rotation</b> is viewed from the <b>driven (drive) end</b> and is termed\n<b>clockwise or counterclockwise</b>.</li>\n<li>The direction of rotation may be <b>stamped on the data plate</b>.</li>\n<li>If a generator with the <b>wrong rotation</b> is used, the <b>polarity of the\noutput voltage is reversed</b> (p. 9-23).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>DC generator maintenance</h3>\n<p>Always <b>follow the manufacturer's instructions</b>. A typical inspection covers\n(p. 9-23):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Security of mounting</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Condition of electrical connections</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Dirt and oil accumulation</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Brush condition</b> (wear, seating, spring tension).</li>\n<li><b>Operation</b> and <b>voltage-regulation operation</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Series</b> field: output <b>varies greatly with load</b>. <b>Shunt</b> field: <b>relatively constant</b> voltage. <b>Compound</b>: both fields.</li>\n<li>Rated for <b>voltage and power (amps)</b>; typical aircraft = <b>14 or 28 V DC</b>.</li>\n<li>Rotation viewed from the <b>driven end</b>; may be stamped on the <b>data plate</b>; wrong rotation <b>reverses output polarity</b>.</li>\n<li>Maintenance: mounting security, connections, dirt/oil, <b>brush condition</b>, operation & voltage regulation.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "In a series-wound DC generator, the field winding is connected:", "options": ["Completely separate from the armature", "In series with the armature and load", "In parallel (shunt) with the armature", "To a permanent magnet only"], "correct": 1, "short": "In series with the armature and load.", "expl": "So field current = load current, and output varies greatly with load.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Which DC generator produces a relatively constant voltage over a range of loads?", "options": ["Parallel (shunt)-wound", "Series-only", "Series-wound", "Reverse-wound"], "correct": 0, "short": "Parallel (shunt)-wound.", "expl": "A shunt field gives fairly steady voltage as load changes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A compound-wound DC generator has:", "options": ["Only a series field", "Only a shunt field", "Both a series and a shunt field", "No field winding at all"], "correct": 2, "short": "Both a series and a shunt field.", "expl": "Compound = series-parallel, combining both characteristics.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A series-wound generator's output voltage:", "options": ["Varies greatly with changes in load", "Stays perfectly constant", "Is unaffected by load", "Is always AC"], "correct": 0, "short": "Varies greatly with load.", "expl": "Because field current is the load current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "DC generators are typically rated for:", "options": ["Wavelength and period", "Frequency and phase", "Capacitance and inductance", "Voltage and power (amps)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Voltage and power (amps).", "expl": "Aircraft DC systems are commonly 14 V or 28 V.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Aircraft DC generators most commonly operate at:", "options": ["14 or 28 volts", "400 volts", "6 or 8 volts", "115 or 120 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "14 or 28 volts DC.", "expl": "Standard light-aircraft DC system voltages.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Generator direction of rotation is viewed from the ____ and stated as clockwise or counterclockwise.", "options": ["driven (drive) end", "field frame", "brush end", "commutator end"], "correct": 0, "short": "The driven (drive) end.", "expl": "Rotation is referenced from the drive end; it may be stamped on the data plate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "If a generator with the wrong direction of rotation is installed, the result is:", "options": ["Higher output voltage", "The polarity of the output voltage is reversed", "The frequency doubles", "No change"], "correct": 1, "short": "Output voltage polarity is reversed.", "expl": "Wrong rotation flips output polarity.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Where might a generator's direction of rotation be found?", "options": ["Only in the flight manual", "On the commutator", "On the brush spring", "Stamped on the data plate"], "correct": 3, "short": "Stamped on the data plate.", "expl": "The data plate may show CW or CCW rotation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A routine DC generator inspection includes checking mounting security, electrical connections, dirt/oil, operation, and:", "options": ["Dielectric strength", "Brush condition", "Slip-ring alignment", "Electrolyte specific gravity"], "correct": 1, "short": "Brush condition.", "expl": "Brush wear/seating/tension is a key generator inspection item.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The overriding rule for DC generator maintenance is to:", "options": ["Use any generic procedure", "Always follow the manufacturer's instructions", "Charge it like a battery", "Never inspect the brushes"], "correct": 1, "short": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions.", "expl": "Manufacturer data governs generator maintenance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}]}, {"id": "b6m3s3", "title": "Generator controls & regulators", "lesson": "\n<p>Every aircraft is designed to operate within a <b>specific voltage range</b>. The\n<b>generator control system</b> keeps the generator's output <b>within limits</b>\nacross all flight variables (engine RPM, electrical load, temperature). These\ncontrols are often called <b>voltage regulators</b> or <b>Generator Control Units\n(GCU)</b> (p. 9-24).</p>\n\n<h3>Functions of generator control systems</h3>\n<p>Most control systems perform several regulation, sensing, and protection\nfunctions for the DC generation system (p. 9-24):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Voltage regulation</b></li>\n<li><b>Overvoltage protection</b></li>\n<li><b>Parallel generator operation</b></li>\n<li><b>Overexcitation protection</b></li>\n<li><b>Differential voltage</b></li>\n<li><b>Reverse-current sensing</b></li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>High-output vs. low-output generators</h3>\n<p><b>High-output generators</b> are mostly found on <b>turbine-powered corporate-type\naircraft</b> and often combine the generator and starter into one unit, called a\n<b>starter-generator</b> (p. 9-25).</p>\n<p><b>Low-output generators</b> use a control that <b>modifies the current flow to\nthe generator field</b> to control output power. The <b>two most common</b> voltage\nregulators are the <b>carbon-pile regulator</b> and the <b>three-unit regulator</b>\n(p. 9-25).</p>\n\n<h3>Carbon-pile regulator</h3>\n<p>A <b>carbon-pile regulator</b> uses a <b>stack of carbon disks</b>. An\nelectromagnet squeezes or relaxes the stack; more squeeze lowers the stack's\nresistance, less squeeze raises it. By varying that resistance in the <b>field\ncircuit</b>, it controls the field current and thus the output voltage (p. 9-25).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide106_1.jpeg\" alt=\"carbon pile regulator\"><figcaption>A carbon-pile regulator varies field resistance with a stack of carbon disks.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Three-unit regulator (know all three units)</h3>\n<p>The <b>three-unit regulator</b> contains three separate relays/units (p. 9-25):</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide108_1.jpeg\" alt=\"three-unit regulator\"><figcaption>The three-unit regulator: voltage regulator, current limiter, reverse-current relay.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Voltage Regulator</b> — holds the output voltage constant by varying the\ngenerator <b>field current</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Current Limiter</b> — limits the generator's <b>output current</b> so it\ncannot exceed its rating (protects the generator from overload).</li>\n<li><b>Reverse-Current Relay (reverse-current cutout)</b> — a one-way \"check valve\"\nfor current: it <b>connects the generator to the bus only when generator voltage is\nhigher than battery voltage</b>, and <b>opens to prevent the battery from\ndischarging back through the generator</b> when generator output drops (at low RPM or\nshutdown).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Generator controls (a.k.a. <b>GCU</b>) keep output within the aircraft's voltage limits.</li>\n<li>Functions include voltage regulation, <b>overvoltage protection</b>, parallel operation, overexcitation protection, differential voltage, <b>reverse-current sensing</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Starter-generator</b> = high-output combined unit on turbine corporate aircraft.</li>\n<li>Two common low-output regulators: <b>carbon-pile</b> and <b>three-unit</b>.</li>\n<li>Three-unit regulator = <b>Voltage Regulator + Current Limiter + Reverse-Current Relay</b>. The reverse-current relay stops the battery discharging back through the generator.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The generator control system's main job is to:", "options": ["Store energy like a battery", "Provide engine starting torque", "Convert AC to DC", "Keep generator output within the aircraft's voltage limits for all flight variables"], "correct": 3, "short": "Keep output within voltage limits.", "expl": "It regulates output across RPM, load, and temperature changes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Generator control units are also commonly called:", "options": ["Inverters", "Commutators", "Voltage regulators / GCUs", "Slip rings"], "correct": 2, "short": "Voltage regulators / Generator Control Units (GCU).", "expl": "Both terms describe the generator control system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Which is a function of a generator control system?", "options": ["Overvoltage protection", "Cabin pressurization", "Tire inflation", "Fuel metering"], "correct": 0, "short": "Overvoltage protection.", "expl": "Functions include voltage regulation, overvoltage/overexcitation protection, parallel operation, differential voltage, reverse-current sensing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A high-output unit that combines the generator and the starter into one device, common on turbine corporate aircraft, is a:", "options": ["Constant-speed drive", "Carbon-pile regulator", "Starter-generator", "Three-unit regulator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Starter-generator.", "expl": "It serves as the starter for start, then as the generator in flight.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The two most common voltage regulators for low-output generators are the:", "options": ["Carbon-pile regulator and the three-unit regulator", "BPCU and the GCU", "Slip ring and the commutator", "Inverter and the rectifier"], "correct": 0, "short": "Carbon-pile and three-unit regulators.", "expl": "Both control output by varying field current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A low-output generator control regulates output power by:", "options": ["Adding capacitors to the load", "Changing the armature speed", "Modifying the current flow to the generator field", "Reversing the commutator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Modifying the field current.", "expl": "Controlling field current controls output voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A carbon-pile regulator controls output voltage by:", "options": ["Varying the resistance of a stack of carbon disks in the field circuit", "Melting a fuse link", "Switching slip rings", "Rectifying the AC output"], "correct": 0, "short": "Varying resistance of a carbon-disk stack.", "expl": "An electromagnet squeezes/relaxes the pile to change field resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide106_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "The three units of a three-unit regulator are the voltage regulator, the current limiter, and the:", "options": ["Constant-speed drive", "Reverse-current relay", "Inverter", "Carbon pile"], "correct": 1, "short": "Reverse-current relay.", "expl": "Voltage regulator + current limiter + reverse-current relay.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide108_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "Within a three-unit regulator, the voltage regulator controls output voltage by:", "options": ["Changing engine RPM", "Opening the load circuit", "Rectifying the output", "Varying the generator field current"], "correct": 3, "short": "Varying the field current.", "expl": "The voltage-regulator unit adjusts field current to hold voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide110_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "The current limiter in a three-unit regulator:", "options": ["Reverses current to the battery", "Increases output voltage", "Provides the magnetic field", "Limits the generator's output current to protect it from overload"], "correct": 3, "short": "Limits output current (overload protection).", "expl": "It caps output current at the generator's rating.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide111_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "The reverse-current relay (reverse-current cutout) prevents:", "options": ["The battery from discharging back through the generator when generator voltage is low", "The generator from overspeeding", "The commutator from sparking", "The field from overexciting"], "correct": 0, "short": "The battery discharging back through the generator.", "expl": "It disconnects the generator when its voltage drops below battery voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide112_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "The reverse-current relay connects the generator to the bus only when:", "options": ["Generator voltage is higher than battery voltage", "The engine is off", "The current limiter opens", "Battery voltage is higher than generator voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Generator voltage exceeds battery voltage.", "expl": "It acts like a one-way check valve for current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}]}]}, {"n": 4, "day": "Unit 4", "title": "Alternators & AC Power Systems", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · DC/AC alternators · 3-phase · exciters · CSD/IDG · 400 Hz · GCU/BPCU", "sections": [{"id": "b6m4s1", "title": "DC alternators & three-phase basics", "lesson": "\n<p>Modern light aircraft use an <b>alternator</b> instead of a DC generator. An\nalternator actually generates <b>AC</b>, which is then <b>rectified to DC</b> for the\naircraft's DC system. Alternators are lighter, put out useful current at low RPM\n(even at idle), and need less maintenance than DC generators.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide113_1.jpeg\" alt=\"DC alternator\"><figcaption>A light-aircraft alternator generates AC internally, then rectifies it to DC.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Why three-phase?</h3>\n<p>Larger machines generate <b>three-phase</b> power: three separate sine waves,\neach <b>120&deg; apart</b>. Three-phase is used because it delivers <b>smoother,\nmore efficient power</b> for a given size and weight than single-phase, and it\nrectifies to a smoother DC (p. 9-27).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide118_1.jpeg\" alt=\"three-phase power\"><figcaption>Three-phase: three sine waves 120&deg; apart for smoother, more efficient power.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Alternator voltage regulation</h3>\n<p>Like a generator, an alternator's output must be regulated. The regulator\ncontrols the <b>field current</b> to hold output steady. Modern aircraft use\n<b>solid-state (transistorized) regulators</b> — no vibrating points or carbon\npiles — which are more accurate and reliable (p. 9-27).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide120_2.jpeg\" alt=\"solid-state regulator\"><figcaption>A solid-state (transistorized) voltage regulator.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>A light-aircraft <b>alternator generates AC</b>, then <b>rectifies it to DC</b>.</li>\n<li>Advantages over a DC generator: lighter, useful output at low/idle RPM, less maintenance.</li>\n<li><b>Three-phase</b> = three sine waves <b>120&deg; apart</b> &rarr; smoother, more efficient power.</li>\n<li>Output is regulated by controlling <b>field current</b>; modern regulators are <b>solid-state</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A light-aircraft alternator actually generates ____, which is then ____ for the DC system.", "options": ["AC; rectified to DC", "AC; stored without change", "DC; inverted to AC", "DC; stored in a capacitor"], "correct": 0, "short": "AC, then rectified to DC.", "expl": "Alternators make AC internally and rectify it to DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "An advantage of an alternator over a DC generator is that it:", "options": ["Needs a commutator", "Requires no voltage regulation", "Produces only DC internally", "Produces useful output even at low/idle RPM"], "correct": 3, "short": "Useful output at low/idle RPM.", "expl": "Alternators are lighter, output well at low RPM, and need less maintenance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Three-phase power consists of three sine waves that are:", "options": ["180° apart", "In phase with each other", "90° apart", "120° apart"], "correct": 3, "short": "120° apart.", "expl": "Three overlapping waves 120° apart give smoother, more efficient power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Three-phase power is used instead of single-phase because it:", "options": ["Requires no rectification", "Delivers smoother, more efficient power for a given size/weight", "Is cheaper to insulate", "Runs at a lower frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Smoother, more efficient power.", "expl": "It also rectifies to smoother DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "An alternator's output voltage is regulated by controlling the:", "options": ["Number of slip rings", "Armature speed only", "Field current", "Commutator segments"], "correct": 2, "short": "Field current.", "expl": "Varying field current holds output voltage steady.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Modern aircraft alternators typically use which type of voltage regulator?", "options": ["Vibrating-point", "Solid-state (transistorized)", "Three-unit", "Carbon-pile"], "correct": 1, "short": "Solid-state (transistorized).", "expl": "More accurate and reliable than carbon-pile or vibrating-point types.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}]}, {"id": "b6m4s2", "title": "AC alternators (three generators in one)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>AC alternators</b> are found on aircraft that use a <b>large amount of\nelectrical power</b> — transport aircraft like the <b>Boeing 757</b> or\n<b>Airbus A-380</b> (p. 9-28).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide122_1.jpeg\" alt=\"AC alternator\"><figcaption>Large transport AC alternator (integrated drive generator).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Three generators in one</h3>\n<p>A large aircraft AC alternator actually contains <b>three generators in one\nunit</b> (p. 9-28):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Pilot exciter</b> — a small generator with a <b>permanent-magnet</b> field;\nits output is rectified and sent to the exciter field. (It needs no external power to\nstart making electricity.)</li>\n<li><b>Exciter generator</b> — its armature output is <b>rectified and sent to the\nmain generator field</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Main AC alternator</b> — its output is <b>three-phase</b> and is used to power\nthe aircraft's electrical loads.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide124_1.jpeg\" alt=\"AC alternator three generators\"><figcaption>The three machines in one AC alternator: pilot exciter, exciter, main alternator.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>To raise output, <b>more current is sent to the exciter field</b>; to lower it,\nless current is sent (p. 9-28). This \"brushless\" arrangement means the main field is\nfed through internal rectifiers instead of brushes and slip rings, which greatly cuts\nmaintenance.</p>\n\n<h3>Getting DC from an AC system</h3>\n<p>Large aircraft are primarily AC, but still need DC for many systems. DC is\nobtained by <b>rectification</b> — <b>transformer-rectifier units (TRUs)</b> change\nthe 115-V AC into the DC needed for DC buses and battery charging (p. 9-33).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>AC alternators serve <b>high-electrical-power transport aircraft</b> (757, A-380).</li>\n<li>Three generators in one: <b>pilot exciter (permanent magnet) &rarr; exciter &rarr; main AC alternator</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>main alternator output is three-phase</b>; raise output by increasing <b>exciter field current</b>.</li>\n<li>DC on a large aircraft comes from <b>rectification</b> (transformer-rectifier units).</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "AC alternators are found on aircraft that:", "options": ["Have no engine-driven power", "Use very little electrical power", "Use a large amount of electrical power (transport aircraft)", "Operate only on battery power"], "correct": 2, "short": "Use a large amount of electrical power.", "expl": "Transport aircraft like the 757 and A-380.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "A large-aircraft AC alternator effectively contains:", "options": ["One generator", "A generator and a battery", "Three generators in one unit", "Two generators"], "correct": 2, "short": "Three generators in one.", "expl": "Pilot exciter, exciter, and main AC alternator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "The output of the main AC alternator is:", "options": ["Three-phase", "Two-phase", "Single-phase", "Pulsed DC"], "correct": 0, "short": "Three-phase.", "expl": "The main alternator supplies three-phase AC to the loads.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "The pilot exciter of an AC alternator uses which type of field?", "options": ["An electromagnet powered by the battery", "A slip ring", "A permanent magnet", "A carbon pile"], "correct": 2, "short": "A permanent magnet.", "expl": "The permanent-magnet pilot exciter needs no external power to begin generating.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "To increase the output of an AC alternator, the control system sends:", "options": ["More current to the exciter field", "Less current to the exciter field", "More current directly to the loads", "Current to the commutator"], "correct": 0, "short": "More current to the exciter field.", "expl": "Exciter field current controls the main alternator output.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "On a large aircraft with an AC electrical system, DC power is typically obtained by:", "options": ["Inverting the AC", "The battery alone", "A separate DC generator only", "Rectification using transformer-rectifier units (TRUs)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Rectification (transformer-rectifier units).", "expl": "TRUs convert 115 V AC to DC for DC buses/charging.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "The brushless design of a large AC alternator reduces maintenance because it:", "options": ["Feeds the main field through internal rectifiers instead of brushes and slip rings", "Runs on DC only", "Eliminates the field winding", "Uses no exciter"], "correct": 0, "short": "Main field fed via internal rectifiers, not brushes/slip rings.", "expl": "No brushes/slip rings to wear.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}]}, {"id": "b6m4s3", "title": "CSD/IDG, 400 Hz & AC control units", "lesson": "\n<h3>Constant-Speed Drive (CSD) and the 400-Hz rule</h3>\n<p>An AC electrical system must hold a steady <b>frequency of 400 Hz</b>. But the\nengine changes RPM constantly, so the alternator can't just bolt to the engine. A\n<b>Constant-Speed Drive (CSD)</b> sits between the engine and the alternator and\nkeeps the alternator turning at the <b>exact speed needed to produce 400 Hz</b>,\nregardless of engine RPM (p. 9-30).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide127_1.jpeg\" alt=\"constant speed drive\"><figcaption>A CSD keeps the alternator at the right speed for 400 Hz as engine RPM varies.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li>It works much like the <b>hydraulic transmission</b> in a car — engine RPM can\nchange while the output speed stays constant.</li>\n<li>If the frequency strays <b>more than 10%</b>, the electrical systems will\n<b>not operate correctly</b> (p. 9-30).</li>\n<li>When the <b>CSD and alternator are built into one unit</b>, it is called an\n<b>Integrated Drive Generator (IDG)</b> (p. 9-30).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>AC power system requirements</h3>\n<p>An aircraft AC electrical system must maintain a constant output of\n<b>115&ndash;120 volts AC</b> at a frequency of <b>400 Hz (&plusmn;10%)</b>\n(p. 9-33).</p>\n\n<h3>AC alternator control units</h3>\n<p>Modern aircraft use several <b>computerized control units</b>, usually in the\nequipment bay, to regulate AC power. Large transport aircraft use <b>redundant\ncomputers</b> for safety if one fails. The two most common units (p. 9-31):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Generator Control Unit (GCU)</b> — controls <b>AC generator functions</b>\nsuch as <b>voltage regulation and frequency control</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Bus Power Control Unit (BPCU)</b> — controls the <b>distribution of electrical\npower between the various distribution buses</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide133_1.jpeg\" alt=\"AC alternator control system\"><figcaption>AC alternator control system with GCU and BPCU.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Current transformers (CT)</h3>\n<p>A <b>current transformer (CT)</b> is an inductive unit that <b>surrounds the main\npower cables</b> of the distribution system. As AC flows through a cable, the CT\nreceives an <b>induced voltage that is directly related to the current</b> flowing\nin the cable — a simple way to measure/monitor load current without breaking into\nthe cable (p. 9-33).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>CSD</b> keeps the alternator at the speed needed for <b>400 Hz</b>; frequency must stay within <b>&plusmn;10%</b>.</li>\n<li><b>IDG</b> = CSD + alternator in one unit.</li>\n<li>AC system output: <b>115&ndash;120 V AC, 400 Hz (&plusmn;10%)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>GCU</b> = voltage regulation & frequency control; <b>BPCU</b> = power distribution between buses.</li>\n<li><b>Current transformer (CT)</b> surrounds a power cable; its induced voltage is proportional to the cable current.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A constant-speed drive (CSD) is used to:", "options": ["Regulate cabin pressure", "Keep the alternator turning at the correct speed to produce 400 Hz despite engine RPM changes", "Store electrical energy", "Rectify AC into DC"], "correct": 1, "short": "Hold alternator speed for a steady 400 Hz.", "expl": "It decouples alternator speed from engine RPM.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "An aircraft AC electrical system operates at a frequency of:", "options": ["400 Hz", "120 Hz", "1,000 Hz", "60 Hz"], "correct": 0, "short": "400 Hz.", "expl": "Aircraft AC systems use 400 Hz (±10%).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "If AC frequency strays more than ____ from nominal, the electrical systems will not operate correctly.", "options": ["25%", "10%", "5%", "1%"], "correct": 1, "short": "10%.", "expl": "Frequency must stay within ±10% of 400 Hz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "When the constant-speed drive and the alternator are built into a single unit, it is called a(n):", "options": ["Current transformer", "Integrated Drive Generator (IDG)", "Bus Power Control Unit", "Transformer-rectifier unit"], "correct": 1, "short": "Integrated Drive Generator (IDG).", "expl": "CSD + alternator combined = IDG.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "An aircraft AC electrical system must maintain a constant output voltage of:", "options": ["115–120 volts AC", "14–28 volts", "220 volts AC", "26 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "115–120 volts AC.", "expl": "At 400 Hz (±10%).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Which control unit handles AC generator functions such as voltage regulation and frequency control?", "options": ["GCU (Generator Control Unit)", "BPCU", "CSD", "TRU"], "correct": 0, "short": "GCU.", "expl": "The GCU controls the generator; the BPCU controls distribution.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Which control unit controls the distribution of electrical power between the various buses?", "options": ["GCU", "CT", "BPCU (Bus Power Control Unit)", "IDG"], "correct": 2, "short": "BPCU.", "expl": "The BPCU manages power distribution among buses.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "A current transformer (CT) in an AC distribution system:", "options": ["Increases the system voltage", "Stores charge like a capacitor", "Rectifies AC to DC", "Surrounds a main power cable and produces an induced voltage proportional to the current in that cable"], "correct": 3, "short": "Surrounds a cable; induced voltage ∝ current.", "expl": "A non-intrusive way to sense/monitor load current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Large transport aircraft use redundant control computers for AC power in order to:", "options": ["Reduce weight", "Provide safety in the event of a system failure", "Eliminate the need for a battery", "Increase the frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Provide safety if one system fails.", "expl": "Redundancy keeps AC power controlled if a computer fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}]}]}, {"n": 5, "day": "Unit 5", "title": "Electrical Systems & Distribution", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · aircraft circuits · inverters · paralleling · buses · split/parallel distribution", "sections": [{"id": "b6m5s1", "title": "Single-engine electrical system & circuits", "lesson": "\n<p>A <b>small single-engine aircraft</b> usually has <b>only one electrical system</b>.\nIts parts (p. 9-34):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An <b>engine-driven generator or alternator</b> — the main power source in flight.</li>\n<li>A <b>battery</b> — used for <b>emergency power</b> and <b>engine starting</b>.</li>\n<li>Power is distributed through one or more common points called an <b>electrical\nbus (bus bar)</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Two ways to protect a circuit</h3>\n<p>Electrical systems are protected two ways (p. 9-34):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Mechanically</b> — wires and components are protected from <b>abrasion and\nexcess wear</b> through proper installation and by adding <b>protective covers and\nshields</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Electrically</b> — wires are protected using <b>circuit breakers and\nfuses</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>The circuits in a small aircraft</h3>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide138_1.jpeg\" alt=\"battery circuit\"><figcaption>Battery circuit: a solenoid connects the high starting current.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Battery circuit</b> — supplies power for <b>engine starting</b> and\n<b>secondary power if the alternator fails</b>. It uses a <b>solenoid\n(contactor)</b> to connect the high current required for starting (p. 9-34).</li>\n<li><b>Generator circuit</b> / <b>Alternator circuit</b> — connects the engine-driven\npower source to the bus through its regulator/control.</li>\n<li><b>External power circuit</b> — lets a <b>ground power unit</b> plug in for\nstarting or ground checks without draining the battery.</li>\n<li><b>Starter circuit</b> — the <b>starter switch</b> energizes the starter solenoid,\nwhich feeds the <b>starter motor</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Avionics power circuit</b> — feeds the <b>avionics bus</b>, which is often\nisolated so avionics can be turned off during start to protect them from voltage\nspikes.</li>\n<li><b>Landing gear circuit</b> — controls and indicates the electrically-operated or\nelectrically-controlled landing gear.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide140_1.jpeg\" alt=\"alternator circuit\"><figcaption>Alternator circuit feeding the main bus.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Small single-engine aircraft = <b>one</b> electrical system: engine-driven generator/alternator + <b>battery</b> (emergency power & starting) + <b>bus (bus bar)</b>.</li>\n<li>Protect circuits <b>mechanically</b> (covers/shields, good installation) and <b>electrically</b> (fuses/circuit breakers).</li>\n<li>The <b>battery circuit uses a solenoid (contactor)</b> to handle the high starting current.</li>\n<li>The battery is <b>secondary power if the alternator fails</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A small single-engine aircraft usually has:", "options": ["Three independent electrical systems", "Two paralleled AC systems", "No electrical system", "Only one electrical system"], "correct": 3, "short": "Only one electrical system.", "expl": "Small aircraft have a single system: source + battery + bus.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "In a small aircraft, the battery is used for:", "options": ["Normal in-flight power generation", "Frequency control", "Emergency power and engine starting", "Cabin heating only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Emergency power and engine starting.", "expl": "The generator/alternator is the in-flight source; the battery backs it up and starts the engine.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Electrical power is distributed through one or more common points known as a(n):", "options": ["Commutator", "Rectifier", "Inverter", "Electrical bus (bus bar)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Electrical bus (bus bar).", "expl": "The bus is the common distribution point.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The two ways electrical systems are protected from faults are:", "options": ["Mechanically and electrically", "Magnetically and optically", "Hydraulically and pneumatically", "Chemically and thermally"], "correct": 0, "short": "Mechanically and electrically.", "expl": "Mechanically via installation/covers; electrically via fuses/breakers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Mechanical protection of aircraft wiring means protecting it from:", "options": ["Overcurrent", "Abrasion and excess wear", "Radio interference", "Voltage spikes"], "correct": 1, "short": "Abrasion and excess wear.", "expl": "Achieved by proper installation and protective covers/shields.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Electrical protection of aircraft wiring is provided by:", "options": ["Conduit", "Lacing cord", "Clamps and grommets", "Circuit breakers and fuses"], "correct": 3, "short": "Circuit breakers and fuses.", "expl": "These open the circuit on overcurrent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The battery circuit uses a ____ to connect the high current required for engine starting.", "options": ["capacitor", "resistor", "diode", "solenoid (contactor)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Solenoid (contactor).", "expl": "The solenoid handles the heavy starting current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The external power circuit allows:", "options": ["The alternator to be removed", "AC to be generated", "The battery to be bypassed permanently", "A ground power unit to be connected for starting/ground checks"], "correct": 3, "short": "A ground power unit to plug in.", "expl": "External power spares the battery for start/ground ops.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The avionics bus is often isolated so that:", "options": ["It can supply the starter", "It regulates the generator", "It powers the landing gear", "Avionics can be switched off during start to protect them from voltage spikes"], "correct": 3, "short": "Avionics can be off during start (spike protection).", "expl": "Starting causes voltage transients that can harm avionics.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "In the starter circuit, the starter switch:", "options": ["Directly carries the full starter current", "Regulates output voltage", "Charges the battery", "Energizes the starter solenoid, which feeds the starter motor"], "correct": 3, "short": "Energizes the starter solenoid.", "expl": "The solenoid then carries the heavy current to the starter motor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}]}, {"id": "b6m5s2", "title": "Inverters, multi-engine & paralleling", "lesson": "\n<h3>Making AC on a light aircraft — the inverter</h3>\n<p>Some light-aircraft instruments and equipment need <b>AC</b>, but the aircraft's\nsystem is DC. A device called an <b>inverter</b> supplies AC when needed by\n<b>changing DC into AC</b> (p. 9-36). Common inverter outputs are <b>26 volt AC</b>\nor <b>115 volt AC</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide148_1.jpeg\" alt=\"inverter\"><figcaption>An inverter changes DC into AC (26 V AC or 115 V AC).</figcaption></figure>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Don't confuse them</span> An <b>inverter</b> changes\n<b>DC &rarr; AC</b>. A <b>rectifier</b> changes <b>AC &rarr; DC</b>. A\n<b>transformer</b> changes AC voltage up or down (AC in, AC out).</div>\n\n<h3>Light multi-engine aircraft — safety and redundancy</h3>\n<p>A light <b>multi-engine</b> aircraft adds redundancy (p. 9-37):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Two generators or alternators</b> (one per engine).</li>\n<li>The electrical system is <b>divided into two or more systems</b>.</li>\n<li>Power reaches equipment through <b>bus distribution</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Paralleling alternators and generators</h3>\n<p>With two power sources on a twin, it is vital that <b>both share the load\nequally</b>. The paralleling system (p. 9-37):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keeps the two sources <b>within a few tenths of a volt</b> of each other so\nneither carries more than its share.</li>\n<li>Often uses a <b>vibrating-point system</b> for regulation on light twins.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide150_1.jpeg\" alt=\"paralleling alternators\"><figcaption>Paralleling keeps two sources sharing the load within a few tenths of a volt.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>An <b>inverter changes DC into AC</b>; typical light-aircraft outputs are <b>26 V AC</b> and <b>115 V AC</b>.</li>\n<li>Inverter = DC&rarr;AC; rectifier = AC&rarr;DC; transformer = AC voltage change.</li>\n<li>Light multi-engine = <b>two generators/alternators</b>, system split into <b>two or more</b> systems, bus distribution.</li>\n<li><b>Paralleling</b> makes two sources share the load equally, held within a <b>few tenths of a volt</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "An inverter is a device that:", "options": ["Changes AC into DC", "Steps AC voltage down", "Stores electrical charge", "Changes DC into AC"], "correct": 3, "short": "Changes DC into AC.", "expl": "Used to supply AC in a DC-powered light aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Typical light-aircraft inverter outputs are:", "options": ["6 or 12 volt DC", "14 or 28 volt DC", "26 or 115 volt AC", "400 or 800 volt AC"], "correct": 2, "short": "26 or 115 volt AC.", "expl": "Common AC values supplied by light-aircraft inverters.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Which device changes AC into DC?", "options": ["Rectifier", "Inverter", "Transformer", "Solenoid"], "correct": 0, "short": "Rectifier.", "expl": "Rectifier: AC→DC; inverter: DC→AC; transformer: AC voltage change.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A light multi-engine aircraft improves safety and redundancy by using:", "options": ["No battery", "Two generators or alternators with the system divided into two or more systems", "A single generator", "Only DC generators"], "correct": 1, "short": "Two sources, split into two or more systems.", "expl": "Redundancy so a single failure doesn't kill all power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "When two generators/alternators operate together on a twin, it is vital that they:", "options": ["Feed separate isolated systems only", "Never operate at the same time", "Share the electrical load equally", "Run at different voltages"], "correct": 2, "short": "Share the load equally.", "expl": "Paralleling balances the load between the two sources.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A paralleling system keeps the two power sources matched to within:", "options": ["Any value, it is not controlled", "A few tenths of a volt", "Within 50 volts", "Exactly 10 volts"], "correct": 1, "short": "A few tenths of a volt.", "expl": "Close voltage matching prevents one source carrying more than its share.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Light twins often regulate paralleled output using a:", "options": ["Constant-speed drive", "Rectifier bank", "Vibrating-point system", "Carbon-pile only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Vibrating-point system.", "expl": "A common regulation method for paralleling on light twins.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A transformer differs from an inverter in that a transformer:", "options": ["Changes DC to AC", "Changes AC to DC", "Changes AC voltage up or down (AC in, AC out)", "Stores charge"], "correct": 2, "short": "Changes AC voltage (AC in/AC out).", "expl": "Transformers only work on AC and change voltage level.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}]}, {"id": "b6m5s3", "title": "Buses & AC power distribution", "lesson": "\n<p>On multi-engine aircraft, the power-distribution system contains <b>several\ndistribution points (buses)</b> plus a variety of control and protection components\nto ensure reliable power (p. 9-38).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide151_1.jpeg\" alt=\"power distribution multiengine\"><figcaption>Multi-engine distribution: several buses with control and protection.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>The hot battery bus</h3>\n<p>The <b>hot battery bus</b> is <b>always powered</b> (connected directly to the\nbattery). It feeds <b>critical flight-safety systems</b> and is the <b>last bus to\nlose power</b> (p. 9-38). That is why essentials like emergency lighting and clocks\ntie to it.</p>\n\n<h3>Generator buses and dual-feed buses</h3>\n<p>Left and right <b>generator buses</b> normally operate <b>in parallel</b>, which\nalso <b>allows single-generator operation</b> if one source fails. They power the\n<b>dual-feed buses</b>. Each dual-feed bus is connected to the main generator buses\nthrough <b>both a current limiter and a diode</b> — and the <b>diode allows current\nto flow in only one direction</b>, isolating faults (p. 9-38).</p>\n\n<h3>Large multi-engine aircraft</h3>\n<p>Large multi-engine aircraft are <b>extremely reliable</b> and primarily AC\n(p. 9-39):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Multiple AC power sources</b> — two or more AC generators.</li>\n<li>A variety of <b>distribution buses</b>.</li>\n<li><b>DC power obtained through rectification</b>.</li>\n<li>Generators driven by the <b>engine, Auxiliary Power Unit (APU), or Ram Air\nTurbine (RAT)</b> — the RAT is a wind-driven emergency source.</li>\n<li>All <b>GCU controlled</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Three AC distribution architectures</h3>\n<div style=\"display:flex;gap:8px;flex-wrap:wrap\">\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:150px\"><img src=\"img/slide156_1.jpeg\" alt=\"split-bus\"><figcaption>Split-bus</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:150px\"><img src=\"img/slide157_1.jpeg\" alt=\"parallel bus\"><figcaption>Parallel</figcaption></figure>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\" style=\"flex:1;min-width:150px\"><img src=\"img/slide158_1.jpeg\" alt=\"split-parallel bus\"><figcaption>Split-parallel</figcaption></figure>\n</div>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Split-bus</b> — each generator powers its <b>own isolated bus</b>; generators\nare <b>not</b> paralleled (common on twin-engine transports).</li>\n<li><b>Parallel</b> — all generators are <b>tied together</b> to feed one common\nsystem, sharing the total load.</li>\n<li><b>Split-parallel</b> — a flexible combination that can run isolated or\nparalleled as needed (used on large four-engine aircraft).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>hot battery bus is always powered</b>, feeds critical systems, and is the <b>last bus to lose power</b>.</li>\n<li>Generator buses normally run <b>in parallel</b>, allowing <b>single-generator operation</b>.</li>\n<li>Dual-feed buses connect through a <b>current limiter and a diode</b>; the <b>diode passes current one direction only</b>.</li>\n<li>Large aircraft: multiple AC generators, DC by <b>rectification</b>, driven by engine/<b>APU</b>/<b>RAT</b>, GCU controlled.</li>\n<li>AC architectures: <b>split-bus</b> (isolated), <b>parallel</b> (all tied), <b>split-parallel</b> (flexible).</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The hot battery bus is characterized by being:", "options": ["Powered only during engine start", "Always powered and the last bus to lose power", "Used only for avionics", "Powered only by the alternator"], "correct": 1, "short": "Always powered; last to lose power.", "expl": "It feeds critical flight-safety systems directly from the battery.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The hot battery bus typically feeds:", "options": ["Ground service outlets", "The starter motor only", "Critical flight-safety systems", "Non-essential cabin loads"], "correct": 2, "short": "Critical flight-safety systems.", "expl": "Essentials tie to the always-powered hot battery bus.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Left and right generator buses normally operate:", "options": ["Completely isolated at all times", "At different frequencies", "In parallel, which also allows single-generator operation", "Only on battery power"], "correct": 2, "short": "In parallel (allows single-generator operation).", "expl": "Paralleled buses keep power available if one generator fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Dual-feed buses are connected to the main generator buses through both a current limiter and a:", "options": ["Capacitor", "Transformer", "Diode", "Slip ring"], "correct": 2, "short": "Diode.", "expl": "The diode allows current to flow in only one direction, isolating faults.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The diode feeding a dual-feed bus:", "options": ["Allows current to flow in only one direction", "Converts AC to DC for the whole aircraft", "Blocks all current", "Allows current to flow both directions"], "correct": 0, "short": "Allows current one direction only.", "expl": "That's the defining property of a diode.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "On a large multi-engine aircraft, DC power is obtained through:", "options": ["The hot battery bus only", "A commutator", "Rectification", "An inverter"], "correct": 2, "short": "Rectification.", "expl": "TRUs rectify the primary AC to DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A wind-driven emergency electrical power source on large aircraft is the:", "options": ["APU", "Ram Air Turbine (RAT)", "CSD", "GCU"], "correct": 1, "short": "Ram Air Turbine (RAT).", "expl": "The RAT deploys into the airstream for emergency power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "In a split-bus power distribution system:", "options": ["All generators are tied together to one bus", "There is only one generator", "Each generator powers its own isolated bus (not paralleled)", "DC only is distributed"], "correct": 2, "short": "Each generator on its own isolated bus.", "expl": "Split-bus keeps sources separate; common on twin transports.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "In a parallel bus system:", "options": ["Each generator is isolated", "Only the battery supplies power", "All generators are tied together to feed one common system sharing the load", "No generators are used"], "correct": 2, "short": "All generators tied together, sharing load.", "expl": "Parallel = generators combined onto one system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A split-parallel bus system is best described as:", "options": ["Single-generator only", "Always fully isolated", "A flexible combination that can run isolated or paralleled as needed", "DC only"], "correct": 2, "short": "A flexible isolated/paralleled combination.", "expl": "Used on large four-engine aircraft for flexibility.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Auxiliary power for a large aircraft's electrical system on the ground is commonly provided by the:", "options": ["APU (Auxiliary Power Unit)", "CSD", "Inverter", "RAT"], "correct": 0, "short": "APU.", "expl": "The APU can drive a generator for ground and backup power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}]}]}, {"n": 6, "day": "Unit 6", "title": "Aircraft Wiring: Diagrams, Types, Sizing, ID & Routing", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · wiring diagrams · wire/insulation · AWG/voltage drop · marking · installation", "sections": [{"id": "b6m6s1", "title": "Wiring diagrams, wire types & materials", "lesson": "\n<h3>Wiring diagrams</h3>\n<p>Wiring diagrams are used for <b>troubleshooting electrical malfunctions</b>.\nThere are three kinds (p. 9-40):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Block diagram</b> — shows the system as labeled boxes and the connections\nbetween them; the big-picture overview.</li>\n<li><b>Pictorial diagram</b> — uses <b>pictures of the actual components</b> to show\nhow things connect; easiest to relate to the real aircraft.</li>\n<li><b>Schematic diagram</b> — uses <b>symbols</b> to show every component and\nconnection; the detailed diagram used for actual troubleshooting.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide162_1.jpeg\" alt=\"wiring diagram\"><figcaption>A schematic wiring diagram uses symbols to show every connection.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Wire types</h3>\n<p>Two wire types are used, both covered in an insulating material (p. 9-41):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Solid</b> wire — a single conductor.</li>\n<li><b>Stranded</b> wire — many small strands twisted together; more flexible and\nresistant to vibration/flexing fatigue, so it is preferred on aircraft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The term <b>wire harness</b> is used when an array of insulated conductors is\n<b>bound together by lacing cord, metal bands, or other binding</b> (p. 9-41).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide165_1.jpeg\" alt=\"wire types\"><figcaption>Solid vs. stranded wire, both insulated.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Conductor materials & plating</h3>\n<p>The two most-used conductors are <b>copper and aluminum</b> (p. 9-41). Copper\nforms a green surface oxide (<b>copper oxide</b> — \"the green stuff\"), so copper\nwires are <b>coated (plated) with tin, silver, or nickel</b>; each coating material\nhas a <b>temperature limit</b> (p. 9-41).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide166_1.jpeg\" alt=\"conductor\"><figcaption>Copper and aluminum are the two common conductors.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Insulation</h3>\n<p>Insulation is selected for its <b>resistance</b> and <b>dielectric strength</b>,\nbased on the <b>environmental conditions</b>. Common aircraft insulations include\n<b>Tefzel</b>, Teflon/Kapton/Teflon, and PTFE/Polyimide/PTFE (p. 9-42).</p>\n\n<h3>Shielding & EMI</h3>\n<p><b>Shielding</b> is applying a <b>metallic covering</b> to wiring/equipment to\n<b>eliminate electromagnetic interference (EMI)</b>. EMI is caused when\nelectromagnetic fields (radio waves) <b>induce high-frequency (HF) voltages</b> in a\nwire or component; those induced voltages can cause <b>system inaccuracies or even\nfailure</b> (p. 9-42).</p>\n\n<h3>Wire substitution</h3>\n<p>If a wire must be replaced, the <b>Aircraft Maintenance Manual (AMM) must be\nfollowed</b>. Any replacement <b>not in the AMM</b> must be approved by the\n<b>Original Aircraft Manufacturer (OAM)</b> (p. 9-42).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Wiring diagram types: <b>block, pictorial, schematic</b>. Schematic = symbols, used to troubleshoot.</li>\n<li>Wire types: <b>solid</b> and <b>stranded</b> (both insulated); <b>wire harness</b> = conductors bound together.</li>\n<li>Common conductors: <b>copper and aluminum</b>. Copper is plated with <b>tin, silver, or nickel</b> (each has a temp limit).</li>\n<li>Insulation chosen for resistance & <b>dielectric strength</b>; e.g., <b>Tefzel</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Shielding</b> = metallic covering to eliminate <b>EMI</b> (induced HF voltages).</li>\n<li>Replacements follow the <b>AMM</b>; anything not in the AMM needs <b>OAM</b> approval.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Wiring diagrams are primarily used for:", "options": ["Corrosion control", "Troubleshooting electrical malfunctions", "Weight and balance", "Fuel planning"], "correct": 1, "short": "Troubleshooting electrical malfunctions.", "expl": "They map the electrical system for fault-finding.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The three types of wiring diagrams are:", "options": ["Block, pictorial, and schematic", "Solid, stranded, and shielded", "Series, parallel, and compound", "Rigid, flexible, and open"], "correct": 0, "short": "Block, pictorial, and schematic.", "expl": "Three standard diagram types.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A schematic wiring diagram represents components using:", "options": ["Color codes only", "Photographs", "Boxes only", "Symbols"], "correct": 3, "short": "Symbols.", "expl": "The schematic uses symbols and is used for detailed troubleshooting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The two types of wire (both insulated) used on aircraft are:", "options": ["Solid and stranded", "AC and DC", "Bare and coated", "Copper and Tefzel"], "correct": 0, "short": "Solid and stranded.", "expl": "Stranded is preferred on aircraft for flexibility/vibration resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "An array of insulated conductors bound together by lacing cord, metal bands, or other binding is called a:", "options": ["Wire harness", "Wire group", "Conduit", "Bus bar"], "correct": 0, "short": "Wire harness.", "expl": "A harness is the bound-together assembly of conductors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The two most commonly used conductor materials for aircraft wire are:", "options": ["Silver and gold", "Steel and iron", "Nickel and tin", "Copper and aluminum"], "correct": 3, "short": "Copper and aluminum.", "expl": "Copper and aluminum are the standard conductors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Copper aircraft wire is plated (coated) with tin, silver, or nickel primarily to:", "options": ["Change it into aluminum", "Prevent copper oxide and set a temperature limit for the wire", "Increase its resistance", "Make it heavier"], "correct": 1, "short": "Prevent copper oxide; each coating has a temp limit.", "expl": "Copper oxidizes (the green stuff); plating protects it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Aircraft wire insulation is selected primarily based on:", "options": ["The length of the run only", "The color of the wire", "Its dielectric strength/resistance and the environmental conditions", "The gauge number only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Dielectric strength/resistance and environment.", "expl": "Tefzel and similar insulations are chosen for the conditions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Shielding is defined as applying a ____ covering to eliminate ____.", "options": ["metallic; electromagnetic interference (EMI)", "rubber; abrasion", "plastic; corrosion", "ceramic; heat"], "correct": 0, "short": "Metallic covering to eliminate EMI.", "expl": "Shielding drains induced HF voltages to ground.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "EMI is caused when electromagnetic fields induce ____ in a wire or component.", "options": ["mechanical vibration", "corrosion", "high-frequency (HF) voltages", "excess heat"], "correct": 2, "short": "High-frequency (HF) voltages.", "expl": "These induced voltages can cause inaccuracies or failure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "If a wire must be replaced with something not listed in the AMM, the replacement must be approved by the:", "options": ["Pilot in command", "Local A&P mechanic", "Original Aircraft Manufacturer (OAM)", "FCC"], "correct": 2, "short": "The Original Aircraft Manufacturer (OAM).", "expl": "The AMM governs; deviations need OAM approval.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}]}, {"id": "b6m6s2", "title": "Wire selection & sizing (AWG, voltage drop, SWAMP)", "lesson": "\n<h3>American Wire Gauge (AWG)</h3>\n<p>Aircraft wire is sized by a standard called the <b>American Wire Gauge (AWG)</b>.\nThe key rule that trips people up: <b>wire diameters get SMALLER as the gauge\nnumbers get LARGER</b> (p. 9-43).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Typical wire sizes range from <b>#40 (very small)</b> to <b>#0000 (very\nlarge)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>24-gauge is the smallest wire normally used on aircraft</b> (p. 9-43).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide173_1.jpeg\" alt=\"AWG wire size chart\"><figcaption>Wire-size chart — bigger gauge number means a smaller wire.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Current-carrying capacity</h3>\n<p>Wire size is chosen so it can safely carry the circuit current. But watch the\nconnector: in some cases a wire can carry <b>more current than the contacts of its\nrelated connector</b> can handle. In that case, it is the <b>contact rating that\ndictates the maximum current</b> to be carried by the wire (p. 9-44).</p>\n\n<h3>Allowable voltage drop</h3>\n<p>Wire must also be big enough to keep <b>voltage drop</b> within limits. The\nvoltage drop in the <b>main power wires</b> from the generation source (or the\nbattery) to the bus should <b>not exceed 2 percent of the regulated voltage</b> when\nthe generator is carrying rated current, or the battery is being discharged at the\n<b>5-minute rate</b> (p. 9-45).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide179_1.jpeg\" alt=\"allowable voltage drop\"><figcaption>Main-power-wire voltage drop is limited to 2% of regulated voltage.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>SWAMP areas</h3>\n<p><b>SWAMP</b> stands for <b>Severe Wind And Moisture Prone</b> areas. They vary\nfrom aircraft to aircraft but are usually (p. 9-45):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Wheel wells</b></li>\n<li><b>Wing flap areas</b></li>\n<li><b>Wing folds</b></li>\n<li><b>Pylons</b></li>\n<li>Other <b>exterior areas</b> with a harsh environment.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wire chosen for SWAMP areas must tolerate that harsh environment.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>AWG</b>: bigger gauge number = <b>smaller</b> wire. Range <b>#40 to #0000</b>; <b>24-gauge</b> is the smallest normally used on aircraft.</li>\n<li>If the connector contacts can't handle the wire's current, the <b>contact rating</b> sets the max current.</li>\n<li>Main-power-wire voltage drop &le; <b>2%</b> of regulated voltage (generator at rated current / battery at 5-minute rate).</li>\n<li><b>SWAMP</b> = <b>S</b>evere <b>W</b>ind <b>A</b>nd <b>M</b>oisture <b>P</b>rone: wheel wells, flap areas, wing folds, pylons.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Aircraft wire is sized according to the standard known as:", "options": ["AN/MS", "Ohm's Law", "American Wire Gauge (AWG)", "SWAMP"], "correct": 2, "short": "American Wire Gauge (AWG).", "expl": "AWG is the wire-sizing standard.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "As AWG gauge numbers get larger, the wire diameter:", "options": ["Gets larger", "Stays the same", "Gets smaller", "Doubles"], "correct": 2, "short": "Gets smaller.", "expl": "Bigger gauge number = smaller wire — a common trick question.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Typical aircraft wire sizes range from:", "options": ["#1 to #10", "#40 to #0000", "#10 to #20", "#0000 to #00000"], "correct": 1, "short": "#40 to #0000.", "expl": "#40 is very small; #0000 is very large.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The smallest wire size normally used on aircraft is:", "options": ["40-gauge", "14-gauge", "24-gauge", "20-gauge"], "correct": 2, "short": "24-gauge.", "expl": "24-gauge is the smallest normally used on aircraft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "When a wire can carry more current than its connector's contacts can handle, the maximum current is dictated by the:", "options": ["Length of the wire", "Contact (connector) rating", "Wire's cross-sectional area", "Insulation color"], "correct": 1, "short": "The contact (connector) rating.", "expl": "The weakest link — the contact rating — limits the current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The voltage drop in the main power wires from the source/battery to the bus should not exceed:", "options": ["1 percent of regulated voltage", "10 percent of regulated voltage", "2 percent of regulated voltage", "5 percent of regulated voltage"], "correct": 2, "short": "2 percent of regulated voltage.", "expl": "Measured with generator at rated current or battery at the 5-minute rate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The allowable main-power-wire voltage drop is checked with the battery being discharged at the:", "options": ["5-minute rate", "20-hour rate", "continuous rate", "1-hour rate"], "correct": 0, "short": "5-minute rate.", "expl": "The 2% limit is referenced to the 5-minute discharge rate (or generator rated current).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "SWAMP stands for:", "options": ["Severe Wind And Moisture Prone", "Sealed Wiring And Marking Procedure", "Special Wire And Metal Plating", "Standard Wire And Metric Practice"], "correct": 0, "short": "Severe Wind And Moisture Prone.", "expl": "Harsh environmental areas needing special wire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Which of the following is typically a SWAMP area?", "options": ["A wheel well", "The overhead reading light", "The instrument panel", "The center console"], "correct": 0, "short": "A wheel well.", "expl": "Wheel wells, wing flap areas, wing folds, and pylons are SWAMP areas.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Which is NOT normally considered a SWAMP area?", "options": ["Wing flap area", "A dry, heated cabin panel", "Pylons", "Wing folds"], "correct": 1, "short": "A dry, heated cabin panel.", "expl": "SWAMP areas are harsh/exterior; a dry cabin panel is not.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}]}, {"id": "b6m6s3", "title": "Wire identification & marking", "lesson": "\n<h3>What the markings mean</h3>\n<p>Aircraft wiring and cable may be marked with a combination of <b>letters,\nnumbers, and colors</b> to identify the wire, the circuit it belongs to, the gauge\nnumber, and other information needed to relate the wire to a wiring diagram\n(p. 9-46).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide183_1.jpeg\" alt=\"wire identification\"><figcaption>A wire identification code ties the wire back to the wiring diagram.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Placement of identification markings (know the numbers)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Markings should be placed at <b>each end of the wire and at 15-inch maximum\nintervals</b> along its length (p. 9-46).</li>\n<li><b>Indirect marking</b> (printing on a heat-shrinkable sleeve installed on the\nwire's outer covering) should be identified at <b>each end and at intervals not\nlonger than 6 feet</b> (p. 9-46).</li>\n<li>The <b>individual wires inside a cable</b> should be identified <b>within 3\ninches of their termination</b> (p. 9-46).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Marking methods</h3>\n<p>Several methods mark directly on the wire: <b>hot-stamp marking, ink-jet\nprinters, and laser-jet printers</b>. Important caution: the <b>hot-stamp method can\ndamage the insulation</b> of newer wire types that use <b>thin insulators</b>\n(p. 9-47).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide187_1.jpeg\" alt=\"wire marking method\"><figcaption>Direct marking methods: hot stamp, ink-jet, laser.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Markings identify the wire, its <b>circuit</b>, and its <b>gauge</b> via letters/numbers/colors.</li>\n<li>Direct marking: <b>each end + every 15 inches (max)</b>.</li>\n<li>Indirect marking (heat-shrink sleeve): <b>each end + intervals no longer than 6 ft</b>.</li>\n<li>Individual wires in a cable: identified within <b>3 inches</b> of termination.</li>\n<li>Methods: <b>hot stamp, ink-jet, laser</b>. <b>Hot stamp can damage thin insulation</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Aircraft wire markings use a combination of letters, numbers, and colors to identify the wire, its circuit, and its:", "options": ["Color of insulation only", "Gauge number", "Length", "Manufacturer's phone number"], "correct": 1, "short": "Gauge number.", "expl": "Plus other info to relate the wire to the wiring diagram.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Wire identification markings should be placed at each end of the wire and at intervals of at most:", "options": ["6 inches", "12 inches", "15 inches", "36 inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "15 inches.", "expl": "Direct marking: each end plus every 15 inches maximum.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Indirect marking (heat-shrink sleeve) should identify the wire at each end and at intervals no longer than:", "options": ["6 feet", "10 feet", "3 feet", "15 inches"], "correct": 0, "short": "6 feet.", "expl": "Indirect marking interval is 6 ft max.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Individual wires inside a cable should be identified within how far of their termination?", "options": ["6 inches", "3 inches", "1 inch", "15 inches"], "correct": 1, "short": "3 inches.", "expl": "Inner cable wires: identified within 3 inches of termination.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Methods used to mark directly on a wire include hot stamp, ink jet, and:", "options": ["Engraving chisel", "Laser jet printers", "Paint brush", "Adhesive tape only"], "correct": 1, "short": "Laser jet printers.", "expl": "Hot stamp, ink jet, and laser are the direct methods.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A caution about the hot-stamp marking method is that it can:", "options": ["Only be used on aluminum wire", "Damage the insulation of newer wire types that use thin insulators", "Not be read by technicians", "Fade quickly"], "correct": 1, "short": "Damage thin insulation on newer wire types.", "expl": "Thin-insulation wire can be harmed by hot stamping.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Indirect marking is accomplished by printing on a ____ and installing it on the wire's outer covering.", "options": ["heat-shrinkable sleeve", "metal band", "paper tag", "clamp"], "correct": 0, "short": "Heat-shrinkable sleeve.", "expl": "The printed sleeve is shrunk onto the wire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}]}, {"id": "b6m6s4", "title": "Wire installation & routing", "lesson": "\n<h3>Open wiring, groups, and bundles</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Open wiring</b> — any wire, wire group, or wire bundle <b>not enclosed in\nconduit</b> (p. 9-47).</li>\n<li><b>Wire group</b> — <b>two or more wires going to the same location</b>,\nphysically tied together to retain their group identity (p. 9-47).</li>\n<li><b>Wire bundle</b> — <b>two or more wire groups</b> tied together because they\nare going in the same direction at the point of the tie (p. 9-48).</li>\n<li><b>Electrically protected wiring</b> — includes in-circuit protection such as\nfuses, circuit breakers, or other limiting devices.</li>\n<li><b>Electrically unprotected wiring</b> — wires or large cables (generally from\nthe generator to the main bus) that <b>do not have that protection</b> (p. 9-48).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Slack (sag), twisting, and support</h3>\n<p><b>Bundle slack (sag)</b>: the run should be <b>neither unduly taut nor\nexcessively slack</b>. A suitable amount of slack between supports normally does not\nexceed a maximum of <b>&frac12; inch deflection</b> with normal hand force\n(p. 9-48).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide191_1.jpeg\" alt=\"bundle sag\"><figcaption>Bundle slack: up to about &frac12; inch deflection under normal hand force.</figcaption></figure>\n<p><b>Twisting</b> parallel wires is required in some places — most commonly wiring\nnear the <b>magnetic compass or flux valve</b>, <b>three-phase distribution\nwiring</b>, and certain <b>radio wiring</b> (p. 9-49).</p>\n<p>Bundles must be <b>adequately supported</b> throughout their length; <b>clamps</b>\nshould have a <b>snug fit without pinching</b> the wires, and <b>nonmetallic clamp\nmaterial</b> is used to support the bundle along the run (p. 9-51).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide192_1.jpeg\" alt=\"twisted wires\"><figcaption>Twisting parallel wires near compasses/flux valves and for three-phase/radio wiring.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Splices and bends</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Splices should be located so they can be <b>easily inspected</b>, and must be\n<b>staggered</b> to prevent the bundle from becoming excessively enlarged.</li>\n<li>There should be <b>no more than one splice in any one wire segment</b> between\nany two connectors or other disconnect points (p. 9-49).</li>\n<li><b>Radius of bends</b> in wire groups or bundles should be <b>not less than 10\ntimes the outside diameter of the largest wire</b> in the group/bundle (p. 9-49).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Routing hazards</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Route to prevent <b>chafing or abrasion</b>, and away from <b>high-temperature</b>\nequipment and lines.</li>\n<li>An <b>arcing fault</b> between an electrical wire and a metallic flammable-fluid\nline may <b>puncture the line and result in a fire</b> — so physically <b>separate\nwire from lines/equipment containing oxygen, fuel, oil, hydraulic fluid, or\nalcohol</b> (p. 9-50).</li>\n<li>Route wires so that <b>fluids drain away from the connectors</b>.</li>\n<li>Route <b>parallel with or at right angle to</b> the airframe stringers/ribs —\n<b>except coaxial cable</b>, which is routed as <b>directly as possible</b> to avoid\nsignal distortion and minimize line loss (p. 9-51).</li>\n<li>Minimum separation between wiring and <b>movable controls</b> must be at least\n<b>&frac12; inch</b> when the bundle is displaced by light hand pressure toward the\ncontrols (p. 9-51).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Conduit</h3>\n<p><b>Conduit</b> is a protective tubular covering for wiring, in <b>rigid</b> and\n<b>flexible</b> forms. The <b>wheel well</b> of a retractable-gear aircraft is the\nmost difficult area for wiring safety, so whenever possible wiring there should be in\nconduit (p. 9-52). Sizing: the conduit's <b>inner diameter should be about 25%\nlarger</b> than the anticipated maximum diameter of the wire bundle. Blowing\n<b>powdered soapstone or tire talcum</b> through the conduit first makes pulling the\nwires much easier (p. 9-52). Where wire in protective tubing has a <b>low point</b>\nbetween the tubing ends, provide a <b>1/8-inch drain hole</b> (p. 9-52).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide203_1.jpeg\" alt=\"conduit routing\"><figcaption>Wheel-well wiring is routed in rigid or flexible conduit where possible.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Open wiring</b> = not in conduit. <b>Group</b> = wires to same location tied together; <b>bundle</b> = groups tied together going the same way.</li>\n<li>Slack max &asymp; <b>&frac12; inch</b> deflection. Bend radius &ge; <b>10&times;</b> largest wire diameter.</li>\n<li><b>One splice max per wire segment</b> between disconnects; splices <b>staggered</b>.</li>\n<li>Twist parallel wires near <b>compass/flux valve</b>, for <b>3-phase</b>, and certain <b>radio</b> wiring.</li>\n<li>Keep <b>&frac12; inch</b> from movable controls; separate from fuel/oil/hydraulic/oxygen lines (arc can start a fire).</li>\n<li>Coax is routed <b>as directly as possible</b> (not parallel to stringers) to minimize loss.</li>\n<li>Conduit ID &asymp; <b>25% larger</b> than bundle; use <b>soapstone/talcum</b> to pull; <b>1/8-inch drain hole</b> at low points.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Open wiring is defined as any wire, group, or bundle that is:", "options": ["Spliced", "Enclosed in conduit", "Shielded", "Not enclosed in conduit"], "correct": 3, "short": "Not enclosed in conduit.", "expl": "Open wiring = not in conduit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A wire group is:", "options": ["Any shielded cable", "Two or more bundles tied together", "Two or more wires going to the same location, tied together to retain group identity", "A single wire in conduit"], "correct": 2, "short": "Wires to the same location tied together.", "expl": "A group keeps its identity; bundles are groups tied together.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A wire bundle is:", "options": ["A grounded shield", "A single solid wire", "Any wire in a SWAMP area", "Two or more wire groups tied together going the same direction"], "correct": 3, "short": "Two or more groups tied together, same direction.", "expl": "Bundles combine groups heading the same way.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Electrically unprotected wiring is generally found:", "options": ["On avionics circuits with breakers", "Inside conduit only", "On lighting circuits", "From the generator to the main bus distribution points"], "correct": 3, "short": "From the generator to the main bus.", "expl": "These large cables lack in-line fuses/breakers.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Bundle slack (sag) between supports should normally not exceed a maximum deflection of:", "options": ["1 inch", "2 inches", "1/2 inch", "1/8 inch"], "correct": 2, "short": "1/2 inch.", "expl": "Up to about 1/2 inch deflection under normal hand force.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Parallel wires most commonly need to be twisted in the vicinity of a:", "options": ["Fuel tank", "Cabin door", "Magnetic compass or flux valve", "Landing light"], "correct": 2, "short": "Magnetic compass or flux valve.", "expl": "Also for three-phase distribution and certain radio wiring.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The maximum number of splices allowed in any one wire segment between two connectors/disconnects is:", "options": ["Two", "One", "Unlimited", "Three"], "correct": 1, "short": "One.", "expl": "No more than one splice per wire segment; splices are staggered.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The radius of bends in wire groups or bundles should be not less than ____ the outside diameter of the largest wire.", "options": ["25 times", "5 times", "10 times", "2 times"], "correct": 2, "short": "10 times.", "expl": "Minimum bend radius = 10× the largest wire's OD.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "An arcing fault between a wire and a metallic flammable-fluid line can:", "options": ["Puncture the line and result in a fire", "Only trip a breaker", "Have no consequence", "Improve grounding"], "correct": 0, "short": "Puncture the line and cause a fire.", "expl": "That's why wires are separated from fuel/oil/hydraulic/oxygen lines.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Unlike most wiring, coaxial cable should be routed:", "options": ["As directly as possible to avoid signal distortion and minimize line loss", "Parallel to stringers only", "Only through SWAMP areas", "In the longest path available"], "correct": 0, "short": "As directly as possible.", "expl": "Direct routing minimizes coax signal loss/distortion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Minimum separation between wiring and movable controls (bundle displaced by light hand pressure) must be at least:", "options": ["1 inch", "1/8 inch", "1/2 inch", "3 inches"], "correct": 2, "short": "1/2 inch.", "expl": "Keep at least 1/2 inch from movable controls.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The inner diameter of conduit should be approximately how much larger than the maximum wire-bundle diameter?", "options": ["Exactly the same", "25% larger", "10% larger", "50% larger"], "correct": 1, "short": "25% larger.", "expl": "Conduit ID ≈ 25% larger than the bundle to allow pulling.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "To make pulling wires through conduit easier, a technician can first blow through it:", "options": ["Sand", "Water", "Powdered soapstone or tire talcum", "Oil"], "correct": 2, "short": "Powdered soapstone or tire talcum.", "expl": "It lubricates the pull.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "When wire in protective tubing has a low point between the tubing ends, provide a drain hole of:", "options": ["1/8 inch", "1/4 inch", "1/2 inch", "1/16 inch"], "correct": 0, "short": "1/8 inch.", "expl": "A 1/8-inch drain hole at the low point lets fluid escape.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}]}]}, {"n": 7, "day": "Unit 7", "title": "Shielding/Bonding, Lacing, Connectors, Switches & Protection", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · shielding/grounding/bonding · lacing · terminals/AN-MS/coax · switches/fuses/CBs", "sections": [{"id": "b6m7s1", "title": "Shielding, grounding & bonding", "lesson": "\n<h3>Shielding</h3>\n<p>The <b>braided shield</b> of a shielded conductor is commonly connected to\n<b>airframe ground on each (both) ends</b> of the shielded cable run. This provides a\npath to ground for any <b>electromagnetic interference (EMI)</b> that could be\nintroduced into the center conductor (p. 9-53).</p>\n<p>Key shielding numbers/facts (p. 9-54):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The shield must cover at least <b>85% of the wire</b> to be effective.</li>\n<li>It <b>blocks high-frequency (HF) voltage</b> from coupling into the conductor.</li>\n<li>It is <b>grounded to the aircraft structure</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide207_1.jpeg\" alt=\"wire shielding\"><figcaption>A braided shield grounded to structure drains EMI away from the center conductor.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Grounding</h3>\n<p><b>Grounding</b> is connecting a metal object, wiring, or other conductive\nmaterial to the aircraft <b>primary structure</b> so that a <b>return path for\ncurrent is established, completing the circuit</b> (p. 9-54). Good practice:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Avoid mixing return currents</b> from various sources — noise couples from one\nto another, a major problem for digital systems.</li>\n<li>The ground return circuit deserves as much design attention as any other lead.</li>\n<li>Reduce EMI by locating the ground return <b>near the origin of the power\nwiring</b> (e.g., the circuit-breaker panel) and routing the power wire and its\nground return as a <b>twisted pair</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Bonding</h3>\n<p><b>Bonding</b> is the electrical connecting of <b>two or more conducting objects\nnot otherwise adequately connected</b> (p. 9-55). Three bonding categories:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Equipment bonding</b> — low-impedance paths to structure for RF return\ncircuits and to reduce EMI.</li>\n<li><b>Metallic surface bonding</b> — all exterior conducting objects connected to\nthe airframe through mechanical joints, conductive hinges, or <b>bond straps</b> so\nthey can conduct <b>static charges and lightning strikes</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Static bonds</b> — isolated conducting parts with an area greater than\n<b>3 square inches</b> and a linear dimension over <b>3 inches</b> that are subject\nto static charging should have a mechanically secure electrical connection to\nstructure.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Testing bonds and grounds (know the number)</h3>\n<p>The resistance of each bond/ground connection should normally <b>not exceed\n0.003 ohms</b>. Bonding jumpers should be made <b>as short as possible</b>, installed\nso that each connection's resistance <b>does not exceed 0.003 ohms</b> (p. 9-55).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide210_1.jpeg\" alt=\"grounding and bonding\"><figcaption>Bond/ground connection resistance must not exceed 0.003 ohms.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Shield grounded to structure at <b>both ends</b>; must cover <b>85%</b> of the wire; blocks <b>HF voltage/EMI</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Grounding</b> establishes the current <b>return path</b> to primary structure; don't mix return currents.</li>\n<li><b>Bonding</b> connects conductors not otherwise connected: equipment, metallic surface (lightning/static), and static bonds.</li>\n<li>Static bond threshold: area &gt; <b>3 sq in</b> and dimension over <b>3 in</b>.</li>\n<li>Bond/ground resistance &le; <b>0.003 ohms</b>; jumpers as <b>short as possible</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The braided shield of a shielded conductor is normally connected to airframe ground:", "options": ["At one end only", "On each (both) ends of the run", "Nowhere — it floats", "In the middle only"], "correct": 1, "short": "On both ends of the run.", "expl": "Grounding both ends provides a path for EMI in the center conductor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "To be effective, a wire shield must cover at least what percent of the wire?", "options": ["85%", "50%", "100%", "70%"], "correct": 0, "short": "85%.", "expl": "Shield coverage must be at least 85%.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The main purpose of shielding a wire is to:", "options": ["Block high-frequency (HF) voltage / EMI", "Reduce the wire's weight", "Add mechanical strength only", "Increase current capacity"], "correct": 0, "short": "Block HF voltage/EMI.", "expl": "The grounded shield drains induced HF interference.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Grounding is defined as connecting conductive material to the aircraft primary structure to:", "options": ["Establish a return path for current, completing the circuit", "Insulate the wire", "Store static charge", "Increase resistance"], "correct": 0, "short": "Establish the current return path.", "expl": "Ground completes the circuit back to the source.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Mixing return currents from various sources should be avoided because it:", "options": ["Couples noise from one source to another (a problem for digital systems)", "Improves bonding", "Saves weight", "Increases voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Couples noise between sources.", "expl": "Especially harmful to digital systems.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Bonding is the electrical connecting of:", "options": ["AC to DC", "Two or more conducting objects not otherwise adequately connected", "A battery to a charger", "A wire to its own insulation"], "correct": 1, "short": "Two or more conductors not otherwise connected.", "expl": "Bonding ties conductive parts together electrically.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Metallic surface bonding of exterior conducting objects is required mainly to safely conduct:", "options": ["Audio signals", "Cabin pressure", "Fuel vapors", "Static charges and lightning strikes"], "correct": 3, "short": "Static charges and lightning strikes.", "expl": "Bond straps/hinges carry static and lightning to structure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A static bond is required for isolated conducting parts with an area greater than ____ and a linear dimension over ____.", "options": ["1 sq in; 1 in", "10 sq in; 10 in", "3 sq in; 3 in", "6 sq in; 6 in"], "correct": 2, "short": "3 sq in; 3 in.", "expl": "Threshold for requiring a static bond to structure.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The resistance of each bond or ground connection should normally not exceed:", "options": ["0.003 ohms", "3 ohms", "0.03 ohms", "0.3 ohms"], "correct": 0, "short": "0.003 ohms.", "expl": "Bond/ground connection resistance limit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Bonding jumpers should be installed:", "options": ["As long as possible for flexibility", "As short as possible, with connection resistance not exceeding 0.003 ohms", "Only on non-conductive parts", "Without regard to resistance"], "correct": 1, "short": "As short as possible; ≤ 0.003 ohms.", "expl": "Short jumpers keep impedance low.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}]}, {"id": "b6m7s2", "title": "Lacing & tying wire bundles", "lesson": "\n<p>Bundles are held together by lacing or tying (p. 9-56):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lacing</b> secures a group/bundle with a <b>continuous piece of cord</b>,\nforming loops at regular intervals around the bundle.</li>\n<li><b>Tying</b> secures the bundle with <b>individual ties</b> placed at regular\nintervals.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Today the <b>preferred method is nylon wire ties</b>; in the past, lacing cord or\nrib-stitching cord was common (p. 9-56).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide212_1.jpeg\" alt=\"lacing wire bundles\"><figcaption>Single-cord vs. double-cord lacing of a wire bundle.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Cord rules and knots</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Single-cord</b> lacing is used for bundles <b>1 inch in diameter or less</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Double-cord</b> lacing is used on bundles <b>1 inch or larger</b>.</li>\n<li>A <b>clove hitch</b> is used as the <b>start knot</b> for lacing (p. 9-56).</li>\n<li>Use wire group or bundle <b>ties where the supports are more than 12 inches\napart</b> (p. 9-57).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide213_1.jpeg\" alt=\"clove hitch\"><figcaption>The clove hitch is the starting knot for lacing a bundle.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Lacing</b> = one continuous cord with loops; <b>tying</b> = individual ties.</li>\n<li>Preferred today: <b>nylon wire ties</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Single cord &le; 1 inch</b> diameter; <b>double cord &ge; 1 inch</b>.</li>\n<li>Start knot = <b>clove hitch</b>. Add ties where supports are more than <b>12 inches</b> apart.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Lacing is defined as securing a bundle with:", "options": ["Metal clamps only", "A continuous piece of cord forming loops at regular intervals", "Heat-shrink tubing", "Individual ties at intervals"], "correct": 1, "short": "A continuous cord forming loops.", "expl": "Lacing = one continuous cord; tying = individual ties.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Tying is defined as securing a bundle with:", "options": ["A single continuous cord", "Conduit", "Individual ties placed at regular intervals", "Solder"], "correct": 2, "short": "Individual ties at regular intervals.", "expl": "Each tie is separate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Today, the preferred method of securing wire bundles is:", "options": ["Rib-stitching cord", "Nylon wire ties", "Waxed lacing cord", "Bare copper wire"], "correct": 1, "short": "Nylon wire ties.", "expl": "Nylon ties are the modern preferred method.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Single-cord lacing is used for wire bundles with a diameter of:", "options": ["Any diameter", "2 inches or larger", "1 inch or less", "1 inch or larger"], "correct": 2, "short": "1 inch or less.", "expl": "Single cord ≤ 1 in; double cord ≥ 1 in.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Double-cord lacing is used on wire bundles that are:", "options": ["Only in conduit", "1 inch or larger", "Always, regardless of size", "Less than 1/2 inch"], "correct": 1, "short": "1 inch or larger.", "expl": "Larger bundles need double-cord lacing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The knot used as the start knot when lacing a bundle is the:", "options": ["Clove hitch", "Bowline", "Square knot", "Half hitch only"], "correct": 0, "short": "Clove hitch.", "expl": "The clove hitch begins the lacing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Wire group or bundle ties should be used where the supports are more than:", "options": ["36 inches apart", "6 inches apart", "12 inches apart", "24 inches apart"], "correct": 2, "short": "12 inches apart.", "expl": "Add ties when supports exceed 12 inches spacing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}]}, {"id": "b6m7s3", "title": "Wire termination & connectors", "lesson": "\n<h3>Stripping & terminals</h3>\n<p>Wire is cut to length and its runs are broken at junctions — <b>connectors,\nterminal blocks, or buses</b>. When stripping, <b>expose as little of the conductor\nas necessary</b> and avoid <b>cutting or nicking the individual strands</b>\n(p. 9-58).</p>\n<p><b>Aluminum wire</b> must be stripped very carefully — aluminum strands\n<b>break easily</b> if nicked or damaged. <b>Aluminum terminal lugs must be used with\naluminum wire</b> (p. 9-58). Because aluminum oxidizes (and the oxide is an\ninsulator), the inside of an aluminum terminal is filled with a compound of\n<b>petroleum jelly and zinc dust</b> to ensure a good connection (p. 9-60).</p>\n\n<h3>Terminal strips & stud stacking (know the numbers)</h3>\n<p>Wires are usually joined at <b>terminal strips</b> — threaded studs on an\ninsulating strip, secured to prevent rotation. Rules (p. 9-59):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When more than <b>four</b> terminals must be connected together, mount a <b>small\nmetal bus</b> across two or more adjacent studs.</li>\n<li>Ideally there should be only <b>three wires on any one stud</b>; the <b>FAA allows\nup to four terminals</b> stacked on a single stud.</li>\n<li>Multiple terminals must be <b>angled out and stacked, alternately up and down</b>,\nto avoid damaging each terminal's tongue.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide223_1.jpeg\" alt=\"terminal stacking\"><figcaption>Proper stacking: terminal tongues alternately up and down; wires angled from the stud.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Terminal lug color code</h3>\n<p><b>Pre-insulated, crimp-type ring-tongue terminals are preferred</b>. The\ninsulation color tells the wire size (p. 9-59):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Red</b> = 22&ndash;18 gauge</li>\n<li><b>Blue</b> = 16&ndash;14 gauge</li>\n<li><b>Yellow</b> = 12&ndash;10 gauge</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Wires <b>larger than 10-gauge</b> typically use <b>uninsulated terminals</b>,\nwhich are then insulated with vinyl or heat-shrinkable tubing (p. 9-60).</p>\n\n<h3>Nicked/broken strand limits</h3>\n<p>The FAA specifies limits for the number of nicked or broken strands. Example: a\n<b>20-gauge copper wire with 19 strands may have two nicks and no broken strands</b>\n(p. 9-60). A properly crimped terminal (using the correct special crimping tool)\nprovides a joint <b>as strong as the tensile strength of the wire itself</b>.</p>\n\n<h3>Emergency splicing</h3>\n<p>Broken wires can be repaired by <b>crimped splices</b>, by using terminal lugs\nwith the tongues cut off, or by <b>soldering and potting</b> broken strands.\n<b>Damaged aluminum wire must NOT be temporarily spliced</b> (p. 9-60).</p>\n\n<h3>Junction boxes</h3>\n<p>Junction boxes in powerplant compartments or other high-heat/fire areas are made\nof <b>stainless steel or heat-resistant aluminum alloy</b>. They are mounted to\nminimize water entry, should be <b>isolated from electrical power whenever the cover\nis removed</b>, and their <b>rigid construction prevents oil-canning</b> of the sides\n(which could cause internal shorts). <b>Remove hand jewelry</b> before working inside\nto avoid shocks/burns (p. 9-61).</p>\n\n<h3>AN/MS connectors</h3>\n<p><b>AN (Army-Navy)</b> and <b>MS (Military Specification)</b> connectors (plugs and\nreceptacles) make maintenance easier when frequent disconnection is required; the\ntypes that use <b>crimped contacts</b> are generally used on aircraft (p. 9-62). Key\nrequirements (p. 9-63):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identified by an <b>MS or OEM specification number</b>.</li>\n<li>Rated for the <b>voltage and current</b> under the maximum ambient temperature\nand circuit load.</li>\n<li><b>Spare contacts</b> are provided for future wiring.</li>\n<li>Redundant-system wires route through <b>separate connectors</b>; indication\nwiring is kept separate from failure-warning wiring.</li>\n<li><b>Adjacent connectors must not be matable</b> — they differ in shell size,\ncoupling, insert arrangement, or keying.</li>\n<li><b>Sealing</b> excludes moisture; connectors are installed so fluids <b>drain\nout, not in</b>; a rear-accessory <b>back shell</b> supports the wire.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide235_1.jpeg\" alt=\"AN/MS connector\"><figcaption>An AN/MS connector (plug and receptacle) with crimped contacts.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Coaxial cable & BNC</h3>\n<p>Circuits connecting equipment to an antenna are especially prone to\n<b>radio-frequency interference (RFI)</b>. <b>Coaxial cable</b> — the most common RF\ncable — both <b>holds in RFI and protects the circuit from outside RFI</b>. Even so,\ncoax runs should be <b>as direct and short as possible</b>. The <b>BNC connector is\nthe most widely used</b> coax connector, and proper installation is critical\n(p. 9-64).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide237_1.jpeg\" alt=\"coaxial cable\"><figcaption>Coaxial cable: center conductor, insulator, braided shield, outer jacket.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Aluminum wire strands nick/break easily; use <b>aluminum terminal lugs</b> (filled with petroleum-jelly/zinc-dust compound).</li>\n<li>Ideal <b>3 wires per stud</b>; FAA max <b>4</b>; stack tongues <b>alternately up/down</b>; &gt;4 tied together &rarr; add a small bus.</li>\n<li>Lug colors: <b>Red 22-18</b>, <b>Blue 16-14</b>, <b>Yellow 12-10</b>; &gt;10-gauge uses uninsulated terminals + tubing.</li>\n<li>Example strand limit: 20-ga 19-strand copper = <b>2 nicks, 0 broken</b>. <b>Damaged aluminum wire is NOT temporarily spliced</b>.</li>\n<li>Hot-area junction boxes = <b>stainless steel / heat-resistant aluminum</b>; isolate from power when open; remove jewelry.</li>\n<li><b>AN/MS</b> connectors: adjacent ones <b>not matable</b>; crimped contacts common; sealed and drained.</li>\n<li><b>Coax</b> = most common RF cable; run short/direct; <b>BNC</b> is the most widely used connector.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "When stripping a wire, you should expose:", "options": ["As much conductor as possible", "Exactly half the wire", "As little of the conductor as necessary to make the connection", "The entire insulation length"], "correct": 2, "short": "As little conductor as necessary.", "expl": "And avoid nicking the strands.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Aluminum wire must be stripped very carefully because its strands:", "options": ["Break very easily if nicked or damaged", "Are non-conductive", "Are too thick to cut", "Cannot be crimped"], "correct": 0, "short": "Break easily if nicked.", "expl": "Aluminum strands are fragile once nicked.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Aluminum wire must be terminated using:", "options": ["Any available lug", "Only solder, no lugs", "Aluminum terminal lugs", "Copper terminal lugs"], "correct": 2, "short": "Aluminum terminal lugs.", "expl": "Match the lug material to the wire to avoid corrosion/poor connection.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The FAA allows a maximum of how many terminals stacked on a single terminal stud?", "options": ["Two", "Six", "Three", "Four"], "correct": 3, "short": "Four.", "expl": "Ideal is 3; FAA maximum is 4 terminals per stud.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "When more than four terminals must be connected together, you should:", "options": ["Stack them all on one stud", "Mount a small metal bus across two or more adjacent studs", "Solder them into a ball", "Use a single large lug"], "correct": 1, "short": "Mount a small metal bus across studs.", "expl": "A bus bar joins many terminals safely.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "When stacking multiple terminals on a stud, the tongues should be placed:", "options": ["All facing down", "All facing up", "Alternately up and down", "At random angles"], "correct": 2, "short": "Alternately up and down.", "expl": "Alternating and angling prevents damaging the tongues.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A red pre-insulated terminal lug is used for wire gauges:", "options": ["12–10 gauge", "22–18 gauge", "8–6 gauge", "16–14 gauge"], "correct": 1, "short": "22–18 gauge.", "expl": "Red = 22-18, Blue = 16-14, Yellow = 12-10.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection", "fig": "img/slide224_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "A yellow pre-insulated terminal lug is used for wire gauges:", "options": ["12–10 gauge", "22–18 gauge", "16–14 gauge", "24–22 gauge"], "correct": 0, "short": "12–10 gauge.", "expl": "Yellow = 12-10 gauge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A blue pre-insulated terminal lug is used for wire gauges:", "options": ["6–4 gauge", "12–10 gauge", "22–18 gauge", "16–14 gauge"], "correct": 3, "short": "16–14 gauge.", "expl": "Blue = 16-14 gauge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Per the FAA example, a 20-gauge copper wire with 19 strands may have:", "options": ["Two nicks and no broken strands", "Any number of nicks", "Five nicks and one broken strand", "No nicks and two broken strands"], "correct": 0, "short": "Two nicks and no broken strands.", "expl": "The FAA sets specific nicked/broken-strand limits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Which statement about emergency wire splicing is correct?", "options": ["Splices may never be crimped", "Broken strands can never be repaired", "Aluminum wire may be temporarily spliced like copper", "Damaged aluminum wire must NOT be temporarily spliced"], "correct": 3, "short": "Damaged aluminum wire must NOT be temporarily spliced.", "expl": "Copper can be crimp/solder-spliced; aluminum cannot be temp-spliced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Junction boxes in engine (high-heat/fire) areas are typically made of:", "options": ["Plastic", "Cardboard composite", "Stainless steel or heat-resistant aluminum alloy", "Copper"], "correct": 2, "short": "Stainless steel or heat-resistant aluminum alloy.", "expl": "They must resist heat and fire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "When working inside an energized junction box, a technician should:", "options": ["Isolate it from power when the cover is removed and remove hand jewelry", "Use metal tools freely across terminals", "Keep rings and watches on", "Leave the cover on"], "correct": 0, "short": "Isolate from power; remove jewelry.", "expl": "Prevents accidental shocks/burns and shorts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Connectors that make maintenance easier when frequent disconnection is required, identified by MS or OEM numbers, are:", "options": ["AN/MS connectors", "Terminal strips", "Bus bars", "Solenoids"], "correct": 0, "short": "AN/MS connectors.", "expl": "AN = Army-Navy, MS = Military Specification; crimped-contact types are common.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection", "fig": "img/slide235_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "Adjacent AN/MS connector pairs must be made non-matable by differing in shell size, coupling, insert arrangement, or:", "options": ["Wire color", "Number of spare contacts", "Length of cable", "Keying arrangement"], "correct": 3, "short": "Keying arrangement.", "expl": "So you can't cross-connect adjacent connectors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The most common type of RF cable used to connect equipment to an antenna is:", "options": ["Solid single-conductor wire", "Twisted pair", "Ribbon cable", "Coaxial cable"], "correct": 3, "short": "Coaxial cable.", "expl": "Coax holds in and blocks RFI; run it short and direct.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection", "fig": "img/slide237_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "The most widely used connector for coaxial cable is the:", "options": ["BNC connector", "AN connector", "D-sub connector", "Spade lug"], "correct": 0, "short": "BNC connector.", "expl": "Proper BNC installation is critical to RF performance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}]}, {"id": "b6m7s4", "title": "Switches & circuit protection", "lesson": "\n<h3>Switches</h3>\n<p>A <b>switch</b> opens and closes a circuit through one or more pairs of\n<b>contacts</b>; current flows when the contacts are <b>closed</b> (p. 9-65).</p>\n<p>Switches must be <b>derated</b> from their nominal current rating for certain\ncircuits (p. 9-65):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>High rush-in (inrush) circuits</b> — incandescent lamps can draw an initial\ncurrent up to <b>15 times</b> the continuous current.</li>\n<li><b>Inductive circuits</b> — magnetic energy stored in solenoid coils/relays is\nreleased as an <b>arc</b> when the switch opens.</li>\n<li><b>Motors</b> — DC motors draw <b>several times</b> their rated current during\nstarting.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Common switch types: <b>SPST</b> (single-pole single-throw), <b>DPST</b>,\n<b>SPDT</b>, <b>DPDT</b>; two- and three-position; spring-loaded\n<b>normally open (NO)</b> and <b>normally closed (NC)</b>; <b>toggle/rocker</b>\n(control most electrical components); <b>rotary</b> (twist a knob, common on radio\npanels); and <b>precision (micro) switches</b> (spring-loaded, return to normal when\npressure is removed; typically SPDT or DPDT) (p. 9-65).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide245_2.jpeg\" alt=\"switch types\"><figcaption>Switch pole/throw types (SPST, SPDT, DPST, DPDT).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Relays and solenoids</h3>\n<p><b>Relays</b> control a <b>large current using a small current</b>. They switch\nmotors and equipment on/off and protect them from overheating. Two types\n(p. 9-66):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Electromechanical</b> — a fixed core and a moving plate with contacts.</li>\n<li><b>Solid-state</b> — works like transistors, with <b>no moving parts</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Solenoids</b> are switching devices used where weight reduction or simplified\ncontrols are needed; they have a <b>movable core/armature</b> (steel or iron) with\nthe coil wrapped around it (p. 9-66).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide246_1.jpeg\" alt=\"relay\"><figcaption>A relay/solenoid switches large current with a small control current.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Circuit protection</h3>\n<p>Circuit breakers or fuses should be located <b>as close as possible to the\nelectrical power source bus</b>, and must <b>open the circuit before the wiring or\nequipment overheats to the point of emitting smoke</b> (p. 9-66).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Fuse</b> — placed <b>in series with the voltage source</b> so all current\nflows through it. It is a strip of metal with a <b>low melting point</b> in a\nglass/plastic housing. Two basic types: <b>fast-acting and slow-blow</b> (p. 9-66).</li>\n<li><b>Circuit breaker</b> — an <b>automatically operated switch</b> that protects\nagainst overload or short circuit and <b>can be reset</b>. Types: <b>push-to-reset,\npush-pull, and toggle</b> (p. 9-67).</li>\n<li><b>Current limiter</b> — opens the circuit before its conductors get hot enough\nto emit smoke; used mainly to protect heavy-current circuits (p. 9-66).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide251_2.png\" alt=\"circuit breaker\"><figcaption>A circuit breaker is a resettable, automatically operated protective switch.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Derate switches for <b>inrush</b> (lamps up to <b>15&times;</b>), <b>inductive</b> (arc), and <b>motor</b> circuits.</li>\n<li>Types: SPST / DPST / SPDT / DPDT; NO/NC; toggle/rocker; rotary; precision (micro).</li>\n<li><b>Relay</b> = large current controlled by small current; electromechanical or solid-state. <b>Solenoid</b> = movable core/armature.</li>\n<li>Protection located <b>near the source bus</b>; opens <b>before smoke</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Fuse</b> = in series, low-melting strip, fast-acting or slow-blow (not resettable). <b>Circuit breaker</b> = resettable auto switch (push-to-reset/push-pull/toggle).</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Current flows through a switch when its contacts are:", "options": ["Closed", "Open", "Removed", "Corroded"], "correct": 0, "short": "Closed.", "expl": "Closed contacts complete the circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Switches in incandescent-lamp (high inrush) circuits must be derated because the lamp can draw an initial current of about:", "options": ["2 times continuous", "5 times continuous", "15 times continuous", "100 times continuous"], "correct": 2, "short": "15 times the continuous current.", "expl": "Cold lamp filaments have very high inrush current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A DC motor circuit requires a derated switch because a DC motor:", "options": ["Draws no current", "Runs only on AC", "Cannot store magnetic energy", "Draws several times its rated current during starting"], "correct": 3, "short": "Draws several times rated current at start.", "expl": "Plus stored magnetic energy arcs when the switch opens.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "SPDT stands for:", "options": ["Series-pole double-throw", "Single-pole double-throw", "Single-pole double-terminal", "Standard-pole double-throw"], "correct": 1, "short": "Single-pole double-throw.", "expl": "SP = one pole; DT = two throw positions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A switch that is spring-loaded and returns to its normal position when pressure is removed, typically SPDT or DPDT, is a:", "options": ["Precision (micro) switch", "Rotary switch", "Rocker switch", "Toggle switch"], "correct": 0, "short": "Precision (micro) switch.", "expl": "Spring-loaded, returns to normal; commonly SPDT/DPDT.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A relay is used to:", "options": ["Convert AC to DC", "Increase voltage", "Store electric charge", "Control a large current using a small current"], "correct": 3, "short": "Control large current with a small current.", "expl": "It also protects equipment and switches motors on/off.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The two main types of relays are:", "options": ["Fast and slow", "Fuse and breaker", "Toggle and rotary", "Electromechanical and solid-state"], "correct": 3, "short": "Electromechanical and solid-state.", "expl": "Electromechanical has moving contacts; solid-state has none.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A solid-state relay differs from an electromechanical relay in that it:", "options": ["Has a fixed core and moving plate", "Requires more current to operate", "Can only switch AC", "Works like a transistor and has no moving parts"], "correct": 3, "short": "Works like a transistor; no moving parts.", "expl": "No mechanical contacts to wear or arc.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Circuit breakers and fuses should be located:", "options": ["Inside the battery", "As far as possible from the power source", "Only at the load", "As close as possible to the electrical power source bus"], "correct": 3, "short": "As close as possible to the source bus.", "expl": "So the whole downstream wire is protected.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A circuit protection device should open the circuit:", "options": ["Only if the pilot commands it", "After the wiring emits smoke", "Never — it just warns", "Before the wiring or equipment overheats to the point of emitting smoke"], "correct": 3, "short": "Before it overheats to the point of emitting smoke.", "expl": "Protection must act before damage/fire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A fuse is placed ____ with the voltage source, so all current flows through it.", "options": ["in series", "at a right angle", "across the load only", "in parallel"], "correct": 0, "short": "In series.", "expl": "All circuit current must pass through the fuse.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The two basic types of fuses are:", "options": ["Toggle and rotary", "Push and pull", "Fast-acting and slow-blow", "Copper and aluminum"], "correct": 2, "short": "Fast-acting and slow-blow.", "expl": "Fast-acting opens quickly; slow-blow tolerates brief surges.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A circuit breaker differs from a fuse mainly because a circuit breaker:", "options": ["Can be reset after it trips", "Only works on AC", "Cannot open a circuit", "Melts a metal strip permanently"], "correct": 0, "short": "Can be reset.", "expl": "A breaker is a resettable automatic switch; a fuse must be replaced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Common circuit breaker types include push-to-reset, toggle, and:", "options": ["Slow-blow", "Bayonet", "Rotary-melt", "Push-pull"], "correct": 3, "short": "Push-pull.", "expl": "Push-to-reset, push-pull, and toggle are the CB types.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}]}]}, {"n": 8, "day": "Unit 8", "title": "Aircraft Lighting Systems", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 · position/anti-collision/landing/taxi/wing-inspection · interior · strobe safety", "sections": [{"id": "b6m8s1", "title": "Exterior lighting", "lesson": "\n<p>Aircraft use a variety of <b>exterior lighting systems</b>: <b>position,\nanti-collision, landing, taxi, and wing-inspection</b> lights. Each powers a lamp\nideal for its job. Lamp technologies include <b>incandescent, halogen, and\nxenon</b> (p. 9-68).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide256_1.jpeg\" alt=\"exterior lights\"><figcaption>Common exterior lights: position, anti-collision, landing, taxi.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Position (navigation) lights</h3>\n<p><b>Position lights</b> indicate the <b>location and movement</b> of an aircraft\nduring <b>night operations</b>. By noting the different colors, another operator can\ntell whether the aircraft is stationary or moving and navigate safely around it\n(p. 9-69). The lamp itself has <b>clear glass</b>; the colors come from placing a\n<b>red or green lens</b> over the bulb. The standard layout: <b>red on the left\nwingtip, green on the right wingtip, white on the tail</b>. Most operate steadily,\nbut some older/larger aircraft have <b>flasher circuits</b> that pulse them.</p>\n\n<h3>Anti-collision lights</h3>\n<p><b>Anti-collision lights</b> come in two basic styles depending on the aircraft's\nage (p. 9-70):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Older</b> — a <b>rotating beacon</b> on top of the vertical stabilizer or the\nbottom of the fuselage. It contains a <b>stationary light bulb and a rotating\nreflector</b> covered by a <b>red glass lens</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Newer</b> — <b>solid-state</b> electronics create a flashing or\n<b>strobe-type</b> anti-collision light (often a bright white <b>xenon</b> tube).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide260_1.jpeg\" alt=\"anticollision light\"><figcaption>Rotating-beacon (older) and strobe (newer) anti-collision lights.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Landing and taxi lights</h3>\n<p><b>Sealed-beam lamps</b> mounted facing forward provide night visibility. They can\nbe in the <b>leading edge of the wing, the cowling, or on landing-gear struts</b>;\nsome aircraft use a <b>retractable</b> light that extends from the wing (p. 9-71).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Landing lights</b> point <b>forward and slightly down</b> (because the\nairplane is nose-high during landing).</li>\n<li><b>Taxi lights</b> point <b>mostly straight forward</b> (level attitude on the\nground).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both are <b>high-powered circuits</b>, so they are usually switched by a\n<b>solenoid or relay</b>. If a landing or taxi light is inoperative, the\n<b>relay/solenoid may be the faulty component</b> — check it when troubleshooting\n(p. 9-71).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide264_1.jpeg\" alt=\"strobe xenon\"><figcaption>A xenon strobe-type light.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Wing-inspection lights</h3>\n<p><b>Wing-inspection lights</b> are typically <b>flush-mounted on the fuselage or\nengine nacelle</b> and directed toward the <b>wing leading edge</b>. If the pilot\nsuspects <b>ice</b>, the wing can be illuminated for a visual inspection from inside\nthe aircraft (p. 9-72).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Lamp types: <b>incandescent, halogen, xenon</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Position lights</b>: night location/movement; clear bulb + <b>red/green lens</b>; <b>red left, green right, white tail</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Anti-collision</b>: older = <b>rotating beacon</b> (stationary bulb + rotating reflector, red lens); newer = <b>solid-state strobe</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Landing</b> lights point forward & slightly down; <b>taxi</b> lights point straight forward; both <b>sealed-beam</b>, switched by <b>relay/solenoid</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Wing-inspection</b> lights aim at the wing leading edge to check for <b>ice</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Lamp technologies used in aircraft lighting include incandescent, halogen, and:", "options": ["Neon", "Xenon", "Fluorescent tube only", "Sodium vapor"], "correct": 1, "short": "Xenon.", "expl": "Incandescent, halogen, and xenon are the common types.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Position (navigation) lights are used mainly to:", "options": ["Illuminate the runway", "Inspect the wing for ice", "Indicate the location and movement of the aircraft during night operations", "Light the instrument panel"], "correct": 2, "short": "Indicate location/movement at night.", "expl": "Their colors let others judge direction and motion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "The colors of position lights are produced by:", "options": ["Colored bulbs", "Adjusting the voltage", "Painting the reflector", "Placing a red or green lens over a clear bulb"], "correct": 3, "short": "A red or green lens over a clear bulb.", "expl": "The lamp glass is clear; the lens gives the color.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "The standard position-light color arrangement is:", "options": ["White left, red right, green tail", "Red left, green right, white tail", "Green left, red right, white tail", "Red left, white right, green tail"], "correct": 1, "short": "Red left, green right, white tail.", "expl": "Red on the left wingtip, green on the right, white at the tail.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Older-style anti-collision lights use a rotating beacon that contains a:", "options": ["Stationary bulb and a rotating reflector covered by a red glass lens", "Rotating bulb and stationary reflector", "Xenon strobe tube", "Sealed-beam lamp"], "correct": 0, "short": "Stationary bulb + rotating reflector (red lens).", "expl": "The reflector spins around a fixed bulb.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Newer anti-collision lights typically use:", "options": ["An incandescent bulb only", "Solid-state electronics to create a flashing/strobe light", "A wing-inspection lamp", "A rotating beacon"], "correct": 1, "short": "Solid-state strobe.", "expl": "Modern anti-collision lights are solid-state strobes (often xenon).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Landing and taxi lights typically use which type of lamp?", "options": ["LED filament only", "Sealed-beam", "Fluorescent", "Neon"], "correct": 1, "short": "Sealed-beam.", "expl": "Sealed-beam lamps face forward for landing/taxi.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Landing lights are aimed:", "options": ["Toward the tail", "Straight down only", "Forward and slightly down (for the nose-high landing attitude)", "Straight up"], "correct": 2, "short": "Forward and slightly down.", "expl": "Because the aircraft is nose-high during landing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Compared with landing lights, taxi lights point:", "options": ["Mostly straight forward (level attitude)", "Down at the nose gear only", "Toward the wing", "Forward and slightly down"], "correct": 0, "short": "Mostly straight forward.", "expl": "Taxi lights suit the level ground attitude.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Because landing and taxi lights are high-powered, they are usually switched by a:", "options": ["Diode", "Rotary switch", "Simple toggle switch alone", "Solenoid or relay"], "correct": 3, "short": "Solenoid or relay.", "expl": "High current is handled by a relay/solenoid; a faulty one can cause an inop light.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "If a landing or taxi light is inoperative, a component to suspect (besides the bulb) is the:", "options": ["Wing-inspection light", "Relay or solenoid", "Pitot tube", "Position light lens"], "correct": 1, "short": "Relay or solenoid.", "expl": "The switching relay/solenoid may be at fault.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Wing-inspection lights are directed toward the wing leading edge so the pilot can check for:", "options": ["Bird strikes only", "Tire wear", "Fuel leaks", "Ice formation"], "correct": 3, "short": "Ice formation.", "expl": "They illuminate the wing for an in-flight ice check.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}]}, {"id": "b6m8s2", "title": "Interior lighting, maintenance & strobe safety", "lesson": "\n<h3>Interior lights</h3>\n<p>Modern aircraft carry a variety of interior lights, including <b>instrument\nlights, overhead lights, step lights, reading lights</b>, and many others (p. 9-72).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide265_1.jpeg\" alt=\"interior lights\"><figcaption>Interior lighting: instrument, overhead, reading, and step lights.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Strobe light safety (critical)</h3>\n<p>Strobe (and other high-voltage) lighting systems use <b>capacitors that retain a\ncharge</b> even after power is off. Always <b>allow the system to sit for 5 minutes\nbefore starting maintenance</b>, so the capacitors can discharge. These systems\noperate at such <b>high voltage that a shock could be fatal</b> (p. 9-73).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide266_1.png\" alt=\"strobe safety\"><figcaption>Wait 5 minutes for strobe capacitors to discharge before servicing.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Maintenance & inspection</h3>\n<p>Inspection of an aircraft's lighting system normally includes checking the\n<b>condition and security of all visible wiring, connections, terminals, fuses, and\nswitches</b>. A <b>continuity light or meter</b> can be used to check circuits\n(p. 9-73).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Interior lights: instrument, overhead, step, reading lights.</li>\n<li><b>Strobe safety</b>: wait <b>5 minutes</b> for capacitors to discharge; the high voltage can be <b>fatal</b>.</li>\n<li>Lighting inspection: condition/security of <b>wiring, connections, terminals, fuses, switches</b>; use a <b>continuity light/meter</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Examples of interior aircraft lighting include instrument, overhead, reading, and:", "options": ["Anti-collision beacons", "Taxi lights", "Step lights", "Landing lights"], "correct": 2, "short": "Step lights.", "expl": "Interior lighting = instrument, overhead, step, reading, etc.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Before servicing a strobe light system, a technician should allow it to sit for at least:", "options": ["5 minutes", "30 seconds", "1 minute", "1 hour"], "correct": 0, "short": "5 minutes.", "expl": "Time for the capacitors to discharge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "The reason for waiting before servicing a strobe system is that its:", "options": ["Bulbs stay hot for hours", "Lens must cool", "Capacitors retain a charge that can be fatal", "Software must reboot"], "correct": 2, "short": "Capacitors retain a lethal charge.", "expl": "Strobe systems run at very high, potentially fatal voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "A strobe/high-voltage lighting system is dangerous because it operates at a voltage that could be:", "options": ["Too low to matter", "Only mildly uncomfortable", "Fatal", "Zero after shutdown"], "correct": 2, "short": "Fatal.", "expl": "High voltage stored in the capacitors can kill.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "A lighting-system inspection normally includes checking the condition and security of visible wiring, connections, terminals, fuses, and:", "options": ["Tires", "Fuel lines", "Hydraulic actuators", "Switches"], "correct": 3, "short": "Switches.", "expl": "Wiring, connections, terminals, fuses, and switches.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "A convenient tool for checking lighting circuits during inspection is a:", "options": ["Hydrometer", "Micrometer", "Torque wrench", "Continuity light or meter"], "correct": 3, "short": "Continuity light or meter.", "expl": "Used to verify circuit continuity.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}]}]}, {"n": 9, "day": "Unit 9", "title": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital Electronics", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.11 · analog vs digital · tubes/solid-state · rectifiers/amplifiers/oscillators · logic gates", "sections": [{"id": "b6m9s1", "title": "Avionics & analog electronics (tubes/solid-state)", "lesson": "\n<p><b>Avionics</b> is a conjunction of the words <b>aviation</b> and\n<b>electronics</b>. An A&amp;P technician needs to be familiar with the <b>general\nworkings</b> of the various avionics, even though <b>maintenance of the actual\navionics devices is often reserved for the avionics manufacturers or certified repair\nstations</b> (p. 11-1). The history of avionics tracks the use of electronics in\naviation; World War I created an urgent need for ground-to-air and aircraft-to-aircraft\n<b>voice communication</b>.</p>\n\n<h3>Analog vs. digital electronics</h3>\n<p>Electronic devices represent and manipulate real-world phenomena using electrical\nsignals (p. 11-2):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Analog</b> representations are <b>continuous</b>. An analog signal can be\nmodified by changing its <b>amplitude, frequency, or phase</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Digital</b> representations are <b>discontinuous</b> — successive chains of\n<b>voltage and non-voltage</b> signals (1s and 0s) that can be transported and\nmanipulated in circuits.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A significant advantage of digital over analog is the <b>control of noise</b>\n(p. 11-2).</p>\n\n<h3>Electron control valves (vacuum tubes)</h3>\n<p>Early aircraft used <b>vacuum tubes</b> that functioned as <b>electron control\nvalves</b> — they control electron flow so a circuit can produce the desired\noutcome. Tubes were later replaced by <b>solid-state devices</b>. Only antique\naircraft still use tubes, because of their size and inability to withstand the\n<b>vibration and shock</b> of the aircraft environment (p. 11-2). The tube family:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Diode</b> — acts as a <b>check valve</b> in an AC circuit; allows current to\nflow during <b>half of the AC cycle</b> but not the other half.</li>\n<li><b>Triode</b> — <b>three elements</b> (cathode, plate, grid); used to control a\nlarge current with a smaller current.</li>\n<li><b>Tetrode</b> — <b>four elements</b> (cathode, plate, grid, screen grid).</li>\n<li><b>Pentode</b> — <b>five elements</b>; especially useful in high-power circuits.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Solid-state devices</h3>\n<p>Solid-state devices began replacing vacuum-tube electron control valves in the\n<b>late 1950s</b>. Their <b>long life, reliability, and resilience in harsh\nenvironments</b> make them ideal for aviation (p. 11-3). They include semiconductors\nand semiconductor diodes, <b>Zener diodes</b>, transistors, <b>SCRs (silicon\ncontrolled rectifiers)</b>, triacs, <b>unijunction transistors (UJT)</b>,\n<b>field-effect transistors (FET)</b>, photodiodes/phototransistors, and\n<b>LEDs</b>.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Avionics</b> = aviation + electronics; avionics repair is usually done by <b>certified repair stations</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Analog = continuous</b> (amplitude/frequency/phase); <b>digital = discontinuous</b> (1s/0s). Digital's big advantage = <b>noise control</b>.</li>\n<li>Tube = <b>electron control valve</b>. <b>Diode</b> = check valve (conducts half the AC cycle). Triode <b>3</b>, tetrode <b>4</b>, pentode <b>5</b> elements.</li>\n<li>Solid-state replaced tubes in the <b>late 1950s</b>: long life, reliable, tolerates vibration/shock.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The word 'avionics' is a conjunction of:", "options": ["Aviation and electronics", "Aviation and mechanics", "Air and ionics", "Avionic and ceramics"], "correct": 0, "short": "Aviation + electronics.", "expl": "Avionics = the electronics used in aviation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Maintenance of the actual avionics devices is usually reserved for:", "options": ["The avionics manufacturers or certified repair stations", "The flight crew", "Any A&P mechanic", "The airport manager"], "correct": 0, "short": "Avionics manufacturers / certified repair stations.", "expl": "The A&P must know the general workings, but detailed repair is specialized.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Analog electronic representations are:", "options": ["Always digital", "Continuous", "Discontinuous (1s and 0s)", "Noise-free"], "correct": 1, "short": "Continuous.", "expl": "Analog signals vary continuously; digital is discontinuous.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An analog signal can be modified by changing its amplitude, frequency, or:", "options": ["Phase", "Binary code", "Truth table", "Voltage level only"], "correct": 0, "short": "Phase.", "expl": "Amplitude, frequency, or phase can carry analog information.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A significant advantage of digital electronics over analog is:", "options": ["The use of vacuum tubes", "The control of noise", "Larger physical size", "Lower cost always"], "correct": 1, "short": "Control of noise.", "expl": "Digital signals resist noise far better than analog.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A vacuum tube in an early avionics circuit functioned as a(n):", "options": ["Electron control valve", "Antenna", "Battery", "Transformer"], "correct": 0, "short": "Electron control valve.", "expl": "Tubes controlled electron flow; solid-state devices replaced them.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Why are vacuum tubes found only in antique aircraft today?", "options": ["They are too cheap", "Their size and inability to withstand vibration and shock", "They are digital", "They produce too little heat"], "correct": 1, "short": "Size and can't withstand vibration/shock.", "expl": "The harsh aircraft environment ruined tubes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "In an AC circuit, a diode acts as a:", "options": ["Amplifier", "Storage cell", "Oscillator", "Check valve, allowing current during half the AC cycle"], "correct": 3, "short": "Check valve (conducts half the AC cycle).", "expl": "A diode passes current one way, blocking the other half-cycle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A triode vacuum tube contains how many elements?", "options": ["Four", "Three (cathode, plate, grid)", "Two", "Five"], "correct": 1, "short": "Three (cathode, plate, grid).", "expl": "Triode = 3 elements; used to control large current with a smaller one.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A pentode vacuum tube has five elements and is especially useful in:", "options": ["High-power circuits", "DC-only circuits", "Antenna tuning", "Low-power circuits"], "correct": 0, "short": "High-power circuits.", "expl": "Pentode = 5 elements, good for high power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Solid-state devices began replacing vacuum-tube control valves in the:", "options": ["Late 1980s", "Early 1920s", "Late 1950s", "Early 2000s"], "correct": 2, "short": "Late 1950s.", "expl": "Solid-state took over from tubes in the late 1950s.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Solid-state devices are ideal for aviation because of their:", "options": ["Long life, reliability, and resilience in harsh environments", "Large size", "Need for frequent replacement", "Sensitivity to vibration"], "correct": 0, "short": "Long life, reliability, harsh-environment resilience.", "expl": "Exactly why they replaced tubes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}]}, {"id": "b6m9s2", "title": "Basic analog circuits (rectifier/amplifier/oscillator)", "lesson": "\n<p>Solid-state semiconductor devices appear in both analog and digital circuits. As\ndigital electronics evolve, analog circuitry is increasingly replaced (p. 11-4).\nThree classic analog building blocks show up on the test: rectifiers, amplifiers, and\noscillators.</p>\n\n<h3>Rectifiers</h3>\n<p>A <b>rectifier</b> circuit <b>changes AC voltage into DC voltage</b> — one of the\n<b>most commonly used</b> circuits in aircraft electronics (p. 11-4). (Remember: a\nrectifier is AC&rarr;DC; an inverter is DC&rarr;AC.)</p>\n\n<h3>Amplifiers</h3>\n<p>An <b>amplifier</b> is a circuit that <b>changes the amplitude of an electric\nsignal</b>, done through the use of <b>transistors</b>. There are three basic\ntransistor amplifier configurations (p. 11-4):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Common-emitter (CE)</b> — controls the amplitude of the signal AND\n<b>inverts the phase</b> of the input signal.</li>\n<li><b>Common-collector (CC)</b> — high <b>current gain</b>, but virtually\n<b>no voltage gain</b> (used as a buffer/impedance match).</li>\n<li><b>Common-base (CB)</b> — uses a bipolar transistor (high voltage gain, low\ncurrent gain).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Oscillators</h3>\n<p>An <b>oscillator</b> does the opposite of a rectifier: it <b>makes AC from DC</b>,\nand can produce various waveforms as needed by electronic circuits. Electronic\noscillation is accomplished by combining a <b>transistor and a tank circuit</b>. A\n<b>tank circuit</b> is a <b>capacitor and coil (inductor) connected in parallel</b>\nwith each other (p. 11-5). Oscillators are what create the carrier-wave frequency in\na transmitter.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rectifier</b>: AC &rarr; DC (very common). <b>Oscillator</b>: DC &rarr; AC.</li>\n<li><b>Amplifier</b> changes signal <b>amplitude</b> using transistors.</li>\n<li><b>Common-emitter</b> inverts the phase; <b>common-collector</b> = high current gain / no voltage gain; <b>common-base</b> uses a bipolar transistor.</li>\n<li>An oscillator = <b>transistor + tank circuit</b>; a <b>tank circuit</b> = <b>capacitor and coil in parallel</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A rectifier circuit changes:", "options": ["AC voltage into DC voltage", "Analog into digital", "Low voltage into high voltage only", "DC voltage into AC voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "AC into DC.", "expl": "Rectifier = AC→DC; one of the most common aircraft electronic circuits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "The rectifier is described as one of the ____ types of circuits in aircraft electronics.", "options": ["most commonly used", "heaviest", "oldest and unused", "least common"], "correct": 0, "short": "Most commonly used.", "expl": "Rectifiers are everywhere DC is needed from AC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An amplifier is a circuit that changes the ____ of an electric signal, using transistors.", "options": ["amplitude", "phase only", "polarity", "frequency"], "correct": 0, "short": "Amplitude.", "expl": "Amplifiers boost/scale signal amplitude via transistors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "The three basic transistor amplifier configurations are:", "options": ["Series, parallel, compound", "AM, FM, SSB", "Common-emitter, common-collector, common-base", "AND, OR, NOT"], "correct": 2, "short": "Common-emitter, common-collector, common-base.", "expl": "The three amplifier configurations.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Which amplifier configuration inverts the phase of the input signal?", "options": ["Common-collector", "Common-emitter", "Common-base", "None of them"], "correct": 1, "short": "Common-emitter.", "expl": "CE controls amplitude and inverts the input phase.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "The common-collector amplifier is characterized by:", "options": ["High current gain, but virtually no voltage gain", "Phase inversion", "No gain of any kind", "High voltage gain, no current gain"], "correct": 0, "short": "High current gain, no voltage gain.", "expl": "CC (emitter-follower) is a buffer/impedance match.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An oscillator circuit functions to:", "options": ["Make AC from DC and produce various waveforms", "Change AC into DC", "Amplify current only", "Store charge"], "correct": 0, "short": "Make AC from DC.", "expl": "Oscillators generate AC waveforms (e.g., the transmitter carrier).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Electronic oscillation is accomplished by combining a transistor with a:", "options": ["Tank circuit", "Rectifier bank", "Slip ring", "Commutator"], "correct": 0, "short": "Tank circuit.", "expl": "Transistor + tank circuit = oscillator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A tank circuit is comprised of a:", "options": ["Transformer and rectifier", "Capacitor and coil (inductor) in parallel", "Two batteries in series", "Resistor and diode in series"], "correct": 1, "short": "Capacitor and coil in parallel.", "expl": "The parallel LC tank sets the oscillation frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}]}, {"id": "b6m9s3", "title": "Digital electronics & logic gates", "lesson": "\n<p>Modern aircraft increasingly use <b>digital electronics</b> in avionics instead of\nanalog. <b>Transistors</b> are used to build circuits that act as <b>digital logic\ngates</b>, and electrical signals are manipulated through those gates. Digital logic\nis based on the <b>binary number system</b>, in which only two conditions exist:\n<b>1 or 0</b> (p. 11-6).</p>\n\n<h3>The logic gates</h3>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide302_1.jpeg\" alt=\"logic gates chart\"><figcaption>The common logic gates and their symbols.</figcaption></figure>\n<ul>\n<li><b>NOT gate (inverter)</b> — the simplest gate: one input, one output; if the\ninput is Logic 1, the output is <b>NOT 1</b> (i.e., 0). It inverts.</li>\n<li><b>Buffer gate</b> — one input, one output; the output is the <b>same as the\ninput</b> (no inversion).</li>\n<li><b>AND gate</b> — output is 1 <b>only when ALL inputs are 1</b>.</li>\n<li><b>OR gate</b> — output is 1 when <b>at least one input is 1</b>.</li>\n<li><b>NAND gate</b> — a combination of <b>AND and NOT</b> (an inverted AND).</li>\n<li><b>NOR gate</b> — an <b>inverted OR</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Exclusive-OR (XOR) gate</b> — same as OR <b>except</b> when <b>both inputs are\n1</b>, where the output is 0 (output is 1 only when the inputs <b>differ</b>).</li>\n<li><b>Negative logic gates</b> — negative-OR and negative-AND gates (their inputs are\ninverted).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Truth tables (memorize these)</h3>\n<table>\n<tr><th>A</th><th>B</th><th>AND</th><th>OR</th><th>NAND</th><th>NOR</th><th>XOR</th></tr>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td></tr>\n<tr><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>1</td></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>1</td><td>0</td><td>0</td><td>0</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>NOT gate: input 0 &rarr; output 1; input 1 &rarr; output 0.</p>\n\n<h3>Digital aircraft systems</h3>\n<p>Handling information in digital form gives a common language for monitoring,\ncontrol, and safety. <b>Self-monitoring, built-in test equipment (BITE)</b> and\n<b>air-to-ground data links</b> increase maintenance efficiency. <b>Digital tuners\nand audio panels</b> consolidate various communication and navigation radio-selection\ncontrols into a single unit (p. 11-7).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Digital logic uses the <b>binary</b> system: only <b>1 or 0</b>.</li>\n<li><b>NOT</b> = inverter (1&rarr;0). <b>Buffer</b> = output equals input.</li>\n<li><b>AND</b> = 1 only if ALL inputs 1. <b>OR</b> = 1 if any input 1.</li>\n<li><b>NAND</b> = inverted AND; <b>NOR</b> = inverted OR; <b>XOR</b> = 1 only when inputs differ (0 when both 1).</li>\n<li><b>BITE</b> = built-in test equipment (self-monitoring). Audio panel/digital tuner consolidates radio controls.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Digital logic is based on the binary number system, which has:", "options": ["Two conditions (1 or 0)", "Ten conditions (0–9)", "Sixteen conditions", "Three conditions"], "correct": 0, "short": "Two conditions (1 or 0).", "expl": "Binary = 1 or 0.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "The simplest logic gate, with one input and one output that inverts the signal, is the:", "options": ["AND gate", "NOT gate (inverter)", "NAND gate", "OR gate"], "correct": 1, "short": "NOT gate (inverter).", "expl": "Input 1 → output 0; it inverts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A buffer gate produces an output that is:", "options": ["Always 0", "Always 1", "The inverse of the input", "The same as the input"], "correct": 3, "short": "The same as the input.", "expl": "One in, one out, no inversion.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An AND gate produces an output of Logic 1 only when:", "options": ["The inputs differ", "At least one input is 1", "All inputs are 1", "Both inputs are 0"], "correct": 2, "short": "All inputs are 1.", "expl": "AND requires every input high.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An OR gate produces an output of Logic 1 when:", "options": ["The inputs are equal", "Both inputs are 0", "All inputs are 1", "At least one input is 1"], "correct": 3, "short": "At least one input is 1.", "expl": "Any high input makes an OR output high.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A NAND gate is a combination of which two gates?", "options": ["OR and NOT", "AND and OR", "NOR and XOR", "AND and NOT"], "correct": 3, "short": "AND and NOT.", "expl": "NAND = inverted AND.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A NOR gate is best described as an:", "options": ["Exclusive-OR", "Inverted AND gate", "Inverted OR gate", "Buffer"], "correct": 2, "short": "Inverted OR gate.", "expl": "NOR = NOT-OR.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An exclusive-OR (XOR) gate differs from an OR gate in that its output is 0 when:", "options": ["Only one input is 1", "Both inputs are 1", "Any input is 1", "Both inputs are 0"], "correct": 1, "short": "Both inputs are 1.", "expl": "XOR = 1 only when inputs differ; 0 when both are 1 (or both 0).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "For an AND gate with inputs A=1 and B=0, the output is:", "options": ["Both 1 and 0", "0", "1", "Undefined"], "correct": 1, "short": "0.", "expl": "AND needs all inputs 1; with B=0 the output is 0.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "For a NOR gate with inputs A=0 and B=0, the output is:", "options": ["1", "Undefined", "Depends on voltage", "0"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.", "expl": "OR(0,0)=0, inverted by NOR = 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Self-monitoring test capability built into avionics is abbreviated:", "options": ["BITE (built-in test equipment)", "BPCU", "BUS", "BNC"], "correct": 0, "short": "BITE (built-in test equipment).", "expl": "BITE plus air-to-ground data links improve maintenance efficiency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A unit that consolidates various communication and navigation radio-selection controls into one is a(n):", "options": ["Audio panel / digital tuner", "Current transformer", "Rectifier", "Inverter"], "correct": 0, "short": "Audio panel / digital tuner.", "expl": "It consolidates radio selection into a single unit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}]}]}, {"n": 10, "day": "Unit 10", "title": "Radio Communication", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.11 · radio waves/propagation · AM/FM/SSB · transmitters/receivers · antennas", "sections": [{"id": "b6m10s1", "title": "Radio waves & propagation", "lesson": "\n<p>Much of aviation communication and navigation is done with <b>radio waves</b>. A\nradio wave is <b>invisible</b> and <b>electromagnetic</b> in nature — part of the\nelectromagnetic spectrum that also includes gamma rays, X-rays, ultraviolet,\ninfrared, and visible light (p. 11-8).</p>\n\n<h3>Frequencies, speed, and the FCC</h3>\n<ul>\n<li>Aviation uses a wide range of frequencies, from <b>low frequency (LF) at\n100 kHz</b> (100,000 cycles/sec) up to <b>super high frequency (SHF) near 10 GHz</b>\n(10,000,000,000 cycles/sec).</li>\n<li>The <b>Federal Communications Commission (FCC)</b> controls the <b>assignment of\nfrequency usage</b> (p. 11-8).</li>\n<li>Radio waves are <b>directional</b> and propagate into space at\n<b>186,000 miles per second</b> (the speed of light).</li>\n<li>The distance a signal travels depends on the <b>frequency</b> and the\n<b>amplification</b> of the AC sent to the antenna (p. 11-8).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide306_1.jpeg\" alt=\"radio communication\"><figcaption>Radio waves are electromagnetic and travel at 186,000 miles per second.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>How radio waves travel (propagation)</h3>\n<p>Waves of different frequencies behave differently in the atmosphere (p. 11-9):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Ground (surface) waves</b> — follow the curvature of the earth; used by the\nlower frequencies (VLF, LF, MF), which have <b>long wavelengths and correspondingly\nlong antennas</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Sky waves</b> — bounce off the ionosphere and return to earth far away; used by\nHF for long-distance work.</li>\n<li><b>Space waves</b> — travel in a <b>straight line (line-of-sight)</b>. Above HF,\nradio waves are known as space waves, and <b>most aviation communication and\nnavigation aids operate with space waves</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>HF radio waves travel in a straight line and do NOT curve to follow the\nearth's surface.</b> The space-wave bands are <b>VHF (30&ndash;300 MHz)</b>,\n<b>UHF (300 MHz&ndash;3 GHz)</b>, and <b>SHF (3&ndash;30 GHz)</b>. <b>VHF communication\nradios are the primary communication radios used in aviation</b> (p. 11-9).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide308_1.jpeg\" alt=\"types of radio waves\"><figcaption>Ground, sky, and space (line-of-sight) wave propagation.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Radio waves = <b>electromagnetic</b>, travel at <b>186,000 mi/s</b>; the <b>FCC</b> assigns frequencies.</li>\n<li>Propagation types: <b>ground (surface)</b>, <b>sky</b>, <b>space (line-of-sight)</b> waves.</li>\n<li><b>HF travels straight and does not curve</b> with the earth; most aviation aids use <b>space waves</b>.</li>\n<li>Bands: <b>VHF 30&ndash;300 MHz</b>, UHF 300 MHz&ndash;3 GHz, SHF 3&ndash;30 GHz. <b>VHF = primary aviation comm</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A radio wave is best described as:", "options": ["A DC voltage", "A mechanical vibration", "A sound wave", "An electromagnetic wave"], "correct": 3, "short": "An electromagnetic wave.", "expl": "Radio waves are part of the electromagnetic spectrum.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Radio waves propagate through space at approximately:", "options": ["3,000 miles per hour", "760 miles per hour", "186,000 miles per second", "1,000 miles per second"], "correct": 2, "short": "186,000 miles per second.", "expl": "The speed of light.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The agency that controls the assignment of radio frequency usage is the:", "options": ["TSA", "FAA", "NTSB", "FCC (Federal Communications Commission)"], "correct": 3, "short": "FCC.", "expl": "The FCC assigns frequency usage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "HF radio waves are characterized by the fact that they:", "options": ["Travel in a straight line and do not curve to follow the earth's surface", "Curve to follow the earth's surface", "Are the primary aviation comm band", "Cannot travel through the atmosphere"], "correct": 0, "short": "Travel straight; do not curve with the earth.", "expl": "HF is line-of-sight in this respect.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Most aviation communication and navigation aids operate with:", "options": ["Sky waves", "Sound waves", "Ground waves", "Space waves"], "correct": 3, "short": "Space waves.", "expl": "Above HF, waves are space waves; most aviation aids use them.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The VHF band covers approximately:", "options": ["100 kHz–1 MHz", "30–300 MHz", "3–30 GHz", "300 MHz–3 GHz"], "correct": 1, "short": "30–300 MHz.", "expl": "VHF = 30-300 MHz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The primary communication radios used in aviation operate in the:", "options": ["LF band", "MF band", "SHF band", "VHF band"], "correct": 3, "short": "VHF band.", "expl": "VHF comm radios are aviation's primary comm.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Radio waves that follow the curvature of the earth are called:", "options": ["Space waves", "Ground (surface) waves", "Sound waves", "Sky waves"], "correct": 1, "short": "Ground (surface) waves.", "expl": "Low frequencies travel as ground waves along the earth.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Radio waves that bounce off the ionosphere to travel long distances are:", "options": ["Ground waves", "Micro waves", "Sky waves", "Space waves"], "correct": 2, "short": "Sky waves.", "expl": "HF sky waves reflect off the ionosphere.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "VLF, LF, and MF radio waves require correspondingly ____ antennas because they have ____ wavelengths.", "options": ["short; long", "short; short", "long; long", "long; short"], "correct": 2, "short": "Long antennas; long wavelengths.", "expl": "Long wavelength → long antenna.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The distance a radio signal travels depends on its frequency and the:", "options": ["Time of day only", "Amplification of the signal sent to the antenna", "Color of the antenna", "Aircraft weight"], "correct": 1, "short": "Amplification of the signal.", "expl": "Frequency and signal amplification set the range.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}]}, {"id": "b6m10s2", "title": "Modulation, transmitters/receivers & antennas", "lesson": "\n<h3>Loading information onto a radio wave</h3>\n<p>A bare radio wave — the <b>carrier wave</b> — carries no useful information by\nitself. To send voice or data, the carrier is <b>modulated</b> by an information\nsignal (p. 11-10). Three schemes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Amplitude Modulation (AM)</b> — the <b>amplitude</b> of the carrier is varied\nby the information signal (e.g., a mic's DC signal is amplified and superimposed on\nthe AC carrier). AM works over <b>long distance</b> but is <b>susceptible to\natmospheric and static noise</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Frequency Modulation (FM)</b> — the carrier keeps a <b>constant amplitude</b>;\ninstead, the information signal alters the carrier's <b>frequency</b> in proportion to\nsignal strength. FM is widely considered <b>superior to AM</b> and is <b>not\nsusceptible to noise</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Single Side Band (SSB)</b> — mixing a carrier with a modulating signal\nproduces three frequencies: the original carrier, the carrier <b>plus</b> the\nmodulating frequency, and the carrier <b>minus</b> it. The two extra frequencies are\nthe <b>sidebands</b>; each sideband contains the information. SSB transmits just one\nsideband, saving power and bandwidth (p. 11-11).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide311_1.jpeg\" alt=\"amplitude modulation\"><figcaption>AM varies the carrier's amplitude; FM varies its frequency.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Basic communication system components</h3>\n<p>A comm system needs a <b>transmitter, receiver, appropriate antennas, microphone,\nspeakers, and headsets</b> (p. 11-11).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Transmitter</b> — a precise <b>oscillator</b> creates the AC carrier-wave\nfrequency, combined with <b>amplifiers</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Receiver</b> — its <b>antenna</b> captures the desired carrier wave (and many\nother waves present in the atmosphere).</li>\n<li><b>Transceiver</b> — a radio that both transmits and receives, using the\n<b>same frequency for both</b> (p. 11-12).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Antennas</h3>\n<p>Antennas are conductors used to transmit and receive RF waves. They have three\ncharacteristics: <b>length, polarization, and directivity</b> (p. 11-12). An antenna\nthat is <b>half the wavelength</b> of the applied frequency is <b>resonant</b>.</p>\n<p>Three basic antenna types are used in aviation (p. 11-13):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Dipole</b> — length approximately <b>half the wavelength</b>; mostly\n<b>horizontally polarized</b>. The common V-shaped VHF navigation antenna (the\n<b>VOR antenna</b>) is a dipole.</li>\n<li><b>Marconi</b> — a <b>one-fourth-wave</b> antenna that achieves half-wave\nefficiency by using the <b>conductive aircraft skin</b> as the second quarter\nwavelength.</li>\n<li><b>Loop</b> — a conductor fashioned into a loop; used as a <b>receiving</b>\nantenna and is <b>highly direction-sensitive</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide317_2.jpeg\" alt=\"antenna types\"><figcaption>Dipole, Marconi, and loop antennas.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Transmission lines</h3>\n<p>Transmitters and receivers connect to their antennas via <b>coaxial cable\n(coax)</b>. Coax has a center wire conductor, a semirigid insulator, a conductive\nbraided cover running the full length, and a waterproof outer covering. Follow the\nmanufacturer's instructions; secure the cables along their length at intervals of\napproximately <b>2 feet</b> (p. 11-13).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>A bare wave = <b>carrier wave</b>; information is added by <b>modulation</b>.</li>\n<li><b>AM</b>: varies amplitude, long distance, <b>noise-susceptible</b>. <b>FM</b>: constant amplitude, varies frequency, <b>not noise-susceptible</b>. <b>SSB</b>: transmits one <b>sideband</b>.</li>\n<li>Transmitter = <b>oscillator + amplifier</b>; <b>transceiver</b> transmits and receives on the same frequency.</li>\n<li>Antenna characteristics: <b>length, polarization, directivity</b>; a <b>half-wavelength</b> antenna is resonant.</li>\n<li><b>Dipole</b> = half-wave (VOR antenna); <b>Marconi</b> = quarter-wave using aircraft skin; <b>loop</b> = direction-sensitive receiving antenna.</li>\n<li>Antenna transmission line = <b>coax</b>, secured about every <b>2 ft</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A basic, unmodulated radio wave that carries no information is called the:", "options": ["Modulating signal", "Carrier wave", "Side band", "Ground wave"], "correct": 1, "short": "Carrier wave.", "expl": "Information is added by modulating the carrier.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "In amplitude modulation (AM), the information signal varies the carrier wave's:", "options": ["Phase only", "Frequency", "Amplitude", "Wavelength only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Amplitude.", "expl": "AM varies amplitude; FM varies frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "A disadvantage of AM is that it is:", "options": ["Susceptible to atmospheric and static noise", "Unable to travel long distances", "Impossible to demodulate", "Limited to digital data"], "correct": 0, "short": "Susceptible to atmospheric/static noise.", "expl": "AM carries long distance but picks up noise.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "In frequency modulation (FM), the carrier wave:", "options": ["Retains a constant amplitude while the frequency is varied", "Is not modulated at all", "Changes amplitude while frequency stays constant", "Is transmitted as a single sideband"], "correct": 0, "short": "Constant amplitude; frequency varied.", "expl": "FM alters frequency in proportion to signal strength.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Compared with AM, FM is:", "options": ["Superior and not susceptible to noise", "Inferior and noisier", "Unable to carry voice", "Only used for navigation"], "correct": 0, "short": "Superior and not susceptible to noise.", "expl": "FM's constant amplitude rejects amplitude noise.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The two additional frequencies produced on either side of a modulated carrier are called:", "options": ["Side bands", "Carriers", "Ground waves", "Harmonics"], "correct": 0, "short": "Side bands.", "expl": "Each sideband contains the information signal; SSB sends one.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "A transceiver is a radio that:", "options": ["Transmits and receives, using the same frequency for both", "Only receives", "Only transmits", "Converts AC to DC"], "correct": 0, "short": "Transmits and receives (same frequency).", "expl": "Transmit + receive in one unit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Within a transmitter, the circuit that creates the AC carrier-wave frequency is the:", "options": ["Antenna", "Rectifier", "Oscillator", "Speaker"], "correct": 2, "short": "Oscillator.", "expl": "The oscillator generates the carrier; amplifiers boost it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "An antenna that is half the wavelength of the applied frequency is said to be:", "options": ["Shielded", "Resonant", "Grounded", "Polarized"], "correct": 1, "short": "Resonant.", "expl": "A half-wavelength antenna is resonant at that frequency.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The three characteristics of an antenna are:", "options": ["Length, polarization, and directivity", "AM, FM, and SSB", "Voltage, current, and resistance", "Ground, sky, and space"], "correct": 0, "short": "Length, polarization, directivity.", "expl": "These define antenna behavior.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The common V-shaped VHF navigation (VOR) antenna is which type?", "options": ["Parabolic", "Marconi", "Dipole", "Loop"], "correct": 2, "short": "Dipole.", "expl": "The VOR antenna is a horizontally polarized dipole (about half-wavelength).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "A Marconi antenna is a one-fourth-wave antenna that achieves half-wave efficiency by using the:", "options": ["Coax shield only", "Second aircraft engine", "Conductive aircraft skin as the second quarter wavelength", "Ionosphere"], "correct": 2, "short": "Conductive aircraft skin.", "expl": "The airframe skin acts as the missing quarter wave.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "A loop antenna is used as a receiving antenna because it is:", "options": ["A quarter-wave design", "Omnidirectional", "Highly direction-sensitive", "Vertically polarized only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Highly direction-sensitive.", "expl": "Its directional sensitivity is used for direction finding (e.g., ADF).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Antenna transmission-line coax should be secured along its length at intervals of approximately:", "options": ["1 foot", "2 feet", "6 inches", "6 feet"], "correct": 1, "short": "2 feet.", "expl": "Secure coax about every 2 ft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}]}]}, {"n": 11, "day": "Unit 11", "title": "Radio Navigation", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.11 · VOR/ADF/RMI · ILS/DME/RNAV · transponder/TCAS/ADS-B · altimeter/ELT/GPS/INS", "sections": [{"id": "b6m11s1", "title": "VOR, ADF & RMI", "lesson": "\n<p>Radio navigation aids give the pilot information that maintains or enhances the\nsafety of flight. Like comm radios, these are avionics devices whose repair is done\nby trained technicians at certified repair stations (p. 11-14).</p>\n\n<h3>VOR (VHF Omnidirectional Range)</h3>\n<p>The <b>VOR</b> is one of the oldest and most useful navigational aids, built after\nWorld War II and still used today. Thousands of land-based transmitter stations\n(VORs) communicate with airborne receivers (p. 11-15).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A ground VOR located at an airport is a <b>TVOR (terminal VOR)</b>.</li>\n<li>The military equivalent is <b>TACAN</b>; where a VOR and TACAN share a location,\nit is a <b>VORTAC</b>.</li>\n<li>VOR uses <b>VHF radio waves, 108&ndash;117.95 MHz</b>, with <b>50 kHz separation</b>\nbetween channels (p. 11-16).</li>\n<li>Reception uses a <b>V-shaped, horizontally polarized, bi-pole (dipole)\nantenna</b>.</li>\n<li>A VOR transmitter produces <b>two signals</b>: a <b>reference signal</b> and a\n<b>variable signal</b> (produced by electronically rotating). The airborne receiver\ncompares them to determine the aircraft's bearing relative to the station.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide324_1.jpeg\" alt=\"VOR\"><figcaption>VOR: VHF 108&ndash;117.95 MHz; the receiver compares a reference and a variable signal.</figcaption></figure>\n<p>Large aircraft may have <b>two dual receivers and dual antennas</b>, with a switch\nfor <b>NAV 1 / NAV 2</b> and for the <b>active or standby</b> frequency. Older\naircraft use a dedicated VOR gauge called an <b>omni-bearing selector (OBS)</b> or a\n<b>course deviation indicator (CDI)</b> (p. 11-16).</p>\n\n<h3>ADF (Automatic Direction Finder)</h3>\n<p>The <b>ADF</b> operates off a ground signal transmitted from a\n<b>nondirectional beacon (NDB)</b>. Modern ADF systems add a <b>sense antenna</b>\nwhose reception field is <b>omnidirectional</b>; combined with the <b>loop\nantenna</b>'s field, it forms a single significant <b>null</b> on one side. This is\nused for tuning and produces an indication <b>pointing toward the ADF/NDB station</b>\nat all times (p. 11-18).</p>\n\n<h3>RMI (Radio Magnetic Indicator)</h3>\n<p>The <b>RMI</b> saves panel space by consolidating information into one instrument:\nit combines indications from a <b>magnetic compass, VOR, and ADF</b> into a single\ndisplay (p. 11-19).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide329_1.jpeg\" alt=\"RMI\"><figcaption>The RMI combines magnetic compass, VOR, and ADF into one instrument.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>VOR</b>: VHF <b>108&ndash;117.95 MHz</b>, <b>50 kHz</b> channel spacing; two signals (<b>reference + variable</b>); VORTAC = VOR + TACAN co-located.</li>\n<li>VOR display = <b>OBS / CDI</b>.</li>\n<li><b>ADF</b> uses an <b>NDB</b> ground signal; the <b>loop + sense antenna</b> make a null that points to the station.</li>\n<li><b>RMI</b> = magnetic compass + VOR + ADF in one instrument.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The VOR navigation system operates on which frequency range?", "options": ["108–117.95 MHz (VHF)", "406.025 MHz", "329.3–335.0 MHz (UHF)", "962–1213 MHz (UHF)"], "correct": 0, "short": "108–117.95 MHz (VHF).", "expl": "VOR is VHF, 108–117.95 MHz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "VOR channels are separated by:", "options": ["50 kHz", "100 kHz", "25 kHz", "1 MHz"], "correct": 0, "short": "50 kHz.", "expl": "50 kHz separation between VOR channels.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "A VOR transmitter produces two signals: a reference signal and a:", "options": ["Variable signal (electronically rotated)", "406 MHz beacon signal", "150 Hz side signal", "Modulated glideslope signal"], "correct": 0, "short": "A variable (rotating) signal.", "expl": "The receiver compares reference vs. variable to find the bearing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "A co-located VOR and military TACAN station is called a:", "options": ["VORTAC", "NDB", "TVOR", "DME"], "correct": 0, "short": "VORTAC.", "expl": "VOR + TACAN sharing a location = VORTAC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The antenna generally used to receive VOR signals is a:", "options": ["V-shaped, horizontally polarized bi-pole (dipole)", "Parabolic dish", "Loop antenna", "Vertical whip"], "correct": 0, "short": "V-shaped horizontally polarized dipole.", "expl": "The classic VOR/dipole antenna.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An older dedicated VOR gauge is also called an omni-bearing selector (OBS) or a:", "options": ["Course deviation indicator (CDI)", "Radio magnetic indicator (RMI)", "Horizontal situation indicator only", "Flight director"], "correct": 0, "short": "Course deviation indicator (CDI).", "expl": "OBS/CDI display VOR course info.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The ADF operates off a ground signal transmitted from a:", "options": ["Localizer", "VORTAC", "Marker beacon", "Nondirectional beacon (NDB)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Nondirectional beacon (NDB).", "expl": "ADF homes on an NDB.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "In a modern ADF, the omnidirectional sense antenna is combined with the loop antenna to create a:", "options": ["Single significant null that points toward the station", "Second carrier wave", "150 Hz modulation", "Glideslope"], "correct": 0, "short": "A single null that points to the station.", "expl": "The null gives an unambiguous bearing to the NDB.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The Radio Magnetic Indicator (RMI) combines indications from the magnetic compass, the VOR, and the:", "options": ["DME", "Transponder", "ADF", "Radio altimeter"], "correct": 2, "short": "ADF.", "expl": "RMI = magnetic compass + VOR + ADF in one instrument.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The purpose of the RMI is primarily to:", "options": ["Save panel space by consolidating related information into one instrument", "Provide vertical guidance for landing", "Transmit an emergency signal", "Measure height above terrain"], "correct": 0, "short": "Consolidate related info into one instrument.", "expl": "It saves panel space.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}]}, {"id": "b6m11s2", "title": "ILS, DME & RNAV", "lesson": "\n<h3>Instrument Landing System (ILS)</h3>\n<p>The <b>ILS</b> is used to land an aircraft when visibility is poor, using several\nradio transmissions for an exact approach (p. 11-20).</p>\n\n<h4>Localizer — horizontal guidance</h4>\n<p>The <b>localizer</b> provides <b>horizontal guidance to the runway centerline</b>.\nIt is a <b>VHF broadcast in the lower range of the VOR frequencies,\n108&ndash;111.95 MHz, on odd frequencies only</b>. A horizontally polarized antenna\nbeyond the far end of the runway sends two modulated signals: the <b>left side of the\napproach is a 90 Hz</b> signal and the <b>right side is a 150 Hz</b> signal. The\naircraft's <b>VOR receiver</b> is tuned to the localizer frequency (p. 11-20).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig medium\"><img src=\"img/slide330_1.jpeg\" alt=\"ILS\"><figcaption>ILS: localizer (horizontal), glideslope (vertical), and marker beacons.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h4>Glideslope — vertical guidance</h4>\n<p>The <b>glideslope</b> provides the <b>vertical guidance</b> to descend for landing\nat approximately a <b>3-degree</b> angle. Its transmitting antenna is off to the side\nof the approach runway, about <b>1,000 feet from the threshold</b>, and is\n<b>horizontally polarized</b>. The glideslope frequency range is <b>UHF, 329.3 to\n335.0 MHz</b>. Like the localizer, it transmits two signals: one modulated at\n<b>90 Hz</b> and one at <b>150 Hz</b> (p. 11-21).</p>\n\n<h4>Marker beacons — distance along the approach</h4>\n<p>Marker beacons indicate the aircraft's position along the glidepath; all transmit\na <b>75 MHz</b> carrier (p. 11-21):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Outer marker</b> — located <b>4&ndash;7 miles</b> from the threshold; 400 Hz\ntone; lights a <b>blue light</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Middle marker</b> — located about <b>3,500 feet</b> from the runway; lights an\n<b>amber light</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Inner marker</b> — modulated with a <b>3,000 Hz</b> tone; lights a <b>white\nlight</b> (used on some approaches).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Distance Measuring Equipment (DME)</h3>\n<p><b>DME</b> is a TACAN feature that civilian aircraft use. It calculates the\n<b>distance from the aircraft to the DME unit at the VORTAC ground station</b> and\ndisplays it; it can also display calculated <b>speed and elapsed time</b>. DME\noperates in the <b>UHF range, 962 to 1213 MHz</b>. The readout can be on a dedicated\nDME display or part of an EHSI, EADI, EFIS, or the primary flight display (p. 11-22).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide334_1.jpeg\" alt=\"DME\"><figcaption>DME shows slant distance to the ground station (UHF 962&ndash;1213 MHz).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Area Navigation (RNAV)</h3>\n<p><b>RNAV</b> systems use <b>waypoints</b> — a designated geographical location or\npoint used for route definition or progress reporting. VOR-based RNAV uses the VOR\nreceiver, antenna, and display (CDI). RNAV flexibility increased greatly with the\ndevelopment of <b>GPS</b> (p. 11-23).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Localizer</b> = horizontal guidance, VHF <b>108&ndash;111.95 MHz odd</b>; left 90 Hz / right 150 Hz; uses the VOR receiver.</li>\n<li><b>Glideslope</b> = vertical guidance, ~<b>3&deg;</b>, UHF <b>329.3&ndash;335.0 MHz</b>; 90 Hz / 150 Hz; antenna ~1,000 ft from threshold.</li>\n<li>Marker beacons all on <b>75 MHz</b>: outer <b>blue</b> (4&ndash;7 mi), middle <b>amber</b> (~3,500 ft), inner <b>white</b> (3,000 Hz).</li>\n<li><b>DME</b> = distance to VORTAC, UHF <b>962&ndash;1213 MHz</b>; can show speed/time.</li>\n<li><b>RNAV</b> uses <b>waypoints</b>; greatly enhanced by GPS.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The ILS is used to:", "options": ["Land an aircraft when visibility is poor", "Identify the aircraft on ATC radar", "Measure height above terrain", "Provide long-range oceanic navigation"], "correct": 0, "short": "Land the aircraft in poor visibility.", "expl": "ILS gives an exact approach path.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The localizer portion of the ILS provides:", "options": ["Horizontal guidance to the runway centerline", "Vertical guidance", "Altitude above terrain", "Distance to the runway"], "correct": 0, "short": "Horizontal guidance to centerline.", "expl": "Localizer = lateral/horizontal guidance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The localizer broadcasts in the lower range of the VOR frequencies:", "options": ["108–111.95 MHz, odd frequencies only", "329.3–335.0 MHz", "75 MHz", "962–1213 MHz"], "correct": 0, "short": "108–111.95 MHz, odd only.", "expl": "Localizer VHF, lower VOR band, odd frequencies.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The glideslope portion of the ILS provides:", "options": ["Aircraft identification", "Horizontal guidance", "Vertical guidance for descent (about a 3° angle)", "Distance information"], "correct": 2, "short": "Vertical guidance (~3°).", "expl": "Glideslope = vertical descent guidance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The glideslope operates in which frequency range?", "options": ["UHF 329.3–335.0 MHz", "108–111.95 MHz", "406 MHz", "75 MHz"], "correct": 0, "short": "UHF 329.3–335.0 MHz.", "expl": "Glideslope is UHF.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Both the localizer and glideslope transmit two modulated signals of:", "options": ["90 Hz and 150 Hz", "50 Hz and 60 Hz", "400 Hz and 1300 Hz", "75 Hz and 100 Hz"], "correct": 0, "short": "90 Hz and 150 Hz.", "expl": "The two ILS modulation tones.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "All ILS marker beacons transmit on a carrier frequency of:", "options": ["75 MHz", "108 MHz", "406 MHz", "329 MHz"], "correct": 0, "short": "75 MHz.", "expl": "Outer/middle/inner markers all use 75 MHz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The outer marker beacon is located about how far from the runway threshold, and lights which color?", "options": ["4–7 miles; blue", "3,500 feet; amber", "10 miles; green", "1,000 feet; white"], "correct": 0, "short": "4–7 miles; blue light.", "expl": "Outer marker = 4–7 mi, blue.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The middle marker beacon lights which color light?", "options": ["White", "Amber", "Blue", "Green"], "correct": 1, "short": "Amber.", "expl": "Middle marker ≈ 3,500 ft from runway; amber light.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "DME calculates the distance from the aircraft to the DME unit at the:", "options": ["Localizer antenna", "VORTAC ground station", "Nearest NDB", "Middle marker"], "correct": 1, "short": "VORTAC ground station.", "expl": "DME shows slant distance to the ground station.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "DME operates in which frequency range?", "options": ["962–1213 MHz (UHF)", "75 MHz", "329.3–335.0 MHz", "108–117.95 MHz"], "correct": 0, "short": "962–1213 MHz.", "expl": "DME is UHF, 962–1213 MHz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Besides distance, DME can also display calculated:", "options": ["Fuel remaining", "Wind direction", "Cabin altitude", "Aircraft speed and elapsed time"], "correct": 3, "short": "Speed and elapsed time.", "expl": "DME can compute groundspeed and time to the station.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "RNAV systems make use of:", "options": ["Marker beacons only", "Glideslope signals", "Waypoints (designated geographical locations/points)", "The magnetic compass only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Waypoints.", "expl": "RNAV routes are defined by waypoints; GPS greatly expanded RNAV.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}]}, {"id": "b6m11s3", "title": "Transponder, altitude encoder, TCAS & ADS-B", "lesson": "\n<h3>Radar beacon transponder</h3>\n<p>A <b>transponder</b> provides <b>positive identification and location</b> of an\naircraft on the radar screens of Air Traffic Control (ATC). An aircraft with an\n<b>altitude-encoding transponder</b> also provides the aircraft's <b>pressure\naltitude</b> (p. 11-24).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>When in verbal contact with ATC, the pilot is instructed to select one of\n<b>4,096 discrete codes</b> on the transponder.</li>\n<li>To send altitude, the transponder control must be in the <b>ALT or Mode C</b>\nposition.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide336_1.jpeg\" alt=\"transponder\"><figcaption>The transponder returns a coded reply that identifies and locates the aircraft on ATC radar.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Altitude encoders</h3>\n<p>An <b>altitude encoder</b> converts the aircraft's <b>pressure altitude</b> into a\ncode sent by the transponder to ATC. Altitude is usually reported in increments of\n<b>100 feet</b> (p. 11-25).</p>\n\n<h3>Collision avoidance — TCAS</h3>\n<p><b>Traffic Collision Avoidance Systems (TCAS)</b> are <b>transponder-based\nair-to-air</b> traffic monitoring and alerting systems (p. 11-26). Two classes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>TCAS I</b> — for the general aviation community and regional airlines; monitors\ntraffic in a <b>35&ndash;40 mile range</b>.</li>\n<li><b>TCAS II</b> — more sophisticated; required internationally in aircraft with\n<b>more than 30 seats or weighing more than 15,000 kg</b>. It does everything TCAS I\ndoes and also <b>analyzes the projected flightpath</b> of approaching aircraft.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>TCAS may also be called <b>ACAS (airborne collision avoidance system)</b>, the\ninternational name for the same system.</p>\n\n<h3>Collision avoidance — ADS-B</h3>\n<p><b>Automatic Dependent Surveillance-Broadcast (ADS-B)</b> was developed from the\nsuccess of <b>global navigation satellite systems (GNSS)</b> such as GPS\n(p. 11-27). It has two segments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ADS-B OUT</b> — combines GPS positioning with on-board flight-status\ninformation (location including <b>altitude, velocity, and time</b>) and broadcasts\nit.</li>\n<li><b>ADS-B IN</b> — offers features not available in TCAS; equipped aircraft\nreceive abundant data to <b>enhance situational awareness</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Transponder</b> gives positive ID/location on ATC radar; pilot selects one of <b>4,096</b> codes; <b>ALT/Mode C</b> sends altitude.</li>\n<li><b>Altitude encoder</b> converts <b>pressure altitude</b> to a code, reported in <b>100-ft</b> increments.</li>\n<li><b>TCAS</b> is <b>transponder-based, air-to-air</b>. TCAS I = 35&ndash;40 mi; TCAS II required in aircraft <b>&gt;30 seats or &gt;15,000 kg</b> and analyzes flightpaths. TCAS = <b>ACAS</b>.</li>\n<li><b>ADS-B OUT</b> broadcasts GPS position + status; <b>ADS-B IN</b> receives data for situational awareness.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A radar beacon transponder provides ATC with:", "options": ["Emergency crash signals", "Weather information", "Positive identification and location of the aircraft on radar", "Vertical landing guidance"], "correct": 2, "short": "Positive ID and location on radar.", "expl": "The transponder replies to ATC radar interrogation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "A pilot is instructed to select one of how many discrete transponder codes?", "options": ["1,200", "4,096", "10,000", "120"], "correct": 1, "short": "4,096.", "expl": "There are 4,096 discrete transponder codes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "To have the transponder report the aircraft's altitude, its control must be in the:", "options": ["GND position", "STBY position", "OFF position", "ALT or Mode C position"], "correct": 3, "short": "ALT or Mode C.", "expl": "Mode C/ALT sends encoded altitude to ATC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An altitude encoder converts the aircraft's ____ into a code sent by the transponder.", "options": ["indicated airspeed", "heading", "pressure altitude", "vertical speed"], "correct": 2, "short": "Pressure altitude.", "expl": "The encoder digitizes pressure altitude for the transponder.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Altitude is usually reported to ATC in increments of:", "options": ["10 feet", "1,000 feet", "500 feet", "100 feet"], "correct": 3, "short": "100 feet.", "expl": "Encoded altitude increments are 100 ft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "TCAS is best described as a system that is:", "options": ["Satellite-based positioning", "An emergency locator", "Transponder-based and air-to-air", "Ground-based and radio-navigation only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Transponder-based, air-to-air.", "expl": "TCAS monitors nearby transponder-equipped traffic.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "TCAS I monitors traffic within a range of about:", "options": ["5–10 miles", "100 miles", "35–40 miles", "200 miles"], "correct": 2, "short": "35–40 miles.", "expl": "TCAS I range is 35–40 mi.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "TCAS II is required internationally in aircraft with more than:", "options": ["50 seats or 25,000 kg", "10 seats or 5,000 kg", "30 seats or weighing more than 15,000 kg", "100 seats"], "correct": 2, "short": "30 seats or 15,000 kg.", "expl": "TCAS II threshold: >30 seats or >15,000 kg.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The international name for the same type of system as TCAS is:", "options": ["GNSS", "ADS-B", "WAAS", "ACAS (airborne collision avoidance system)"], "correct": 3, "short": "ACAS.", "expl": "TCAS may be called ACAS internationally.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "ADS-B was made possible by the success of:", "options": ["Global navigation satellite systems (GNSS) such as GPS", "LORAN towers", "Vacuum-tube electronics", "Marker beacons"], "correct": 0, "short": "GNSS/GPS.", "expl": "ADS-B is built on satellite positioning.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "ADS-B OUT broadcasts GPS position combined with on-board flight status such as:", "options": ["Fuel type", "Passenger count", "Altitude, velocity, and time", "Engine oil pressure"], "correct": 2, "short": "Altitude, velocity, and time.", "expl": "ADS-B OUT = position + status broadcast.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "ADS-B IN differs from TCAS in that it:", "options": ["Only transmits", "Is ground-based", "Receives abundant data to enhance situational awareness beyond TCAS features", "Works without GPS"], "correct": 2, "short": "Receives data to enhance situational awareness.", "expl": "ADS-B IN offers features TCAS does not.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}]}, {"id": "b6m11s4", "title": "Altimeters, weather radar, ELT, LORAN, GPS & INS", "lesson": "\n<h3>Radio (radar) altimeter</h3>\n<p>A <b>radio altimeter</b> (radar altimeter) measures the distance from the aircraft\nto the <b>terrain directly beneath it</b>. It is used primarily during instrument\napproach and low-level or night flight <b>below 2,500 feet</b>, and supplies the\nprimary altitude information for the landing <b>decision height</b>. Using a\ntransceiver and a directional antenna, it broadcasts a carrier wave at <b>4.3 GHz</b>\nstraight down and displays the height above terrain, known as <b>above ground level\n(AGL)</b> (p. 11-28).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide347_1.jpeg\" alt=\"radio altimeter\"><figcaption>The radio altimeter measures height above terrain (AGL) at 4.3 GHz.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Weather radar</h3>\n<p>Three common types of weather aids are used in flight decks (p. 11-29):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>On-board radar</b> for detecting and displaying weather activity.</li>\n<li><b>Lightning detectors</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Satellite (or other source) weather</b> information uploaded to the aircraft\nfrom an outside source.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Emergency Locator Transmitter (ELT)</h3>\n<p>An <b>ELT</b> is an independent, <b>battery-powered</b> transmitter <b>activated by\nthe excessive G-forces experienced during a crash</b> (p. 11-30). Key facts:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>It transmits a digital signal <b>every 50 seconds</b> on a frequency of\n<b>406.025 MHz</b> at <b>5 watts</b> for at least <b>24 hours</b>.</li>\n<li>Required to be installed per <b>14 CFR 91.207</b>.</li>\n<li>Must be <b>inspected within 12 months</b> of the previous inspection for proper\ninstallation, <b>battery corrosion</b>, operation of the controls and crash sensor,\nand sufficient signal at the antenna.</li>\n<li>Installed as <b>far aft in the fuselage as practicable, just forward of the\nempennage</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide354_1.jpeg\" alt=\"ELT\"><figcaption>The ELT transmits on 406.025 MHz when triggered by crash G-forces.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>LORAN</h3>\n<p><b>LORAN (Long Range Aid to Navigation)</b> is a type of RNAV that is <b>no longer\navailable</b> in the United States. It used radio-wave pulses from a series of towers\nplus an on-board receiver/computer to locate the aircraft; <b>twelve LORAN tower\n\"chains\"</b> were built across North America. The accuracy and proliferation of\n<b>GPS</b> caused the U.S. government to cease support of LORAN, citing redundancy and\nexpense (p. 11-31).</p>\n\n<h3>Global Positioning System (GPS)</h3>\n<p><b>GPS</b> is the fastest-growing type of navigation in aviation, using\n<b>NAVSTAR satellites</b> maintained in orbit by the U.S. government (p. 11-32).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>GPS has <b>three segments</b>: the <b>space segment</b>, the <b>control\nsegment</b>, and the <b>user segment</b>.</li>\n<li>Current GPS accuracy is within about <b>20 meters horizontally</b> (a bit more\nvertically).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>WAAS and INS/IRS</h3>\n<p>The <b>Wide Area Augmentation System (WAAS)</b> <b>refines aircraft GPS positions\nin the USA</b> for greater accuracy (p. 11-33).</p>\n<p>The <b>Inertial Navigation System (INS) / Inertial Reference System (IRS)</b> is\nused on some large aircraft for long-range navigation. It is a <b>self-contained\nsystem that does NOT require input radio signals</b> from any ground navigation\nfacility, and is integrated into the airliner's <b>flight management system</b> and\n<b>automatic flight control system</b> (p. 11-34).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Radio/radar altimeter</b>: height above terrain (<b>AGL</b>), used <b>below 2,500 ft</b>, carrier <b>4.3 GHz</b>, supplies decision height.</li>\n<li>Weather aids: on-board <b>radar</b>, <b>lightning detectors</b>, uploaded <b>satellite</b> weather.</li>\n<li><b>ELT</b>: crash-G activated; <b>406.025 MHz</b>, <b>5 W</b>, every <b>50 s</b>, &ge;<b>24 h</b>; per <b>91.207</b>; inspect within <b>12 months</b>; mounted <b>aft, just forward of the empennage</b>.</li>\n<li><b>LORAN</b> = discontinued RNAV. <b>GPS</b> = NAVSTAR, 3 segments (<b>space/control/user</b>), ~<b>20 m</b> accuracy.</li>\n<li><b>WAAS</b> refines GPS accuracy. <b>INS/IRS</b> = self-contained, <b>no ground radio input</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "A radio (radar) altimeter measures the distance from the aircraft to:", "options": ["The cloud tops", "The terrain directly beneath it", "The nearest VOR", "The destination airport"], "correct": 1, "short": "The terrain directly beneath it.", "expl": "It reads height above ground level (AGL).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The radio altimeter is used primarily during approach and low-level/night flight below:", "options": ["500 feet", "10,000 feet", "2,500 feet", "1,000 feet"], "correct": 2, "short": "2,500 feet.", "expl": "Radio altimeter is used below 2,500 ft, supplying decision height.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The radio altimeter broadcasts a carrier wave at:", "options": ["4.3 GHz", "1.2 GHz", "406 MHz", "10 GHz"], "correct": 0, "short": "4.3 GHz.", "expl": "Radio altimeter carrier = 4.3 GHz, aimed straight down.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The height displayed by a radio altimeter is known as:", "options": ["Density altitude", "Pressure altitude", "Flight level", "Above ground level (AGL)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Above ground level (AGL).", "expl": "It shows height above the terrain below.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Which is one of the three common types of weather aids in a flight deck?", "options": ["On-board radar for detecting weather", "Transponder", "Marker beacon", "Altitude encoder"], "correct": 0, "short": "On-board weather radar.", "expl": "Also lightning detectors and uploaded satellite weather.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An Emergency Locator Transmitter (ELT) is activated by:", "options": ["Engine shutdown", "The excessive G-forces experienced during a crash", "The pilot only", "Loss of GPS signal"], "correct": 1, "short": "Crash G-forces.", "expl": "The crash sensor triggers the ELT (it can also be manual).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An ELT transmits on a frequency of:", "options": ["75 MHz", "406.025 MHz", "121.5 MHz only", "4.3 GHz"], "correct": 1, "short": "406.025 MHz.", "expl": "Modern ELTs transmit a digital signal on 406.025 MHz.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An ELT transmits a digital signal every 50 seconds at 5 watts for at least:", "options": ["24 hours", "1 hour", "12 hours", "72 hours"], "correct": 0, "short": "24 hours.", "expl": "ELT operates at least 24 hours after activation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "ELT installation is required by which regulation?", "options": ["14 CFR 43.13", "14 CFR 91.413", "14 CFR 65.81", "14 CFR 91.207"], "correct": 3, "short": "14 CFR 91.207.", "expl": "Part 91.207 governs ELTs.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An ELT must be inspected within how many months of the previous inspection?", "options": ["6 months", "24 months", "36 months", "12 months"], "correct": 3, "short": "12 months.", "expl": "ELT inspection interval is within 12 months.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An ELT is installed:", "options": ["In an engine nacelle", "As far aft in the fuselage as practicable, just forward of the empennage", "In the nose of the aircraft", "Under the cockpit floor"], "correct": 1, "short": "Aft, just forward of the empennage.", "expl": "Aft mounting improves post-crash survivability of the signal.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "LORAN is a navigation system that is:", "options": ["A type of RNAV no longer available in the United States", "The fastest-growing type of navigation", "Satellite-based", "Required on all transport aircraft"], "correct": 0, "short": "A discontinued (RNAV) system in the U.S.", "expl": "GPS made LORAN redundant.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "GPS uses which satellites, maintained by the U.S. government?", "options": ["WAAS", "TACAN", "GOES", "NAVSTAR"], "correct": 3, "short": "NAVSTAR.", "expl": "GPS is based on the NAVSTAR satellite constellation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The three segments of GPS are the:", "options": ["Reference, variable, and null segments", "Outer, middle, and inner segments", "Space, control, and user segments", "Ground, sky, and space segments"], "correct": 2, "short": "Space, control, and user.", "expl": "The three GPS segments.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Current GPS horizontal accuracy is within about:", "options": ["200 meters", "2 meters", "2 kilometers", "20 meters"], "correct": 3, "short": "20 meters.", "expl": "About 20 m horizontally (a bit more vertically).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The Wide Area Augmentation System (WAAS) is used to:", "options": ["Refine aircraft GPS positions in the USA for greater accuracy", "Transmit emergency signals", "Replace GPS entirely", "Provide weather radar"], "correct": 0, "short": "Refine GPS positions in the USA.", "expl": "WAAS augments/improves GPS accuracy.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An Inertial Navigation System (INS)/Inertial Reference System (IRS) is unique because it:", "options": ["Requires continuous VOR input", "Is self-contained and does NOT require input radio signals from the ground", "Only works below 2,500 feet", "Is a type of transponder"], "correct": 1, "short": "Self-contained; no ground radio input.", "expl": "INS/IRS navigates independently, integrated into the FMS/AFCS.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}]}]}, {"n": 12, "day": "Unit 12", "title": "Avionics Installation, Autopilot & EFIS", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.11 install/interference + Ch.10 autopilot/EFIS · STC/337 · static wicks · AFCS · glass cockpit", "sections": [{"id": "b6m12s1", "title": "Avionics installation & interference", "lesson": "\n<p>Most avionics equipment is only repairable by the manufacturer or an\nFAA-certified repair station. However, the <b>airframe technician must competently\nremove, install, inspect, maintain, and troubleshoot</b> avionics (p. 11-72).</p>\n\n<h3>Approval of new installations</h3>\n<p>Most new avionics installations are approved and performed under a\n<b>Supplemental Type Certificate (STC)</b>, and an <b>FAA Form 337</b> must also be\nfiled (p. 11-72). Before altering an aircraft by adding avionics, the technician must\nconsider (p. 11-72):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The <b>space available</b>, and the <b>size and weight</b> of the equipment.</li>\n<li>Any <b>previously accomplished alterations</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>power consumption</b> of the added equipment.</li>\n<li>Planning the installation to allow <b>easy access</b> for inspection,\nmaintenance, and exchange of units.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><b>Cooling and moisture</b>: plan the installation so the unit can <b>dissipate\nheat readily</b>, and use proper <b>baffling</b> to prevent water from reaching the\nelectronics. <b>Vibration isolation</b>: special <b>shock-mounted racks</b> isolate\nequipment from vibrating structure (p. 11-73).</p>\n\n<h3>Reducing radio interference</h3>\n<p>Suppressing unwanted electromagnetic fields and electrostatic interference is\nessential on all aircraft. The four tools (p. 11-74):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Shielding</b> — shielding wires to electrical components and ignition systems\n<b>dissipates radio-frequency noise energy</b>; all electrical components should also\nbe <b>bonded to the aircraft structure (ground)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Isolation</b> — <b>separating the source of the noise</b> from the input\ncircuits of the affected equipment.</li>\n<li><b>Bonding</b> — provides the electric connection between metallic parts to\nprevent variable resistance in the airframe, giving a <b>low-impedance ground\nreturn</b> that minimizes interference from static-electricity charges.</li>\n<li><b>Static discharge wicks</b> — discharge <b>precipitation static</b> at points a\n<b>critical distance away from the avionics antennas</b>, where there is little or no\ncoupling of the static to cause interference or noise.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide372_1.jpeg\" alt=\"static dissipation\"><figcaption>Static discharge wicks bleed off precipitation static away from the antennas.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Antenna & transmission-line installation</h3>\n<p>A transmitting or receiving antenna connects directly to its transmitter/receiver\nby a <b>transmission line — shielded wire known as coax</b> (p. 11-75). Rules:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>To assure optimum operation, coaxial cable should <b>NOT be routed or tied to\nother wire bundles</b>.</li>\n<li>When bending coax, the bend must be at least <b>10 times the cable diameter</b>.</li>\n<li>Poor performance or inoperative avionics can result from <b>loose cable\nconnections, switched cable terminations, improper bonding, worn shock mounts,\nimproper safety wiring, and failure to perform an operational check after\ninstallation</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>New avionics installs are done under an <b>STC</b> with an <b>FAA Form 337</b> filed.</li>\n<li>Consider space, size/weight, prior alterations, <b>power consumption</b>, and <b>access</b>; plus <b>cooling/baffling</b> and <b>shock-mounted racks</b>.</li>\n<li>Interference tools: <b>shielding, isolation, bonding, static discharge wicks</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Static wicks</b> discharge precipitation static away from antennas.</li>\n<li>Coax = shielded transmission line; <b>don't tie it to other bundles</b>; bend &ge; <b>10&times; diameter</b>; always do an <b>operational check</b> after install.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Most new avionics installations are approved and performed under a(n):", "options": ["Supplemental Type Certificate (STC), with FAA Form 337 filed", "Type Certificate Data Sheet only", "Verbal approval from the pilot", "Airworthiness Directive"], "correct": 0, "short": "STC, with FAA Form 337 filed.", "expl": "New installs use an STC and a Form 337.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Even though avionics repair is specialized, the airframe technician must competently:", "options": ["Manufacture replacement circuit boards", "Redesign the avionics", "Certify the software", "Remove, install, inspect, maintain, and troubleshoot avionics"], "correct": 3, "short": "Remove, install, inspect, maintain, troubleshoot.", "expl": "The A&P handles installation and general upkeep.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Before adding avionics, the technician must consider space, size/weight, prior alterations, and:", "options": ["The power consumption of the added equipment", "The pilot's preferences only", "The aircraft's registration number", "The paint color"], "correct": 0, "short": "Power consumption of the equipment.", "expl": "Plus access for inspection/maintenance/exchange.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Avionics installations should be planned to allow:", "options": ["Maximum vibration", "Water to pool on the unit", "Easy access for inspection, maintenance, and exchange of units", "The unit to be sealed in permanently"], "correct": 2, "short": "Easy access for inspection/maintenance/exchange.", "expl": "Accessibility is a key planning factor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "To protect avionics from heat and water, the installation should provide:", "options": ["No cooling at all", "A sealed box with no airflow", "Heat dissipation and proper baffling to keep water out", "Direct exposure to rain"], "correct": 1, "short": "Heat dissipation + baffling against water.", "expl": "Cooling and moisture protection are required.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Special shock-mounted racks are used to:", "options": ["Provide electrical grounding only", "Isolate equipment from vibrating structure", "Increase equipment vibration", "Add weight"], "correct": 1, "short": "Isolate equipment from vibration.", "expl": "Vibration isolation protects sensitive avionics.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Which is one of the four tools for reducing radio interference?", "options": ["Static discharge wicks", "Adding fuel", "Painting", "Increasing frequency"], "correct": 0, "short": "Static discharge wicks.", "expl": "Shielding, isolation, bonding, and static discharge wicks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Static discharge wicks work by discharging precipitation static:", "options": ["Through the fuel system", "Directly at the antenna", "At points a critical distance away from the avionics antennas", "Into the cabin"], "correct": 2, "short": "Away from the antennas (critical distance).", "expl": "This keeps the static from coupling into the antennas as noise.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "In interference control, 'isolation' means:", "options": ["Separating the source of the noise from the input circuits of the affected equipment", "Grounding every component", "Bonding two metal parts", "Shielding the antenna"], "correct": 0, "short": "Separating the noise source from the affected equipment.", "expl": "Isolation keeps noise and victim circuits apart.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "Bonding reduces interference by providing a:", "options": ["High-resistance path", "Low-impedance ground return that minimizes static interference", "Higher operating voltage", "Larger antenna"], "correct": 1, "short": "Low-impedance ground return.", "expl": "Good bonding prevents variable resistance and drains static.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "When installing coaxial cable to an antenna, the coax should:", "options": ["Be bent as sharply as possible", "Never be secured", "NOT be routed or tied to other wire bundles", "Be tied to other wire bundles for support"], "correct": 2, "short": "NOT be tied to other wire bundles.", "expl": "Keeping coax separate assures optimum RF operation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "When bending coaxial cable, the bend radius must be at least:", "options": ["5 times the cable diameter", "2 times the cable diameter", "10 times the cable diameter", "25 times the cable diameter"], "correct": 2, "short": "10 times the cable diameter.", "expl": "A too-tight bend degrades the coax.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "A step that must not be skipped after an avionics installation is:", "options": ["Repainting the aircraft", "Performing an operational check", "Disconnecting the bonding", "Removing the shock mounts"], "correct": 1, "short": "Performing an operational check.", "expl": "Skipping the op-check can leave avionics poorly performing or inoperative.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}]}, {"id": "b6m12s2", "title": "Autopilot, flight director & EFIS", "lesson": "\n<p>An <b>autopilot</b> flies the aircraft automatically using electrical signals from\ngyro-sensing units connected to flight instruments that indicate direction, rate of\nturn, bank, and pitch (p. 10-61).</p>\n\n<h3>The four basic autopilot components</h3>\n<p>Most autopilot systems have <b>four basic components</b> (plus switches and\nauxiliary units) (p. 10-61):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Sensing elements</b> — the <b>attitude and directional gyros, the turn\ncoordinator, and an altitude control</b>. They sense what the aircraft is doing.</li>\n<li><b>Computing element (computer/amplifier)</b> — analog or digital; it\n<b>interprets the sensing data, integrates commands and navigational input, and sends\nsignals to the output elements</b> to move the flight controls as required.</li>\n<li><b>Output elements</b> — the <b>servos</b> that actuate the flight control\nsurfaces; independent devices for each control channel that integrate into the regular\nflight-control system.</li>\n<li><b>Command elements</b> — the command unit, called the <b>flight controller</b>,\nis the <b>human interface</b> that lets the pilot tell the autopilot what to do.</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide378_1.jpeg\" alt=\"autopilot components\"><figcaption>Sensing, computing, output (servo), and command elements of an autopilot.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Feedback / follow-up</h3>\n<p>Various electric <b>feedback (follow-up) signals</b> are generated to\n<b>progressively reduce the error signal</b> in the autopilot so that <b>continuous\nover-correction does not take place</b>. This is typically done with <b>transducers on\nthe surface actuators or in the autopilot servo units</b> (p. 10-62).</p>\n\n<h3>Yaw damper</h3>\n<p>Many aircraft tend to <b>oscillate around their vertical axis</b> (Dutch roll)\nwhile flying a fixed heading, requiring near-continuous rudder input to counteract. A\n<b>yaw damper</b> corrects this motion automatically; it can be part of the autopilot\nor a completely independent unit (p. 10-63).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide380_1.jpeg\" alt=\"yaw damper\"><figcaption>A yaw damper counters oscillation about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Helicopter stability augmentation system (SAS)</h3>\n<p>Some helicopters use a <b>stability augmentation system (SAS)</b> to help\nstabilize the helicopter in flight and hover. The simplest is a <b>force-trim\nsystem</b>, which uses a <b>magnetic clutch and springs</b> to hold the cyclic\ncontrol where it was released. More advanced systems use electric actuators that input\nto the hydraulic servos. The SAS <b>reduces pilot workload</b> and can be\n<b>overridden or disconnected by the pilot at any time</b> (p. 10-64).</p>\n\n<h3>Automatic Flight Control System (AFCS)</h3>\n<p>An autopilot with many features and various related systems integrated into a\nsingle system is an <b>Automatic Flight Control System (AFCS)</b>. Modern general\naviation AFCS are fully integrated with digital <b>attitude heading and reference\nsystems (AHRS)</b> and navigational aids including glideslope. Functionality is\ndistributed across interrelated computers and uses <b>intelligent servos</b> that\nhandle some error-correction calculations (p. 10-66).</p>\n\n<h3>Flight director</h3>\n<p>A <b>flight director</b> is an instrument system that computes and indicates the\naircraft <b>attitude required to attain and maintain a preselected flight\ncondition</b>. A <b>command bar</b> on the attitude indicator shows the pilot how\nmuch and in what direction to change attitude. Essentially, a flight director is\n<b>an autopilot system without the servos</b> — all the same sensing and computing is\ndone, but the <b>pilot flies the aircraft by following the commands</b> displayed.\nThe display instrument is the flight director indicator (FDI), attitude director\nindicator (ADI), or <b>electronic attitude director indicator (EADI)</b> (p. 10-67).</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide384_1.jpeg\" alt=\"flight director\"><figcaption>A flight director shows command bars; the pilot flies to them (no servos).</figcaption></figure>\n\n<h3>Electronic instruments & EFIS</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>EADI</b> — an advanced attitude indicator that displays attitude plus numerous\nother situational flight parameters in close proximity for easy observation.</li>\n<li><b>EHSI</b> — an evolved version of the horizontal situation indicator (HSI),\nwhich grew from the gyroscopic directional gyro.</li>\n<li><b>EFIS (Electronic Flight Instrument System)</b> — flight instruments were the\nfirst to adopt computer technology, using flat-screen <b>multifunction displays\n(MFD)</b>. The <b>electronic depiction of ADI and HSI information is the core purpose\nof EFIS</b>. A <b>\"glass cockpit\"</b> uses flat-panel display screens with\ncomputer-produced images replacing individual mechanical gauges.</li>\n<li><b>ECAM (Electronic Centralized Aircraft Monitor)</b> — automatically performs\nmonitoring duties for the pilot; when a problem or failure occurs, the primary display\nplus an <b>aural and visual cue</b> alerts the pilot. Older ECAM used two CRT\nmonitors; newer aircraft use LCDs (p. 10-70).</li>\n</ul>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide387_1.jpeg\" alt=\"EFIS glass cockpit\"><figcaption>EFIS \"glass cockpit\": flat-panel displays replace mechanical gauges.</figcaption></figure>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Autopilot's four components: <b>sensing, computing, output (servos), command (flight controller)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Feedback/follow-up</b> reduces the error signal so the autopilot doesn't over-correct.</li>\n<li><b>Yaw damper</b> stops oscillation about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).</li>\n<li><b>Helicopter SAS</b> stabilizes hover/flight; simplest = <b>force trim (magnetic clutch + springs)</b>; pilot can override.</li>\n<li><b>AFCS</b> = integrated autopilot + systems (AHRS, glideslope, intelligent servos).</li>\n<li><b>Flight director</b> = autopilot <b>without servos</b>; pilot flies the <b>command bars</b>; shown on EADI/ADI.</li>\n<li><b>EFIS</b> core purpose = electronic <b>ADI + HSI</b>; <b>glass cockpit</b> = flat panels replace gauges; <b>ECAM</b> auto-monitors and alerts.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Most autopilot systems consist of how many basic components?", "options": ["Four", "Three", "Six", "Two"], "correct": 0, "short": "Four.", "expl": "Sensing, computing, output, and command elements.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The autopilot sensing elements include the attitude and directional gyros, the turn coordinator, and a(n):", "options": ["Servo", "Command bar", "Altitude control", "Flight controller"], "correct": 2, "short": "Altitude control.", "expl": "These elements sense what the aircraft is doing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The autopilot output elements are the:", "options": ["Servos that actuate the flight control surfaces", "Attitude indicator", "Gyros", "Command unit"], "correct": 0, "short": "Servos.", "expl": "Output servos move the flight controls.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The command element (flight controller) of an autopilot is the:", "options": ["Servo motor", "Amplifier only", "Set of gyros", "Human interface that lets the pilot tell the autopilot what to do"], "correct": 3, "short": "The human interface (flight controller).", "expl": "The pilot commands the autopilot through it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The function of the autopilot computing element is to:", "options": ["Interpret sensing data, integrate commands/nav input, and signal the output elements", "Sense the rate of turn", "Move the control surfaces directly", "Display the attitude to the pilot"], "correct": 0, "short": "Interpret data and signal the outputs.", "expl": "The computer/amplifier is the brain of the autopilot.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The purpose of the feedback (follow-up) element is to:", "options": ["Sense altitude", "Progressively reduce the error signal so continuous over-correction does not occur", "Increase the error signal", "Power the servos"], "correct": 1, "short": "Reduce the error signal (prevent over-correction).", "expl": "Follow-up transducers keep the autopilot from hunting.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "A yaw damper is used to correct an aircraft's tendency to:", "options": ["Pitch up and down", "Roll into a spiral", "Lose altitude", "Oscillate around its vertical axis (Dutch roll) on a fixed heading"], "correct": 3, "short": "Oscillate about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).", "expl": "The yaw damper automatically counters that yaw oscillation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "A yaw damper:", "options": ["Only works on helicopters", "Replaces the rudder", "Can be part of the autopilot or a completely independent unit", "Must always be part of the autopilot"], "correct": 2, "short": "Can be part of the autopilot or independent.", "expl": "Either arrangement is used.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The simplest helicopter stability augmentation system (SAS) is a force-trim system that uses:", "options": ["A magnetic clutch and springs to hold the cyclic control", "A GPS receiver", "Only hydraulic servos", "A second engine"], "correct": 0, "short": "A magnetic clutch and springs.", "expl": "Force trim holds the cyclic where it was released.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "A helicopter SAS reduces pilot workload and:", "options": ["Replaces the main rotor", "Can be overridden or disconnected by the pilot at any time", "Only works on the ground", "Cannot be disconnected"], "correct": 1, "short": "Can be overridden/disconnected by the pilot.", "expl": "The pilot retains authority over the SAS.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "An autopilot with many features and related systems integrated into a single system is called a(n):", "options": ["Flight director", "Transponder", "Yaw damper", "Automatic Flight Control System (AFCS)"], "correct": 3, "short": "Automatic Flight Control System (AFCS).", "expl": "AFCS integrates autopilot + AHRS + nav + intelligent servos.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "A flight director is essentially:", "options": ["A weather radar", "An autopilot with extra servos", "An autopilot system without the servos — the pilot flies the command bars", "A transponder with altitude encoding"], "correct": 2, "short": "An autopilot without servos (pilot flies command bars).", "expl": "Same sensing/computing, but the pilot manipulates the controls.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The flight director's command bar is displayed on the:", "options": ["Attitude indicator (ADI/EADI)", "Transponder", "DME readout", "Fuel gauge"], "correct": 0, "short": "The attitude indicator (ADI/EADI).", "expl": "The command bar shows required attitude change.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The core purpose of an Electronic Flight Instrument System (EFIS) is the electronic depiction of:", "options": ["Engine oil temperature and fuel flow", "The transponder code", "ADI and HSI information", "Radio frequencies only"], "correct": 2, "short": "ADI and HSI information.", "expl": "EFIS electronically presents attitude (ADI) and horizontal situation (HSI).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The term 'glass cockpit' refers to:", "options": ["Windshields made of tempered glass", "The use of flat-panel display screens with computer-produced images replacing mechanical gauges", "Anti-ice heated windows", "A pressurized cabin"], "correct": 1, "short": "Flat-panel displays replacing mechanical gauges.", "expl": "Computer images replace individual analog instruments.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The Electronic Centralized Aircraft Monitor (ECAM) automatically:", "options": ["Flies the aircraft", "Regulates the generators", "Performs monitoring duties and alerts the pilot with aural and visual cues when a failure occurs", "Transmits the ELT signal"], "correct": 2, "short": "Monitors and alerts the pilot on failures.", "expl": "ECAM watches systems and cues the pilot on problems.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}]}]}, {"n": 14, "day": "Unit 14", "title": "Integrated Flight Deck: Autopilot, ECAM/EICAS, FMS & Alerting", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.10 (pp. 10-58 to 10-74) · autopilot channels/altitude hold · ECAM · EICAS · FMS · annunciators · aural warnings", "sections": [{"id": "b6m14s1", "title": "How autopilots work: error correction, channels & altitude hold", "lesson": "\n<p>Module 12 named the <b>four autopilot components</b> (sensing, computing,\noutput/servos, command). This section teaches <b>how the autopilot actually thinks</b>\n— the part written exams love and the class deck skipped.</p>\n\n<h3>The whole idea: error correction</h3>\n<p>The <b>basis for autopilot operation is error correction</b> (p. 10-61). You tell\nthe autopilot the flight condition you want (a heading, an attitude, an altitude).\nWhenever the aircraft <b>fails to meet that selected condition, an \"error\" has\noccurred</b>. The autopilot automatically moves the controls to <b>cancel the error</b>\nand put the aircraft back where you asked.</p>\n\n<h3>Two ways to do it: position-based vs. rate-based</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Position-based autopilot</b> — <b>memorizes the desired attitude</b> and moves\nthe control surfaces so the aircraft <b>returns to that exact attitude</b> (p. 10-61).</li>\n<li><b>Rate-based autopilot</b> — works from <b>how fast the aircraft is moving</b>\n(rate of change) and moves the surfaces to <b>counter that rate</b> (p. 10-61). Rate\nsignals come from a <b>rate gyro</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Single-axis autopilot: the wing leveler</h3>\n<p>The simplest autopilot is a <b>single-axis wing leveler</b> found on small aircraft.\nIt is <b>vacuum-operated</b>, controls <b>only the ailerons</b>, and uses the\naircraft's <b>turn coordinator as its sensing element</b>. Vacuum is metered to a cable\nservo that moves the ailerons when the turn coordinator senses roll (p. 10-59). A\ntwo-axis system adds pitch (elevator); a three-axis system adds yaw (rudder).</p>\n\n<h3>Closed loop: the follow-up signal</h3>\n<p>Every channel is a <b>closed-loop system</b>. As a servo drives a surface, a\n<b>follow-up (feedback) signal</b> builds up that <b>opposes the input signal</b>. When\nthe two signals are <b>equal in magnitude, the servo stops moving</b> (p. 10-62). This\nis what keeps the autopilot from continuously over-correcting (hunting).</p>\n\n<h3>The three control channels</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Rudder channel</b> — gets <b>two</b> signals: a <b>course signal</b> from the\ncompass system (proportional to how far off the preset heading you are) and a\n<b>rate signal</b> from the turn-and-bank (rate) gyro (proportional to the rate of\nturn). They are combined and amplified, then sent to the rudder servo (p. 10-62).</li>\n<li><b>Aileron channel</b> — gets its signal from a transmitter in the <b>gyro horizon\n(attitude) indicator</b>; corrects roll about the <b>longitudinal axis</b> (p. 10-62).</li>\n<li><b>Elevator channel</b> — like the aileron channel, but detects and corrects\n<b>pitch</b> changes (p. 10-62).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Altitude hold vs. altitude select</h3>\n<p>A remotely mounted unit with an <b>altitude pressure diaphragm</b> feeds the pitch\n(elevator) servos (p. 10-62):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Altitude hold</b> — generates error signals whenever the aircraft <b>drifts off\na preselected altitude</b>, and the pitch servos correct back to it.</li>\n<li><b>Altitude select</b> — <b>continuously commands</b> the pitch servos until a\n<b>preselected altitude is reached</b>, then hands off to altitude hold to maintain it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Yaw damper (why it exists)</h3>\n<p>Many aircraft <b>oscillate about the vertical (yaw) axis</b> on a fixed heading\n(Dutch roll), needing near-continuous rudder. A <b>yaw damper</b> corrects this\nautomatically, taking its error signals from the <b>turn-coordinator rate gyro</b>. It\ncan be part of the autopilot or a completely independent unit (p. 10-63).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Autopilot basis = <b>error correction</b>: cancel any deviation from the selected condition.</li>\n<li><b>Position-based</b> returns to a memorized attitude; <b>rate-based</b> counters the rate of change (rate gyro).</li>\n<li>Simplest autopilot = <b>single-axis wing leveler</b>: vacuum-operated, ailerons only, <b>turn coordinator</b> = sensing element.</li>\n<li><b>Follow-up (feedback)</b> opposes the input; when they're <b>equal, the servo stops</b> — a closed loop, no over-correction.</li>\n<li>Rudder channel = <b>course signal (compass) + rate signal (rate gyro)</b>; aileron = <b>gyro horizon</b>; elevator = <b>pitch</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Altitude hold</b> keeps a current altitude; <b>altitude select</b> drives to a chosen altitude then holds it.</li>\n<li><b>Yaw damper</b> stops yaw-axis oscillation (Dutch roll); signal from the <b>turn coordinator</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The fundamental basis for autopilot system operation is:", "options": ["Error correction — canceling any deviation from the selected flight condition", "Amplifying the pilot's control forces", "Storing flight plans in a database", "Converting AC power to DC for the servos"], "correct": 0, "short": "Error correction.", "expl": "The autopilot moves the controls to cancel any error between the aircraft's actual and selected condition.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "A position-based autopilot corrects errors by:", "options": ["Ignoring small deviations", "Counting cycles per second", "Memorizing the desired attitude and returning the aircraft to it", "Measuring only the rate of turn"], "correct": 2, "short": "Returns to a memorized attitude.", "expl": "It remembers the desired attitude and drives the surfaces back to it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "A rate-based autopilot moves the control surfaces based on:", "options": ["The memorized attitude only", "The radio frequency in use", "The cabin altitude", "The aircraft's rate of movement, sensed by a rate gyro"], "correct": 3, "short": "Rate of movement (rate gyro).", "expl": "It counters the rate of change rather than returning to a fixed position.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The simplest single-axis autopilot found on small aircraft is a:", "options": ["Full three-axis digital AFCS", "Wing leveler that is vacuum-operated and controls only the ailerons", "Yaw damper controlling only the rudder", "Flight management computer"], "correct": 1, "short": "Vacuum-operated wing leveler (ailerons only).", "expl": "Its sensing element is the turn coordinator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The sensing element of a single-axis wing leveler is the:", "options": ["Magnetic compass", "Turn coordinator", "Altimeter", "Fuel-flow transmitter"], "correct": 1, "short": "Turn coordinator.", "expl": "Vacuum is metered to a cable servo when the turn coordinator senses roll.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "In an autopilot channel, the follow-up (feedback) signal:", "options": ["Opposes the input signal, stopping the servo when the two are equal", "Powers the annunciator lights", "Adds to the input signal to speed up the servo", "Selects the radio frequency"], "correct": 0, "short": "Opposes the input; servo stops when equal.", "expl": "This closed loop prevents continuous over-correction (hunting).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "An autopilot channel that uses a follow-up signal to close the loop is called a:", "options": ["Open-loop system", "Rate-of-climb system", "Static system", "Closed-loop system"], "correct": 3, "short": "Closed-loop system.", "expl": "Feedback completes the loop between input and output.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The rudder channel of an analog autopilot receives two signals: a rate signal from the rate gyro and a course signal from the:", "options": ["Fuel quantity system", "Weather radar", "Landing gear squat switch", "Compass system"], "correct": 3, "short": "Compass system.", "expl": "The course signal is proportional to how far off the preset heading the aircraft is.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The aileron channel of an autopilot gets its input signal from the:", "options": ["DME receiver", "Altimeter diaphragm", "Turn-and-bank rate gyro", "Gyro horizon (attitude) indicator"], "correct": 3, "short": "Gyro horizon (attitude indicator).", "expl": "It corrects roll about the longitudinal axis.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The autopilot's altitude HOLD function:", "options": ["Controls the ailerons only", "Sets the transponder code", "Generates error signals whenever the aircraft drifts off a preselected altitude", "Drives the aircraft to a new chosen altitude"], "correct": 2, "short": "Corrects drift off the current preselected altitude.", "expl": "The pitch servos correct back to the held altitude.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The autopilot's altitude SELECT function:", "options": ["Damps yaw oscillations", "Levels the wings", "Continuously commands the pitch servos until a preselected altitude is reached", "Holds the current altitude indefinitely"], "correct": 2, "short": "Drives to a chosen altitude, then hands off to hold.", "expl": "Signals go to the pitch servos until the selected altitude is captured.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "Altitude hold and altitude select both act through the aircraft's:", "options": ["Nose-wheel steering", "Rudder servo only", "Aileron servos only", "Pitch (elevator) servos"], "correct": 3, "short": "Pitch (elevator) servos.", "expl": "An altitude pressure diaphragm unit feeds the pitch channel.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "A yaw damper receives its error signals from the:", "options": ["Turn-coordinator rate gyro", "Compass card", "Fuel-flow sensor", "Altimeter"], "correct": 0, "short": "Turn-coordinator rate gyro.", "expl": "It automatically counters oscillation about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "A three-axis autopilot controls the ailerons, elevator, and:", "options": ["Spoilers only", "Landing gear", "Rudder", "Flaps"], "correct": 2, "short": "Rudder.", "expl": "Single-axis = ailerons; two-axis adds elevator (pitch); three-axis adds rudder (yaw).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}]}, {"id": "b6m14s2", "title": "ECAM, EICAS & the Flight Management System (FMS)", "lesson": "\n<p>On transport aircraft the crew can't watch dozens of gauges and fly at the same\ntime. <b>Centralized monitoring systems</b> do the watching automatically and only\nspeak up when something needs the pilot. Two families exist: <b>ECAM</b> (Airbus\nlineage) and <b>EICAS</b> (Boeing lineage). Plus the <b>FMS</b> — the master computer\nthat runs the whole flight.</p>\n\n<h3>ECAM — Electronic Centralized Aircraft Monitor</h3>\n<p><b>ECAM automatically performs the pilot's monitoring duties</b> (p. 10-68). When a\nproblem or failure is detected, the primary display plus an <b>aural and visual cue</b>\nalerts the pilot, and it <b>displays the corrective action to take</b>. This frees the\npilot to fly until a problem occurs.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Two monitors.</b> The <b>upper/left monitor</b> shows <b>system status and any\nwarnings with corrective actions in checklist format</b>; the <b>lower/right monitor</b>\nshows a <b>pictorial diagram (synoptic)</b> of the system being referred to (p. 10-68).\nEarly CRTs; newer aircraft use LCDs.</li>\n<li><b>Flight warning computers.</b> Aircraft inputs feed <b>two flight warning\ncomputers</b>; analog inputs pass through a <b>system data analog converter</b> first.\nThe computers process the data and drive the <b>signal generators</b> that light the\nmonitors (p. 10-68).</li>\n<li><b>Four modes:</b> <b>flight phase</b> (normal — preflight, takeoff, climb, cruise,\ndescent, approach, post-landing), <b>advisory</b>, <b>failure-related</b>, and\n<b>manual</b> (p. 10-68). <b>Failure-related mode takes precedence over all other modes</b>,\nregardless of what is selected when the failure happens.</li>\n<li><b>Color coding</b> draws attention in order of importance; the flight warning\ncomputers <b>self-test on startup</b> and a maintenance panel allows further testing.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>EICAS — Engine Indicating & Crew Alerting System</h3>\n<p><b>EICAS does the same job as ECAM</b> but is the Boeing-lineage system, and\n<b>all EICAS displays engine as well as airframe parameters</b> (p. 10-70).</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Two monitors, two computers</b>, with a <b>display select panel</b>. Both monitors\nget information from the <b>same computer</b>; the <b>second computer is a standby</b>\n(p. 10-71).</li>\n<li><b>Primary (top) monitor</b> = full-time <b>primary engine parameters (EPR, N1, EGT)</b>\nplus advisories and warnings. <b>Secondary (bottom) monitor</b> = <b>secondary engine\nparameters and nonengine system status</b>, and is used for <b>maintenance diagnosis on\nthe ground</b> (p. 10-71).</li>\n<li><b>Color coding and message prioritizing</b> are used; caution/warning lights and\n<b>aural tones</b> are incorporated.</li>\n<li><b>Event record:</b> EICAS <b>automatically records the parameters of a failure\nevent</b> for later review; the pilot can also press an <b>event record button</b> to\ncapture a suspected problem for maintenance.</li>\n<li><b>BITE (built-in test equipment):</b> EICAS monitors itself; a <b>maintenance panel</b>\n(for technicians only, used on the ground) displays system information for analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>FMS — Flight Management System</h3>\n<p>The <b>FMS is the highest level of automated flight system</b> — a <b>master computer\nsystem with control over all other systems</b> (p. 10-72). It coordinates flight,\nengine, and airframe parameters either automatically or by instructing the pilot,\ncovering the whole flight from preflight planning to parking.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>FMC (Flight Management Computer)</b> — the main component. It communicates over\n<b>data buses</b> with the <b>EICAS/ECAM, ADC, thrust management computer (autothrottle),\nEFIS symbol generators, AFCS, inertial reference system, collision-avoidance system, and\nall radio nav aids</b> (p. 10-72).</li>\n<li><b>CDU (Control Display Unit)</b> — the crew interface, on the center pedestal. It\nhas a <b>full alphanumeric keypad, a CRT/LCD screen, status/condition annunciators, and\nfunction keys</b> (p. 10-72).</li>\n<li><b>Dual FMCs</b> operate independently (pilot's and copilot's units) but crosstalk\nover the data buses; if one FMC or CDU fails, the remaining units keep <b>full control\nwithout compromise</b>.</li>\n<li>The FMC stores <b>hundreds of flight plans</b> and a <b>navigation database</b>;\nintegrated with NAV/COM it can <b>change radio frequencies as the flight plan is enacted</b>,\nrun lean/fuel-conserving operations, and factor weather and traffic — asking the crew\nfor a decision through the CDU.</li>\n<li><b>BITE:</b> the FMC continuously monitors itself; maintenance can retrieve fault\nmessages and open <b>maintenance pages that call out the faulty line-replaceable units\n(LRUs)</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>ECAM</b> and <b>EICAS</b> both <b>automatically monitor systems</b> and alert with <b>aural + visual</b> cues; <b>EICAS always shows engine parameters</b>.</li>\n<li>ECAM: <b>2 monitors</b> (status/checklist + synoptic diagram), <b>2 flight warning computers</b>, <b>4 modes</b>; <b>failure mode overrides all</b>.</li>\n<li>EICAS: <b>2 monitors / 2 computers</b> (1 active, 1 standby); <b>top = primary engine (EPR, N1, EGT)</b>, <b>bottom = secondary + status + ground maintenance</b>; <b>auto event record</b>.</li>\n<li><b>FMS = master computer</b>; the <b>FMC</b> talks to everything over data buses; crew interface = <b>CDU</b>; <b>dual FMCs</b>, one can fail with no compromise.</li>\n<li>Monitoring systems (ECAM/EICAS/FMS) all use <b>BITE</b> and <b>maintenance pages that name faulty LRUs</b>.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The basic concept behind a centralized monitoring system such as ECAM is:", "options": ["Replacing all radio navigation", "Charging the aircraft battery", "Automatic performance of the pilot's system-monitoring duties, alerting only when a problem occurs", "Flying the aircraft without any pilot"], "correct": 2, "short": "Automatic monitoring; alerts on a problem.", "expl": "This frees the pilot to fly until something needs attention.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "On a two-monitor ECAM system, the upper/left monitor primarily displays:", "options": ["A pictorial synoptic diagram only", "Primary engine parameters only", "The weather radar picture", "System status and warnings with corrective actions in checklist format"], "correct": 3, "short": "Status/warnings + corrective actions (checklist).", "expl": "The lower/right monitor shows the matching system diagram.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "The lower/right monitor of an ECAM system typically shows:", "options": ["The fuel-quantity totalizer only", "The autopilot command bars", "The transponder code", "A pictorial (synoptic) diagram of the system referred to on the primary monitor"], "correct": 3, "short": "A pictorial system diagram (synoptic).", "expl": "It accompanies the checklist shown on the primary monitor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "ECAM aircraft inputs are processed by:", "options": ["A single flight warning computer with no redundancy", "The transponder", "The battery charger", "Two flight warning computers, with analog inputs first passing through a data analog converter"], "correct": 3, "short": "Two flight warning computers.", "expl": "They drive the signal generators that light the monitors.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "Among the four ECAM modes (flight phase, advisory, failure-related, manual), the mode that takes precedence over all others is:", "options": ["Flight phase mode", "Manual mode", "Failure-related mode", "Advisory mode"], "correct": 2, "short": "Failure-related mode.", "expl": "It overrides whatever mode is selected when a failure occurs.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "A key difference of EICAS compared with ECAM is that EICAS:", "options": ["Always displays engine parameters as well as airframe parameters", "Uses no computers", "Cannot display any engine data", "Is only used on light single-engine aircraft"], "correct": 0, "short": "EICAS always shows engine parameters.", "expl": "All EICAS displays engine plus airframe parameters.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "An EICAS uses a two-monitor, two-computer arrangement in which:", "options": ["Both monitors get information from the same computer, and the second computer is a standby", "There is only one computer and no backup", "Each monitor has its own dedicated primary computer", "The computers only work on the ground"], "correct": 0, "short": "One active computer feeds both; the other is standby.", "expl": "This gives redundancy if the active computer fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "On an EICAS, the primary (top) monitor shows full-time primary engine parameters, namely:", "options": ["Cabin temperature and door status only", "Only the aircraft clock", "Radio frequencies and transponder code", "EPR, N1, and EGT (plus advisories and warnings)"], "correct": 3, "short": "EPR, N1, EGT.", "expl": "Secondary parameters and system status go on the lower monitor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "The lower (secondary) EICAS monitor is also used for:", "options": ["Transmitting the ELT signal", "Maintenance diagnosis when the aircraft is on the ground", "Powering the servos", "Displaying the primary engine parameters"], "correct": 1, "short": "Ground maintenance diagnosis.", "expl": "It shows secondary engine parameters and nonengine system status.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "A distinctive EICAS feature is that it automatically:", "options": ["Recharges the battery", "Flies the approach", "Files the FAA Form 337", "Records the parameters of a failure event for later review by maintenance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Auto-records failure-event parameters.", "expl": "The pilot can also press an event-record button for a suspected problem.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "The Flight Management System (FMS) is best described as:", "options": ["A backup magnetic compass", "A type of static inverter", "A master computer system that has control over all other systems", "A single annunciator light"], "correct": 2, "short": "The master computer system.", "expl": "It coordinates flight, engine, and airframe parameters for the whole flight.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "The main component of an FMS is the:", "options": ["Turn coordinator", "Transformer rectifier unit", "Flight management computer (FMC)", "Carbon-pile regulator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Flight management computer (FMC).", "expl": "It communicates with the EICAS/ECAM, ADC, AFCS, IRS, nav aids, and more over data buses.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "The crew interfaces with the FMS through the:", "options": ["Master caution light", "Control display unit (CDU) — an alphanumeric keypad with a display screen", "Pitot tube", "Rudder pedals"], "correct": 1, "short": "Control display unit (CDU).", "expl": "It is normally on the center pedestal of the flight deck.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "A typical FMS uses two FMCs so that if one FMC or CDU fails:", "options": ["The autopilot must be shut off permanently", "The battery is disconnected", "The remaining units continue with full control and no system compromise", "The aircraft loses all navigation"], "correct": 2, "short": "Remaining units keep full control.", "expl": "The dual units operate independently but crosstalk over data buses.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "Monitoring and management systems (EICAS, ECAM, FMS) all include BITE, which lets maintenance:", "options": ["Retrieve fault messages and open maintenance pages that name the faulty LRUs", "Refuel the aircraft automatically", "Adjust the flight controls in flight", "Change the annunciator lens colors"], "correct": 0, "short": "Retrieve faults and identify faulty LRUs.", "expl": "BITE = built-in test equipment; the system monitors itself.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}]}, {"id": "b6m14s3", "title": "Warnings & cautions: annunciators, master caution & aural warnings", "lesson": "\n<p>Whatever the display technology, the pilot must be told <b>what is wrong and how\nbadly</b>. That is the job of <b>warnings and cautions</b>: annunciator lights, master\ncaution/warning circuits, and aural (sound) alerts.</p>\n\n<h3>Annunciator panels & the standard colors</h3>\n<p>An <b>annunciator panel</b> is a panel of <b>warning lights in plain sight of the\npilot</b>, each labeled with the name of the system it represents and covered with a\ncolored lens (p. 10-73). Annunciators serve two purposes: to <b>display current\nconditions</b> and to <b>notify of unsatisfactory conditions</b>. Standard colors give\nquick-reference meaning:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Green</b> = a <b>satisfactory</b> (normal) condition.</li>\n<li><b>Yellow/amber</b> = <b>caution</b> — a serious condition that <b>requires further\nmonitoring</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Red</b> = an <b>unsatisfactory</b> condition needing <b>immediate attention or\naction</b>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On complex aircraft a <b>centralized warning system</b> gathers critical messages for\nmany systems into a single organized panel. On glass-cockpit aircraft, <b>EFIS itself\nannunciates advisory and warning messages</b>, usually on the primary display unit.</p>\n\n<h3>Master caution / master warning</h3>\n<p><b>Master caution lights</b> draw the crew's attention to a critical situation in\naddition to the specific annunciator that names the problem (p. 10-73). They are\n<b>centrally wired</b> and illuminate whenever <b>any</b> participating system needs\nattention. Key logic:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The pilot may <b>cancel (reset) the master caution</b>, <b>but the individual\nsystem annunciator stays lit until the fault is actually rectified</b>.</li>\n<li>Cancelling <b>re-arms the master caution</b> so it can warn of the <b>next</b> fault\neven before the first one is fixed.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Press-to-test</h3>\n<p><b>Press-to-test</b> is provided for the whole annunciator system: it\n<b>energizes all warning circuitry and lights</b> to confirm readiness. This test often\nreveals a <b>burned-out bulb</b> that must be replaced (p. 10-73).</p>\n\n<h3>Aural warning systems</h3>\n<p><b>Aural warning systems work together with the illuminated annunciators</b>,\n<b>audibly</b> informing the pilot with <b>various tones and phrases</b> (p. 10-74).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Classic example: a retractable-gear aircraft sounds a <b>bell (horn) if the throttle\nis retarded and the landing gear is not down and locked</b>.</li>\n<li>A transport-category aural system alerts for <b>abnormal takeoff, landing,\npressurization, Mach/airspeed conditions, an engine or wheel-well fire, crew-call, and\ncollision-avoidance recommendations</b>, among others.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3>Clocks</h3>\n<p>An <b>FAA-approved time indicator</b> (clock or chronometer) is <b>required in the\nflight deck of IFR-certified aircraft</b> (p. 10-74).</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Annunciator colors: <b>green = satisfactory</b>, <b>yellow/amber = caution (monitor)</b>, <b>red = unsatisfactory (act now)</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Master caution</b> is <b>centrally wired</b>; pilot can <b>cancel it, but the specific annunciator stays lit until the fault is fixed</b>; cancelling re-arms it for the next fault.</li>\n<li><b>Press-to-test</b> energizes all warning lights to confirm readiness — often finds a <b>burned-out bulb</b>.</li>\n<li><b>Aural warnings</b> use tones/phrases with the lights; gear-up-with-throttle-retarded = <b>bell/horn</b>.</li>\n<li><b>IFR-certified aircraft require an FAA-approved clock</b> in the flight deck.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "An annunciator panel is:", "options": ["The autopilot's servo unit", "The aircraft's static discharge system", "A panel of labeled warning lights in plain sight of the pilot, covered with colored lenses", "A device that converts AC to DC"], "correct": 2, "short": "A panel of labeled colored warning lights.", "expl": "It displays current conditions and notifies of unsatisfactory ones.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "On an annunciator panel, the color GREEN indicates:", "options": ["A caution requiring further monitoring", "A failed light bulb", "A satisfactory (normal) condition", "An unsatisfactory condition needing immediate action"], "correct": 2, "short": "Satisfactory / normal.", "expl": "Green is the reassuring color.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "On an annunciator panel, YELLOW (amber) is used to indicate:", "options": ["The aircraft is on the ground", "A caution — a serious condition that requires further monitoring", "A completely normal condition", "An immediate emergency requiring action now"], "correct": 1, "short": "Caution — monitor further.", "expl": "Amber means watch it; it is between green and red.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "On an annunciator panel, RED indicates:", "options": ["A condition that only needs monitoring", "A satisfactory condition", "An unsatisfactory condition needing immediate attention or action", "A dimming test"], "correct": 2, "short": "Unsatisfactory — act immediately.", "expl": "Red is the most urgent color.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "A master caution light is:", "options": ["Wired only to the fuel system", "A green normal-status light", "Part of the pitot-static system", "Centrally wired and illuminates whenever any participating system needs attention"], "correct": 3, "short": "Centrally wired attention-getter.", "expl": "It works alongside the specific annunciator that names the problem.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "When a pilot cancels (resets) an illuminated master caution light:", "options": ["The fault is automatically repaired", "The specific system annunciator stays lit until the fault is rectified, but the master caution re-arms for the next fault", "All annunciator lights go out permanently", "The autopilot disengages"], "correct": 1, "short": "Individual annunciator stays lit; master re-arms.", "expl": "Cancelling only resets the master, not the underlying condition.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "The purpose of cancelling the master caution (while the specific light stays on) is to:", "options": ["Reset the transponder", "Turn off the affected system", "Re-arm the master caution so it can warn of a subsequent fault before the first is fixed", "Silence all future warnings for the flight"], "correct": 2, "short": "Re-arm it for the next fault.", "expl": "It ensures a new problem is still announced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "The 'press-to-test' function of an annunciator system:", "options": ["Records the flight data", "Energizes all warning circuitry and lights to confirm readiness", "Flies a test approach", "Drains the battery for testing"], "correct": 1, "short": "Energizes all warning lights to confirm readiness.", "expl": "It often exposes a burned-out bulb that needs replacing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "A press-to-test check most commonly reveals the need to:", "options": ["Recalibrate the altimeter", "Replace the pitot tube", "Overhaul the engine", "Replace a burned-out annunciator light bulb"], "correct": 3, "short": "Replace a burned-out bulb.", "expl": "Confirming the lights work is the main point of the test.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "Aircraft aural warning systems:", "options": ["Only operate on the ground", "Replace the annunciator lights entirely", "Are used only for radio communication", "Work in conjunction with the illuminated annunciators, using tones and phrases to alert the crew"], "correct": 3, "short": "Audible tones/phrases alongside the lights.", "expl": "They audibly inform the pilot of a situation requiring attention.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "In a retractable-gear aircraft, the aural warning system sounds a bell/horn when:", "options": ["The gear is safely down and locked", "The throttle is retarded and the landing gear is not down and locked", "The autopilot is engaged", "The transponder is set to standby"], "correct": 1, "short": "Throttle retarded + gear not down/locked.", "expl": "It warns of an unsafe landing configuration.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "Which is an example of a condition a transport-category aural warning system alerts for?", "options": ["A normal cruise altitude", "An engine or wheel-well fire", "A properly closed cabin door", "A satisfactory generator load"], "correct": 1, "short": "Engine/wheel-well fire (among others).", "expl": "It also covers abnormal takeoff/landing, pressurization, Mach, crew-call, and collision advisories.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "On a glass-cockpit aircraft, advisory and warning messages are often annunciated:", "options": ["By the EFIS itself, usually on the primary display unit", "By the transponder", "By the static wicks", "Only by a separate mechanical gauge"], "correct": 0, "short": "By EFIS on the primary display.", "expl": "EFIS provides message annunciation as part of its monitoring capability.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "An FAA-approved time indicator (clock/chronometer) in the flight deck is required for:", "options": ["IFR-certified aircraft", "Only experimental aircraft", "Aircraft with no electrical system", "Gliders only"], "correct": 0, "short": "IFR-certified aircraft.", "expl": "A clock is required equipment for IFR certification.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}]}]}, {"n": 15, "day": "Unit 15", "title": "Large-Aircraft Power Conversion & Paralleling", "ref": "FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9 (pp. 9-36 to 9-59, 9-99) · inverters · transformer rectifier units (TRU) · trip-free breakers · paralleling/load sharing · GCU/BPCU/BTB", "sections": [{"id": "b6m15s1", "title": "Power conversion: inverters, TRUs & trip-free protection", "lesson": "\n<p>Big aircraft generate <b>AC</b> (it's lighter to distribute and easy to change\nvoltage). But the battery and many loads still need <b>DC</b>, and a few instruments\nneed AC even on a DC ship. So aircraft carry <b>power-conversion devices</b> that turn\none kind of power into the other. Know these three cold — the written exam mixes them\nup on purpose.</p>\n\n<h3>Inverter: DC &rarr; AC</h3>\n<p>An <b>inverter changes DC into AC</b> (p. 9-36). On a DC aircraft it supplies the AC\nthat certain instruments and equipment need; common outputs are <b>26-volt AC</b> and\n<b>115-volt AC</b>.</p>\n<figure class=\"fig narrow\"><img src=\"img/slide148_1.jpeg\" alt=\"inverter\"><figcaption>An inverter changes DC into AC (typically 26 V AC or 115 V AC).</figcaption></figure>\n<p>On a large <b>all-AC</b> aircraft, a <b>static inverter</b> is fed from the <b>hot\nbattery bus</b> and supplies <b>essential AC loads if all AC generators fail</b> — the\nlast-ditch AC source (p. 9-44). (\"Static\" means solid-state, no rotating parts.)</p>\n\n<h3>Transformer Rectifier Unit (TRU): AC &rarr; DC</h3>\n<p>The <b>transformer rectifier unit (TRU, or TR)</b> is the opposite of an inverter:\nit <b>changes AC into DC</b> (p. 9-84). On a large AC aircraft it is how the DC buses and\nthe battery get their power. Inside a TRU (p. 9-84):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A <b>transformer steps the voltage down</b> — for example from <b>115-volt AC to\n26-volt AC</b>.</li>\n<li>A <b>rectifier then changes that 26-volt AC into 26-volt DC</b>.</li>\n<li>The <b>26-volt DC output is compatible with the aircraft battery</b>, so the TRU can\nfeed the DC buses and keep the battery charged.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Because <b>DC is not phase-sensitive</b>, the DC buses are normally <b>connected\ntogether</b> in service; if a bus problem occurs, the <b>bus power control unit (BPCU)</b>\ncan <b>isolate one or more DC buses</b> to keep distribution correct.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><span class=\"bt\">Don't confuse them</span>\n<b>Inverter</b> = DC&rarr;AC. &nbsp; <b>TRU/rectifier</b> = AC&rarr;DC. &nbsp;\n<b>Transformer</b> = changes AC voltage only (no AC/DC conversion). &nbsp;\n<b>Converter</b> = a general term for a device that changes one form of power to another.</div>\n\n<h3>Trip-free circuit breakers (a protection refinement)</h3>\n<p>You already know a circuit breaker is a <b>resettable</b> automatic switch that\nprotects a circuit from overload/short. One extra rule the exam checks: <b>all resettable\ncircuit breakers must open the circuit regardless of the position of the operating\ncontrol (handle) when a fault exists</b>. Breakers built this way are called\n<b>trip-free</b> — you <b>cannot hold a trip-free breaker closed</b> against a fault\n(p. 9-99). <b>Automatic-reset circuit breakers</b> reset themselves and <b>must NOT be\nused as circuit-protection devices in aircraft</b>. Always find and clear the fault\nbefore resetting a tripped breaker.</p>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Inverter = DC&rarr;AC</b> (26 V / 115 V AC). A <b>static inverter</b> off the <b>hot battery bus</b> feeds essential AC if all AC generators fail.</li>\n<li><b>TRU (transformer rectifier unit) = AC&rarr;DC</b>: transformer steps 115 V AC down to ~26 V AC, rectifier makes 26 V DC (battery-compatible).</li>\n<li><b>DC is not phase-sensitive</b>, so DC buses are tied together; the <b>BPCU</b> can isolate a faulty DC bus.</li>\n<li>Transformer = AC voltage change only; converter = general power-form changer.</li>\n<li><b>Trip-free</b> breaker <b>opens regardless of handle position</b> during a fault; <b>automatic-reset breakers are prohibited</b> as aircraft protection.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "An inverter is a device that changes:", "options": ["AC voltage from high to low only", "DC into AC", "DC voltage from high to low only", "AC into DC"], "correct": 1, "short": "DC into AC.", "expl": "Typical light-aircraft inverter outputs are 26 V AC and 115 V AC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "A static inverter on a large all-AC aircraft is fed from the hot battery bus and is used to:", "options": ["Charge the battery from AC power", "Supply essential AC loads if all AC generators fail", "Increase the AC frequency to 800 Hz", "Replace the transformer rectifier unit"], "correct": 1, "short": "Backup essential AC if all AC generators fail.", "expl": "'Static' means solid-state, no rotating parts.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "A transformer rectifier unit (TRU) changes:", "options": ["AC into DC", "AC frequency only", "DC into AC", "DC into a higher DC voltage only"], "correct": 0, "short": "AC into DC.", "expl": "It is the opposite of an inverter.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "Inside a TRU, the two functional parts are a:", "options": ["Transformer to step the voltage down and a rectifier to change AC to DC", "Carbon pile and a relay", "Gyro and a servo", "Battery and an alternator"], "correct": 0, "short": "Step-down transformer + rectifier.", "expl": "For example, 115 V AC steps down to 26 V AC, then rectifies to 26 V DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "A TRU's typical DC output is about 26 volts DC because that output must be:", "options": ["Compatible with the aircraft battery so it can feed DC buses and charge the battery", "High enough to run the AC generators", "Used only for the landing lights", "The same as the 115 V AC input"], "correct": 0, "short": "Battery-compatible 26 V DC.", "expl": "It powers the DC buses and keeps the battery charged.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "On a large AC aircraft, the DC buses are normally connected together during operation because:", "options": ["The battery requires it for cooling", "The inverter demands it", "DC power is not phase-sensitive", "DC power is phase-sensitive and must be synchronized"], "correct": 2, "short": "DC is not phase-sensitive.", "expl": "AC buses, by contrast, must be phase-managed before tying.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "If a DC bus problem occurs on a large aircraft, the unit that can isolate one or more DC buses is the:", "options": ["Transformer rectifier unit (TRU)", "Static inverter", "Bus power control unit (BPCU)", "Turn coordinator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Bus power control unit (BPCU).", "expl": "It reconfigures distribution to keep it correct.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "Which device changes AC voltage from one level to another WITHOUT converting between AC and DC?", "options": ["A transformer", "A rectifier", "An inverter", "A TRU"], "correct": 0, "short": "A transformer.", "expl": "Inverter = DC to AC; rectifier/TRU = AC to DC; transformer only changes AC voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "A circuit breaker that opens the circuit regardless of the position of its operating handle when a fault exists is called:", "options": ["Non-resettable", "Slow-blow", "Trip-free", "Automatic-reset"], "correct": 2, "short": "Trip-free.", "expl": "You cannot hold a trip-free breaker closed against a fault.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "All resettable circuit breakers in aircraft are required to be trip-free so that:", "options": ["The breaker resets itself automatically", "The breaker can never be reset", "The breaker melts open like a fuse", "The circuit opens on a fault even if the operating control is held closed"], "correct": 3, "short": "Opens on a fault even if held closed.", "expl": "This protects the circuit from a jammed or held handle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "Automatic-reset circuit breakers:", "options": ["Must NOT be used as circuit-protection devices in aircraft", "Are the preferred protection for all aircraft circuits", "Are required on the battery bus", "Are the same as trip-free breakers"], "correct": 0, "short": "Prohibited as aircraft circuit protection.", "expl": "They reset themselves, which could re-energize a faulted circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "Before resetting a circuit breaker that has tripped, the technician should:", "options": ["Replace it with a larger breaker", "Check the circuit and remove the fault", "Immediately reset it several times", "Tape the handle in the closed position"], "correct": 1, "short": "Find and clear the fault first.", "expl": "Resetting into an uncleared fault can cause damage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "A 'converter,' in general electrical terms, is a device that:", "options": ["Only opens a circuit on overload", "Only measures voltage", "Only stores electrical energy", "Changes one form of electrical power to another"], "correct": 3, "short": "Changes one form of power to another.", "expl": "Inverters and rectifiers/TRUs are specific kinds of converters.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}]}, {"id": "b6m15s2", "title": "Paralleling & load sharing on multi-engine aircraft", "lesson": "\n<p>Twin- and multi-engine aircraft carry <b>two or more generators/alternators</b>. If\none produced most of the load while the other loafed, the busy one would overheat and\nfail early. So the system must make them <b>share the electrical load equally</b>. That\nprocess is called <b>paralleling</b>.</p>\n\n<h3>What paralleling means</h3>\n<p><b>Paralleling is the process of equalizing the alternator (or generator) outputs so\nboth share the load equally</b> (p. 9-42). On <b>DC systems</b> found on light twins, it\nis fairly simple: if both alternators feed the <b>same load bus</b> and both produce the\n<b>same output voltage</b>, they automatically share the load equally.</p>\n\n<h3>The voltage-matching rule</h3>\n<p>The catch is that the two outputs must be held to <b>very nearly the same voltage</b>.\nThe paralleling system must keep both alternators within a <b>few tenths of a volt</b> of\neach other. On most twin-engine aircraft the system voltage runs about <b>26.5 to 28\nvolts DC</b> with the alternators operating (p. 9-42). If one alternator's voltage drifts\nhigher, it would try to carry more than its share.</p>\n\n<h3>The paralleling coil</h3>\n<p>A simple <b>vibrating-point paralleling system</b> puts a <b>paralleling coil</b> in\neach voltage regulator, connected to the output of each alternator (p. 9-42). The\nparalleling coils are <b>wired in series between the output terminals of both\nalternators</b> and work together with the <b>voltage coil</b> of each regulator. If the\ntwo outputs are unequal, current flows through the paralleling coils and nudges each\nregulator to <b>raise the low alternator and lower the high one</b> until the outputs\nmatch — automatically keeping the load split even.</p>\n\n<h3>Large-aircraft AC: GCUs, the BPCU, and bus-tie breakers</h3>\n<p>On large AC aircraft each generator has its own <b>generator control unit (GCU)</b>,\nand a <b>bus power control unit (BPCU)</b> oversees the whole bus network (p. 9-59):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The GCU handles that generator's <b>voltage regulation and protection</b> and opens\nits <b>generator breaker (GB)</b> to disconnect a failed generator.</li>\n<li>The BPCU commands the <b>bus-tie breakers (BTBs)</b> to reconfigure the buses — for\nexample, closing BTBs so a healthy generator (or the APU generator) can feed a bus whose\nown generator has failed.</li>\n<li>Normally each AC generator powers its <b>own bus independently</b>; the tie breakers\nlet the system reroute power around a failure so essential loads stay fed.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">Must-Know</span>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Paralleling = equalizing outputs so alternators/generators share the load equally.</b></li>\n<li>DC paralleling is simple: <b>same bus + same voltage &rarr; equal load share</b>; hold both within a <b>few tenths of a volt</b> (about <b>26.5&ndash;28 V DC</b>).</li>\n<li>A <b>paralleling coil</b> in each regulator, <b>wired in series between the two alternator outputs</b>, works with the voltage coil to keep them matched.</li>\n<li>Large AC aircraft: each generator has a <b>GCU</b> (regulation/protection, opens the <b>GB</b>); the <b>BPCU</b> runs the <b>bus-tie breakers (BTBs)</b> to reroute power around a failed generator.</li>\n</ul></div>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "Paralleling of alternators or generators is the process of:", "options": ["Converting their AC output to DC", "Connecting them in series to double the voltage", "Disconnecting the weaker one", "Equalizing their outputs so they share the electrical load equally"], "correct": 3, "short": "Equalizing outputs for equal load sharing.", "expl": "Otherwise one unit would overheat carrying most of the load.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "Why is sharing the load equally between two alternators important?", "options": ["It increases the system voltage", "It reduces the aircraft's total electrical load", "An overloaded alternator would overheat and fail early", "It eliminates the need for a battery"], "correct": 2, "short": "Prevents one unit from overheating.", "expl": "Balanced load extends the life of both machines.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "On a light-twin DC system, two alternators share the load equally when they are connected to the same bus and:", "options": ["Are wired in series", "Produce the same output voltage", "Spin at different speeds", "Have different output voltages"], "correct": 1, "short": "Produce the same output voltage.", "expl": "Same bus + same voltage = equal load split.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "A DC paralleling system must hold the two alternator voltages within:", "options": ["Any range at all", "About 5 volts of each other", "A few tenths of a volt of each other", "About 20 volts of each other"], "correct": 2, "short": "A few tenths of a volt.", "expl": "Even a small voltage difference unbalances the load sharing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "On most twin-engine aircraft, the system voltage with the alternators operating is about:", "options": ["115 volts AC", "400 volts DC", "12 to 14 volts DC", "26.5 to 28 volts DC"], "correct": 3, "short": "26.5 to 28 volts DC.", "expl": "This is the normal operating band for the DC system.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "In a simple vibrating-point paralleling system, each voltage regulator contains a:", "options": ["Carbon pile only", "Paralleling coil connected to the alternator output", "Turn coordinator", "Second battery"], "correct": 1, "short": "A paralleling coil.", "expl": "It works together with the regulator's voltage coil.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "The paralleling coils of the two regulators are wired:", "options": ["Across the landing lights", "To the pitot heat circuit", "In series between the output terminals of both alternators", "In parallel with the battery only"], "correct": 2, "short": "In series between both alternator outputs.", "expl": "Unequal outputs drive current through them to rebalance the alternators.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "If one alternator's output voltage drifts higher than the other's, the paralleling system will:", "options": ["Increase the system frequency", "Nudge the regulators to raise the low unit and lower the high unit until they match", "Shut off both alternators", "Disconnect the battery"], "correct": 1, "short": "Rebalance the two outputs.", "expl": "This keeps the load split even automatically.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "On a large AC aircraft, each generator's voltage regulation and protection is handled by its own:", "options": ["Generator control unit (GCU)", "Paralleling coil", "Static inverter", "Transformer rectifier unit (TRU)"], "correct": 0, "short": "Generator control unit (GCU).", "expl": "The GCU also opens the generator breaker to disconnect a failed generator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "The GCU disconnects a failed generator by opening its:", "options": ["Bus-tie breaker (BTB)", "Battery relay", "Generator breaker (GB)", "Pitot heat switch"], "correct": 2, "short": "Generator breaker (GB).", "expl": "This isolates the bad generator from its bus.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "On a large AC aircraft, the unit that oversees the bus network and commands the bus-tie breakers is the:", "options": ["Air data computer", "Bus power control unit (BPCU)", "Turn coordinator", "Carbon-pile regulator"], "correct": 1, "short": "Bus power control unit (BPCU).", "expl": "It reconfigures the buses to route power around failures.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "Bus-tie breakers (BTBs) are used to:", "options": ["Reconfigure the buses so a healthy generator (or APU) can feed a bus whose own generator failed", "Regulate the alternator field current", "Convert AC to DC", "Sound the aural warning"], "correct": 0, "short": "Reroute power around a failed generator.", "expl": "The BPCU closes/opens BTBs to keep essential loads fed.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}, {"q": "During normal operation on a large multiengine AC aircraft, each AC generator typically:", "options": ["Powers its own bus independently", "Is wired in series with every other generator", "Is left disconnected", "Feeds only the battery"], "correct": 0, "short": "Powers its own bus independently.", "expl": "The tie breakers only reconnect buses when a failure requires it.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Paralleling & Load Sharing"}]}]}, {"n": 16, "day": "Final", "title": "Block 6 Comprehensive Final & Marathon", "ref": "Mixed exam · reuses the verified Unit 1-15 banks · FAA-H-8083-31B Ch.9/11/10", "sections": [{"id": "b6m13s1", "title": "Electrical Half Exam (45 Q — Ch.9)", "lesson": "\n<p>This is your <b>exam-prep marathon</b>. Every question here is drawn from the\nverified banks you already studied in Units 1&ndash;12 — no new material, just a big\nmixed test to prove you know it cold. Order and answer choices reshuffle every time,\nso you can retake as often as you like.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">How to use it</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Take the <b>Electrical Half</b> and <b>Avionics Half</b> to drill each domain.</li>\n<li>Finish with the <b>Comprehensive Final</b> — a 60-question mixed exam.</li>\n<li>You need <b>70%</b> to pass each, just like the block quiz. If you miss, re-read\nthe unit the question came from and retake.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Good luck — if you can pass the Comprehensive Final, you are ready for the Block 6\nsubject quiz.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The radius of bends in wire groups or bundles should be not less than ____ the outside diameter of the largest wire.", "options": ["25 times", "5 times", "10 times", "2 times"], "correct": 2, "short": "10 times.", "expl": "Minimum bend radius = 10× the largest wire's OD.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Three-phase power consists of three sine waves that are:", "options": ["180° apart", "In phase with each other", "90° apart", "120° apart"], "correct": 3, "short": "120° apart.", "expl": "Three overlapping waves 120° apart give smoother, more efficient power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Open wiring is defined as any wire, group, or bundle that is:", "options": ["Spliced", "Enclosed in conduit", "Shielded", "Not enclosed in conduit"], "correct": 3, "short": "Not enclosed in conduit.", "expl": "Open wiring = not in conduit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The distortion of the electron orbits within a capacitor's dielectric is called:", "options": ["Dielectric stress", "Impedance", "Hysteresis", "Back voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Dielectric stress.", "expl": "This distortion also stores electrostatic charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Battery capacity is measured quantitatively in:", "options": ["Volts", "Ampere-hours", "Watts", "Ohms"], "correct": 1, "short": "Ampere-hours.", "expl": "Ampere-hours at a specified discharge rate/cut-off voltage at room temperature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Methods used to mark directly on a wire include hot stamp, ink jet, and:", "options": ["Engraving chisel", "Laser jet printers", "Paint brush", "Adhesive tape only"], "correct": 1, "short": "Laser jet printers.", "expl": "Hot stamp, ink jet, and laser are the direct methods.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Large transport aircraft use redundant control computers for AC power in order to:", "options": ["Reduce weight", "Provide safety in the event of a system failure", "Eliminate the need for a battery", "Increase the frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Provide safety if one system fails.", "expl": "Redundancy keeps AC power controlled if a computer fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "The main difference between a DC generator and an AC generator is that the DC generator uses a ____ while the AC generator uses ____.", "options": ["commutator; slip rings", "carbon pile; a field frame", "diode; a rectifier", "slip rings; a commutator"], "correct": 0, "short": "Commutator vs. slip rings.", "expl": "Commutator = DC output; slip rings = AC output.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A relay is used to:", "options": ["Convert AC to DC", "Increase voltage", "Store electric charge", "Control a large current using a small current"], "correct": 3, "short": "Control large current with a small current.", "expl": "It also protects equipment and switches motors on/off.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The unit of inductance is the:", "options": ["Farad", "Ohm", "Henry", "Hertz"], "correct": 2, "short": "Henry (H).", "expl": "Inductance is measured in henrys.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Because landing and taxi lights are high-powered, they are usually switched by a:", "options": ["Diode", "Rotary switch", "Simple toggle switch alone", "Solenoid or relay"], "correct": 3, "short": "Solenoid or relay.", "expl": "High current is handled by a relay/solenoid; a faulty one can cause an inop light.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Electrical protection of aircraft wiring is provided by:", "options": ["Conduit", "Lacing cord", "Clamps and grommets", "Circuit breakers and fuses"], "correct": 3, "short": "Circuit breakers and fuses.", "expl": "These open the circuit on overcurrent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A switch that is spring-loaded and returns to its normal position when pressure is removed, typically SPDT or DPDT, is a:", "options": ["Precision (micro) switch", "Rotary switch", "Rocker switch", "Toggle switch"], "correct": 0, "short": "Precision (micro) switch.", "expl": "Spring-loaded, returns to normal; commonly SPDT/DPDT.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The knot used as the start knot when lacing a bundle is the:", "options": ["Clove hitch", "Bowline", "Square knot", "Half hitch only"], "correct": 0, "short": "Clove hitch.", "expl": "The clove hitch begins the lacing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "In a basic AC generator, the ends of the rotating loop connect to:", "options": ["A carbon pile", "The field frame", "Two slip (collector) rings", "A single commutator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Two slip (collector) rings.", "expl": "Brushes ride the slip rings (C1, C2) to take off the AC. A commutator is for DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC", "fig": "img/slide070_1.png"}, {"q": "An arcing fault between a wire and a metallic flammable-fluid line can:", "options": ["Puncture the line and result in a fire", "Only trip a breaker", "Have no consequence", "Improve grounding"], "correct": 0, "short": "Puncture the line and cause a fire.", "expl": "That's why wires are separated from fuel/oil/hydraulic/oxygen lines.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A NiCd cell delivers a nominal voltage during discharge of about:", "options": ["1.2 volts", "2.1 volts", "1.67 volts", "2.35 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.2 volts.", "expl": "NiCd nominal cell voltage = 1.2 V.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "The external power circuit allows:", "options": ["The alternator to be removed", "AC to be generated", "The battery to be bypassed permanently", "A ground power unit to be connected for starting/ground checks"], "correct": 3, "short": "A ground power unit to plug in.", "expl": "External power spares the battery for start/ground ops.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A series-wound generator's output voltage:", "options": ["Varies greatly with changes in load", "Stays perfectly constant", "Is unaffected by load", "Is always AC"], "correct": 0, "short": "Varies greatly with load.", "expl": "Because field current is the load current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Generator direction of rotation is viewed from the ____ and stated as clockwise or counterclockwise.", "options": ["driven (drive) end", "field frame", "brush end", "commutator end"], "correct": 0, "short": "The driven (drive) end.", "expl": "Rotation is referenced from the drive end; it may be stamped on the data plate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Automatic-reset circuit breakers:", "options": ["Must NOT be used as circuit-protection devices in aircraft", "Are the preferred protection for all aircraft circuits", "Are required on the battery bus", "Are the same as trip-free breakers"], "correct": 0, "short": "Prohibited as aircraft circuit protection.", "expl": "They reset themselves, which could re-energize a faulted circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "Which control unit handles AC generator functions such as voltage regulation and frequency control?", "options": ["GCU (Generator Control Unit)", "BPCU", "CSD", "TRU"], "correct": 0, "short": "GCU.", "expl": "The GCU controls the generator; the BPCU controls distribution.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Aircraft wire insulation is selected primarily based on:", "options": ["The length of the run only", "The color of the wire", "Its dielectric strength/resistance and the environmental conditions", "The gauge number only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Dielectric strength/resistance and environment.", "expl": "Tefzel and similar insulations are chosen for the conditions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Which DC generator produces a relatively constant voltage over a range of loads?", "options": ["Parallel (shunt)-wound", "Series-only", "Series-wound", "Reverse-wound"], "correct": 0, "short": "Parallel (shunt)-wound.", "expl": "A shunt field gives fairly steady voltage as load changes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The state of charge of a lead-acid cell is measured with a hydrometer, which reads:", "options": ["Cell voltage", "Internal resistance", "Specific gravity of the electrolyte", "Cell temperature"], "correct": 2, "short": "Specific gravity of the electrolyte.", "expl": "Higher specific gravity = more charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "AC alternators are found on aircraft that:", "options": ["Have no engine-driven power", "Use very little electrical power", "Use a large amount of electrical power (transport aircraft)", "Operate only on battery power"], "correct": 2, "short": "Use a large amount of electrical power.", "expl": "Transport aircraft like the 757 and A-380.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Bundle slack (sag) between supports should normally not exceed a maximum deflection of:", "options": ["1 inch", "2 inches", "1/2 inch", "1/8 inch"], "correct": 2, "short": "1/2 inch.", "expl": "Up to about 1/2 inch deflection under normal hand force.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The two types of wire (both insulated) used on aircraft are:", "options": ["Solid and stranded", "AC and DC", "Bare and coated", "Copper and Tefzel"], "correct": 0, "short": "Solid and stranded.", "expl": "Stranded is preferred on aircraft for flexibility/vibration resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Frequency is defined as:", "options": ["The peak-to-peak voltage", "The distance between two waves", "The number of cycles in one second", "The time for one cycle"], "correct": 2, "short": "Number of cycles per second.", "expl": "Measured in hertz (Hz).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The overriding rule for DC generator maintenance is to:", "options": ["Use any generic procedure", "Always follow the manufacturer's instructions", "Charge it like a battery", "Never inspect the brushes"], "correct": 1, "short": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions.", "expl": "Manufacturer data governs generator maintenance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The diode feeding a dual-feed bus:", "options": ["Allows current to flow in only one direction", "Converts AC to DC for the whole aircraft", "Blocks all current", "Allows current to flow both directions"], "correct": 0, "short": "Allows current one direction only.", "expl": "That's the defining property of a diode.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Within a three-unit regulator, the voltage regulator controls output voltage by:", "options": ["Changing engine RPM", "Opening the load circuit", "Rectifying the output", "Varying the generator field current"], "correct": 3, "short": "Varying the field current.", "expl": "The voltage-regulator unit adjusts field current to hold voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide110_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "Before servicing a strobe light system, a technician should allow it to sit for at least:", "options": ["5 minutes", "30 seconds", "1 minute", "1 hour"], "correct": 0, "short": "5 minutes.", "expl": "Time for the capacitors to discharge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Auxiliary power for a large aircraft's electrical system on the ground is commonly provided by the:", "options": ["APU (Auxiliary Power Unit)", "CSD", "Inverter", "RAT"], "correct": 0, "short": "APU.", "expl": "The APU can drive a generator for ground and backup power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Grounding is defined as connecting conductive material to the aircraft primary structure to:", "options": ["Establish a return path for current, completing the circuit", "Insulate the wire", "Store static charge", "Increase resistance"], "correct": 0, "short": "Establish the current return path.", "expl": "Ground completes the circuit back to the source.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A circuit protection device should open the circuit:", "options": ["Only if the pilot commands it", "After the wiring emits smoke", "Never — it just warns", "Before the wiring or equipment overheats to the point of emitting smoke"], "correct": 3, "short": "Before it overheats to the point of emitting smoke.", "expl": "Protection must act before damage/fire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The only accurate way to determine the state of charge of a NiCd battery is:", "options": ["Reading specific gravity with a hydrometer", "Feeling the case temperature", "A measured discharge with a NiCd battery charger", "Measuring open-circuit voltage"], "correct": 2, "short": "A measured discharge with a NiCd charger.", "expl": "NiCd state of charge can't be read by hydrometer or voltage alone.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Indirect marking is accomplished by printing on a ____ and installing it on the wire's outer covering.", "options": ["heat-shrinkable sleeve", "metal band", "paper tag", "clamp"], "correct": 0, "short": "Heat-shrinkable sleeve.", "expl": "The printed sleeve is shrunk onto the wire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Which of the following is typically a SWAMP area?", "options": ["A wheel well", "The overhead reading light", "The instrument panel", "The center console"], "correct": 0, "short": "A wheel well.", "expl": "Wheel wells, wing flap areas, wing folds, and pylons are SWAMP areas.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The three units of a three-unit regulator are the voltage regulator, the current limiter, and the:", "options": ["Constant-speed drive", "Reverse-current relay", "Inverter", "Carbon pile"], "correct": 1, "short": "Reverse-current relay.", "expl": "Voltage regulator + current limiter + reverse-current relay.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide108_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "A picofarad (pF) equals:", "options": ["10⁶ F", "10⁻⁶ F", "10⁻³ F", "10⁻¹² F"], "correct": 3, "short": "10⁻¹² F.", "expl": "pF = one-trillionth of a farad; µF = 10⁻⁶.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "A circuit breaker differs from a fuse mainly because a circuit breaker:", "options": ["Can be reset after it trips", "Only works on AC", "Cannot open a circuit", "Melts a metal strip permanently"], "correct": 0, "short": "Can be reset.", "expl": "A breaker is a resettable automatic switch; a fuse must be replaced.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "If the resistance of a circuit is doubled while voltage is held constant, the current:", "options": ["Quadruples", "Stays the same", "Doubles", "Is cut in half"], "correct": 3, "short": "Is cut in half.", "expl": "I = E/R; doubling R halves I when E is constant.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "In a purely resistive AC circuit (no reactance), the power factor is:", "options": ["1", "1.414", "0.707", "0"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.", "expl": "Phase angle is 0°, so true power equals apparent power and PF = 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The effective (RMS) value of AC equals:", "options": ["2 × peak", "0.5 × peak", "0.707 × peak", "1.414 × peak"], "correct": 2, "short": "0.707 × peak.", "expl": "RMS = 0.707 × peak; conversely peak = 1.414 × RMS. RMS does the same work as equal DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}]}, {"id": "b6m13s2", "title": "Avionics Half Exam (45 Q — Ch.11/10)", "lesson": "\n<p>This is your <b>exam-prep marathon</b>. Every question here is drawn from the\nverified banks you already studied in Units 1&ndash;12 — no new material, just a big\nmixed test to prove you know it cold. Order and answer choices reshuffle every time,\nso you can retake as often as you like.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">How to use it</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Take the <b>Electrical Half</b> and <b>Avionics Half</b> to drill each domain.</li>\n<li>Finish with the <b>Comprehensive Final</b> — a 60-question mixed exam.</li>\n<li>You need <b>70%</b> to pass each, just like the block quiz. If you miss, re-read\nthe unit the question came from and retake.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Good luck — if you can pass the Comprehensive Final, you are ready for the Block 6\nsubject quiz.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The term 'glass cockpit' refers to:", "options": ["Windshields made of tempered glass", "The use of flat-panel display screens with computer-produced images replacing mechanical gauges", "Anti-ice heated windows", "A pressurized cabin"], "correct": 1, "short": "Flat-panel displays replacing mechanical gauges.", "expl": "Computer images replace individual analog instruments.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "An EICAS uses a two-monitor, two-computer arrangement in which:", "options": ["Both monitors get information from the same computer, and the second computer is a standby", "There is only one computer and no backup", "Each monitor has its own dedicated primary computer", "The computers only work on the ground"], "correct": 0, "short": "One active computer feeds both; the other is standby.", "expl": "This gives redundancy if the active computer fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "GPS uses which satellites, maintained by the U.S. government?", "options": ["WAAS", "TACAN", "GOES", "NAVSTAR"], "correct": 3, "short": "NAVSTAR.", "expl": "GPS is based on the NAVSTAR satellite constellation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Avionics installations should be planned to allow:", "options": ["Maximum vibration", "Water to pool on the unit", "Easy access for inspection, maintenance, and exchange of units", "The unit to be sealed in permanently"], "correct": 2, "short": "Easy access for inspection/maintenance/exchange.", "expl": "Accessibility is a key planning factor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "On an annunciator panel, YELLOW (amber) is used to indicate:", "options": ["The aircraft is on the ground", "A caution — a serious condition that requires further monitoring", "A completely normal condition", "An immediate emergency requiring action now"], "correct": 1, "short": "Caution — monitor further.", "expl": "Amber means watch it; it is between green and red.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "The Electronic Centralized Aircraft Monitor (ECAM) automatically:", "options": ["Flies the aircraft", "Regulates the generators", "Performs monitoring duties and alerts the pilot with aural and visual cues when a failure occurs", "Transmits the ELT signal"], "correct": 2, "short": "Monitors and alerts the pilot on failures.", "expl": "ECAM watches systems and cues the pilot on problems.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The flight director's command bar is displayed on the:", "options": ["Attitude indicator (ADI/EADI)", "Transponder", "DME readout", "Fuel gauge"], "correct": 0, "short": "The attitude indicator (ADI/EADI).", "expl": "The command bar shows required attitude change.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The Wide Area Augmentation System (WAAS) is used to:", "options": ["Refine aircraft GPS positions in the USA for greater accuracy", "Transmit emergency signals", "Replace GPS entirely", "Provide weather radar"], "correct": 0, "short": "Refine GPS positions in the USA.", "expl": "WAAS augments/improves GPS accuracy.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "A yaw damper receives its error signals from the:", "options": ["Turn-coordinator rate gyro", "Compass card", "Fuel-flow sensor", "Altimeter"], "correct": 0, "short": "Turn-coordinator rate gyro.", "expl": "It automatically counters oscillation about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "A triode vacuum tube contains how many elements?", "options": ["Four", "Three (cathode, plate, grid)", "Two", "Five"], "correct": 1, "short": "Three (cathode, plate, grid).", "expl": "Triode = 3 elements; used to control large current with a smaller one.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A disadvantage of AM is that it is:", "options": ["Susceptible to atmospheric and static noise", "Unable to travel long distances", "Impossible to demodulate", "Limited to digital data"], "correct": 0, "short": "Susceptible to atmospheric/static noise.", "expl": "AM carries long distance but picks up noise.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Among the four ECAM modes (flight phase, advisory, failure-related, manual), the mode that takes precedence over all others is:", "options": ["Flight phase mode", "Manual mode", "Failure-related mode", "Advisory mode"], "correct": 2, "short": "Failure-related mode.", "expl": "It overrides whatever mode is selected when a failure occurs.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "Which is one of the three common types of weather aids in a flight deck?", "options": ["On-board radar for detecting weather", "Transponder", "Marker beacon", "Altitude encoder"], "correct": 0, "short": "On-board weather radar.", "expl": "Also lightning detectors and uploaded satellite weather.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The simplest single-axis autopilot found on small aircraft is a:", "options": ["Full three-axis digital AFCS", "Wing leveler that is vacuum-operated and controls only the ailerons", "Yaw damper controlling only the rudder", "Flight management computer"], "correct": 1, "short": "Vacuum-operated wing leveler (ailerons only).", "expl": "Its sensing element is the turn coordinator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "ADS-B was made possible by the success of:", "options": ["Global navigation satellite systems (GNSS) such as GPS", "LORAN towers", "Vacuum-tube electronics", "Marker beacons"], "correct": 0, "short": "GNSS/GPS.", "expl": "ADS-B is built on satellite positioning.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An exclusive-OR (XOR) gate differs from an OR gate in that its output is 0 when:", "options": ["Only one input is 1", "Both inputs are 1", "Any input is 1", "Both inputs are 0"], "correct": 1, "short": "Both inputs are 1.", "expl": "XOR = 1 only when inputs differ; 0 when both are 1 (or both 0).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "An OR gate produces an output of Logic 1 when:", "options": ["The inputs are equal", "Both inputs are 0", "All inputs are 1", "At least one input is 1"], "correct": 3, "short": "At least one input is 1.", "expl": "Any high input makes an OR output high.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A VOR transmitter produces two signals: a reference signal and a:", "options": ["Variable signal (electronically rotated)", "406 MHz beacon signal", "150 Hz side signal", "Modulated glideslope signal"], "correct": 0, "short": "A variable (rotating) signal.", "expl": "The receiver compares reference vs. variable to find the bearing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "DME calculates the distance from the aircraft to the DME unit at the:", "options": ["Localizer antenna", "VORTAC ground station", "Nearest NDB", "Middle marker"], "correct": 1, "short": "VORTAC ground station.", "expl": "DME shows slant distance to the ground station.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Most autopilot systems consist of how many basic components?", "options": ["Four", "Three", "Six", "Two"], "correct": 0, "short": "Four.", "expl": "Sensing, computing, output, and command elements.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "Radio waves that bounce off the ionosphere to travel long distances are:", "options": ["Ground waves", "Micro waves", "Sky waves", "Space waves"], "correct": 2, "short": "Sky waves.", "expl": "HF sky waves reflect off the ionosphere.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "A rate-based autopilot moves the control surfaces based on:", "options": ["The memorized attitude only", "The radio frequency in use", "The cabin altitude", "The aircraft's rate of movement, sensed by a rate gyro"], "correct": 3, "short": "Rate of movement (rate gyro).", "expl": "It counters the rate of change rather than returning to a fixed position.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "Besides distance, DME can also display calculated:", "options": ["Fuel remaining", "Wind direction", "Cabin altitude", "Aircraft speed and elapsed time"], "correct": 3, "short": "Speed and elapsed time.", "expl": "DME can compute groundspeed and time to the station.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Which is one of the four tools for reducing radio interference?", "options": ["Static discharge wicks", "Adding fuel", "Painting", "Increasing frequency"], "correct": 0, "short": "Static discharge wicks.", "expl": "Shielding, isolation, bonding, and static discharge wicks.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "ADS-B OUT broadcasts GPS position combined with on-board flight status such as:", "options": ["Fuel type", "Passenger count", "Altitude, velocity, and time", "Engine oil pressure"], "correct": 2, "short": "Altitude, velocity, and time.", "expl": "ADS-B OUT = position + status broadcast.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "TCAS I monitors traffic within a range of about:", "options": ["5–10 miles", "100 miles", "35–40 miles", "200 miles"], "correct": 2, "short": "35–40 miles.", "expl": "TCAS I range is 35–40 mi.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An altitude encoder converts the aircraft's ____ into a code sent by the transponder.", "options": ["indicated airspeed", "heading", "pressure altitude", "vertical speed"], "correct": 2, "short": "Pressure altitude.", "expl": "The encoder digitizes pressure altitude for the transponder.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Which is an example of a condition a transport-category aural warning system alerts for?", "options": ["A normal cruise altitude", "An engine or wheel-well fire", "A properly closed cabin door", "A satisfactory generator load"], "correct": 1, "short": "Engine/wheel-well fire (among others).", "expl": "It also covers abnormal takeoff/landing, pressurization, Mach, crew-call, and collision advisories.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "A rectifier circuit changes:", "options": ["AC voltage into DC voltage", "Analog into digital", "Low voltage into high voltage only", "DC voltage into AC voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "AC into DC.", "expl": "Rectifier = AC→DC; one of the most common aircraft electronic circuits.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "LORAN is a navigation system that is:", "options": ["A type of RNAV no longer available in the United States", "The fastest-growing type of navigation", "Satellite-based", "Required on all transport aircraft"], "correct": 0, "short": "A discontinued (RNAV) system in the U.S.", "expl": "GPS made LORAN redundant.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The fundamental basis for autopilot system operation is:", "options": ["Error correction — canceling any deviation from the selected flight condition", "Amplifying the pilot's control forces", "Storing flight plans in a database", "Converting AC power to DC for the servos"], "correct": 0, "short": "Error correction.", "expl": "The autopilot moves the controls to cancel any error between the aircraft's actual and selected condition.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "The three characteristics of an antenna are:", "options": ["Length, polarization, and directivity", "AM, FM, and SSB", "Voltage, current, and resistance", "Ground, sky, and space"], "correct": 0, "short": "Length, polarization, directivity.", "expl": "These define antenna behavior.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The radio altimeter is used primarily during approach and low-level/night flight below:", "options": ["500 feet", "10,000 feet", "2,500 feet", "1,000 feet"], "correct": 2, "short": "2,500 feet.", "expl": "Radio altimeter is used below 2,500 ft, supplying decision height.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "For a NOR gate with inputs A=0 and B=0, the output is:", "options": ["1", "Undefined", "Depends on voltage", "0"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.", "expl": "OR(0,0)=0, inverted by NOR = 1.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Altitude is usually reported to ATC in increments of:", "options": ["10 feet", "1,000 feet", "500 feet", "100 feet"], "correct": 3, "short": "100 feet.", "expl": "Encoded altitude increments are 100 ft.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "RNAV systems make use of:", "options": ["Marker beacons only", "Glideslope signals", "Waypoints (designated geographical locations/points)", "The magnetic compass only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Waypoints.", "expl": "RNAV routes are defined by waypoints; GPS greatly expanded RNAV.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The localizer portion of the ILS provides:", "options": ["Horizontal guidance to the runway centerline", "Vertical guidance", "Altitude above terrain", "Distance to the runway"], "correct": 0, "short": "Horizontal guidance to centerline.", "expl": "Localizer = lateral/horizontal guidance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "A transceiver is a radio that:", "options": ["Transmits and receives, using the same frequency for both", "Only receives", "Only transmits", "Converts AC to DC"], "correct": 0, "short": "Transmits and receives (same frequency).", "expl": "Transmit + receive in one unit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Even though avionics repair is specialized, the airframe technician must competently:", "options": ["Manufacture replacement circuit boards", "Redesign the avionics", "Certify the software", "Remove, install, inspect, maintain, and troubleshoot avionics"], "correct": 3, "short": "Remove, install, inspect, maintain, troubleshoot.", "expl": "The A&P handles installation and general upkeep.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "A yaw damper is used to correct an aircraft's tendency to:", "options": ["Pitch up and down", "Roll into a spiral", "Lose altitude", "Oscillate around its vertical axis (Dutch roll) on a fixed heading"], "correct": 3, "short": "Oscillate about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).", "expl": "The yaw damper automatically counters that yaw oscillation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "A NAND gate is a combination of which two gates?", "options": ["OR and NOT", "AND and OR", "NOR and XOR", "AND and NOT"], "correct": 3, "short": "AND and NOT.", "expl": "NAND = inverted AND.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "A distinctive EICAS feature is that it automatically:", "options": ["Recharges the battery", "Flies the approach", "Files the FAA Form 337", "Records the parameters of a failure event for later review by maintenance"], "correct": 3, "short": "Auto-records failure-event parameters.", "expl": "The pilot can also press an event-record button for a suspected problem.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "TCAS is best described as a system that is:", "options": ["Satellite-based positioning", "An emergency locator", "Transponder-based and air-to-air", "Ground-based and radio-navigation only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Transponder-based, air-to-air.", "expl": "TCAS monitors nearby transponder-equipped traffic.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An annunciator panel is:", "options": ["The autopilot's servo unit", "The aircraft's static discharge system", "A panel of labeled warning lights in plain sight of the pilot, covered with colored lenses", "A device that converts AC to DC"], "correct": 2, "short": "A panel of labeled colored warning lights.", "expl": "It displays current conditions and notifies of unsatisfactory ones.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "In an AC circuit, a diode acts as a:", "options": ["Amplifier", "Storage cell", "Oscillator", "Check valve, allowing current during half the AC cycle"], "correct": 3, "short": "Check valve (conducts half the AC cycle).", "expl": "A diode passes current one way, blocking the other half-cycle.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}]}, {"id": "b6m13s3", "title": "Comprehensive Final Exam (60 Q — all units)", "lesson": "\n<p>This is your <b>exam-prep marathon</b>. Every question here is drawn from the\nverified banks you already studied in Units 1&ndash;12 — no new material, just a big\nmixed test to prove you know it cold. Order and answer choices reshuffle every time,\nso you can retake as often as you like.</p>\n<div class=\"box key\"><span class=\"bt\">How to use it</span>\n<ul>\n<li>Take the <b>Electrical Half</b> and <b>Avionics Half</b> to drill each domain.</li>\n<li>Finish with the <b>Comprehensive Final</b> — a 60-question mixed exam.</li>\n<li>You need <b>70%</b> to pass each, just like the block quiz. If you miss, re-read\nthe unit the question came from and retake.</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>Good luck — if you can pass the Comprehensive Final, you are ready for the Block 6\nsubject quiz.</p>\n", "quiz": [{"q": "The radius of bends in wire groups or bundles should be not less than ____ the outside diameter of the largest wire.", "options": ["25 times", "5 times", "10 times", "2 times"], "correct": 2, "short": "10 times.", "expl": "Minimum bend radius = 10× the largest wire's OD.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "Three-phase power consists of three sine waves that are:", "options": ["180° apart", "In phase with each other", "90° apart", "120° apart"], "correct": 3, "short": "120° apart.", "expl": "Three overlapping waves 120° apart give smoother, more efficient power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Open wiring is defined as any wire, group, or bundle that is:", "options": ["Spliced", "Enclosed in conduit", "Shielded", "Not enclosed in conduit"], "correct": 3, "short": "Not enclosed in conduit.", "expl": "Open wiring = not in conduit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The term 'glass cockpit' refers to:", "options": ["Windshields made of tempered glass", "The use of flat-panel display screens with computer-produced images replacing mechanical gauges", "Anti-ice heated windows", "A pressurized cabin"], "correct": 1, "short": "Flat-panel displays replacing mechanical gauges.", "expl": "Computer images replace individual analog instruments.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "The distortion of the electron orbits within a capacitor's dielectric is called:", "options": ["Dielectric stress", "Impedance", "Hysteresis", "Back voltage"], "correct": 0, "short": "Dielectric stress.", "expl": "This distortion also stores electrostatic charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "Battery capacity is measured quantitatively in:", "options": ["Volts", "Ampere-hours", "Watts", "Ohms"], "correct": 1, "short": "Ampere-hours.", "expl": "Ampere-hours at a specified discharge rate/cut-off voltage at room temperature.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Methods used to mark directly on a wire include hot stamp, ink jet, and:", "options": ["Engraving chisel", "Laser jet printers", "Paint brush", "Adhesive tape only"], "correct": 1, "short": "Laser jet printers.", "expl": "Hot stamp, ink jet, and laser are the direct methods.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "An EICAS uses a two-monitor, two-computer arrangement in which:", "options": ["Both monitors get information from the same computer, and the second computer is a standby", "There is only one computer and no backup", "Each monitor has its own dedicated primary computer", "The computers only work on the ground"], "correct": 0, "short": "One active computer feeds both; the other is standby.", "expl": "This gives redundancy if the active computer fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "Large transport aircraft use redundant control computers for AC power in order to:", "options": ["Reduce weight", "Provide safety in the event of a system failure", "Eliminate the need for a battery", "Increase the frequency"], "correct": 1, "short": "Provide safety if one system fails.", "expl": "Redundancy keeps AC power controlled if a computer fails.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "GPS uses which satellites, maintained by the U.S. government?", "options": ["WAAS", "TACAN", "GOES", "NAVSTAR"], "correct": 3, "short": "NAVSTAR.", "expl": "GPS is based on the NAVSTAR satellite constellation.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Avionics installations should be planned to allow:", "options": ["Maximum vibration", "Water to pool on the unit", "Easy access for inspection, maintenance, and exchange of units", "The unit to be sealed in permanently"], "correct": 2, "short": "Easy access for inspection/maintenance/exchange.", "expl": "Accessibility is a key planning factor.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Installation"}, {"q": "On an annunciator panel, YELLOW (amber) is used to indicate:", "options": ["The aircraft is on the ground", "A caution — a serious condition that requires further monitoring", "A completely normal condition", "An immediate emergency requiring action now"], "correct": 1, "short": "Caution — monitor further.", "expl": "Amber means watch it; it is between green and red.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Warnings & Cautions"}, {"q": "The main difference between a DC generator and an AC generator is that the DC generator uses a ____ while the AC generator uses ____.", "options": ["commutator; slip rings", "carbon pile; a field frame", "diode; a rectifier", "slip rings; a commutator"], "correct": 0, "short": "Commutator vs. slip rings.", "expl": "Commutator = DC output; slip rings = AC output.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A relay is used to:", "options": ["Convert AC to DC", "Increase voltage", "Store electric charge", "Control a large current using a small current"], "correct": 3, "short": "Control large current with a small current.", "expl": "It also protects equipment and switches motors on/off.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The unit of inductance is the:", "options": ["Farad", "Ohm", "Henry", "Hertz"], "correct": 2, "short": "Henry (H).", "expl": "Inductance is measured in henrys.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The Electronic Centralized Aircraft Monitor (ECAM) automatically:", "options": ["Flies the aircraft", "Regulates the generators", "Performs monitoring duties and alerts the pilot with aural and visual cues when a failure occurs", "Transmits the ELT signal"], "correct": 2, "short": "Monitors and alerts the pilot on failures.", "expl": "ECAM watches systems and cues the pilot on problems.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "Because landing and taxi lights are high-powered, they are usually switched by a:", "options": ["Diode", "Rotary switch", "Simple toggle switch alone", "Solenoid or relay"], "correct": 3, "short": "Solenoid or relay.", "expl": "High current is handled by a relay/solenoid; a faulty one can cause an inop light.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "Electrical protection of aircraft wiring is provided by:", "options": ["Conduit", "Lacing cord", "Clamps and grommets", "Circuit breakers and fuses"], "correct": 3, "short": "Circuit breakers and fuses.", "expl": "These open the circuit on overcurrent.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "The flight director's command bar is displayed on the:", "options": ["Attitude indicator (ADI/EADI)", "Transponder", "DME readout", "Fuel gauge"], "correct": 0, "short": "The attitude indicator (ADI/EADI).", "expl": "The command bar shows required attitude change.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "A switch that is spring-loaded and returns to its normal position when pressure is removed, typically SPDT or DPDT, is a:", "options": ["Precision (micro) switch", "Rotary switch", "Rocker switch", "Toggle switch"], "correct": 0, "short": "Precision (micro) switch.", "expl": "Spring-loaded, returns to normal; commonly SPDT/DPDT.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "The Wide Area Augmentation System (WAAS) is used to:", "options": ["Refine aircraft GPS positions in the USA for greater accuracy", "Transmit emergency signals", "Replace GPS entirely", "Provide weather radar"], "correct": 0, "short": "Refine GPS positions in the USA.", "expl": "WAAS augments/improves GPS accuracy.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "The knot used as the start knot when lacing a bundle is the:", "options": ["Clove hitch", "Bowline", "Square knot", "Half hitch only"], "correct": 0, "short": "Clove hitch.", "expl": "The clove hitch begins the lacing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A yaw damper receives its error signals from the:", "options": ["Turn-coordinator rate gyro", "Compass card", "Fuel-flow sensor", "Altimeter"], "correct": 0, "short": "Turn-coordinator rate gyro.", "expl": "It automatically counters oscillation about the vertical axis (Dutch roll).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "In a basic AC generator, the ends of the rotating loop connect to:", "options": ["A carbon pile", "The field frame", "Two slip (collector) rings", "A single commutator"], "correct": 2, "short": "Two slip (collector) rings.", "expl": "Brushes ride the slip rings (C1, C2) to take off the AC. A commutator is for DC.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC", "fig": "img/slide070_1.png"}, {"q": "An arcing fault between a wire and a metallic flammable-fluid line can:", "options": ["Puncture the line and result in a fire", "Only trip a breaker", "Have no consequence", "Improve grounding"], "correct": 0, "short": "Puncture the line and cause a fire.", "expl": "That's why wires are separated from fuel/oil/hydraulic/oxygen lines.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A NiCd cell delivers a nominal voltage during discharge of about:", "options": ["1.2 volts", "2.1 volts", "1.67 volts", "2.35 volts"], "correct": 0, "short": "1.2 volts.", "expl": "NiCd nominal cell voltage = 1.2 V.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "A triode vacuum tube contains how many elements?", "options": ["Four", "Three (cathode, plate, grid)", "Two", "Five"], "correct": 1, "short": "Three (cathode, plate, grid).", "expl": "Triode = 3 elements; used to control large current with a smaller one.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "The external power circuit allows:", "options": ["The alternator to be removed", "AC to be generated", "The battery to be bypassed permanently", "A ground power unit to be connected for starting/ground checks"], "correct": 3, "short": "A ground power unit to plug in.", "expl": "External power spares the battery for start/ground ops.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "A series-wound generator's output voltage:", "options": ["Varies greatly with changes in load", "Stays perfectly constant", "Is unaffected by load", "Is always AC"], "correct": 0, "short": "Varies greatly with load.", "expl": "Because field current is the load current.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "Generator direction of rotation is viewed from the ____ and stated as clockwise or counterclockwise.", "options": ["driven (drive) end", "field frame", "brush end", "commutator end"], "correct": 0, "short": "The driven (drive) end.", "expl": "Rotation is referenced from the drive end; it may be stamped on the data plate.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "A disadvantage of AM is that it is:", "options": ["Susceptible to atmospheric and static noise", "Unable to travel long distances", "Impossible to demodulate", "Limited to digital data"], "correct": 0, "short": "Susceptible to atmospheric/static noise.", "expl": "AM carries long distance but picks up noise.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "Among the four ECAM modes (flight phase, advisory, failure-related, manual), the mode that takes precedence over all others is:", "options": ["Flight phase mode", "Manual mode", "Failure-related mode", "Advisory mode"], "correct": 2, "short": "Failure-related mode.", "expl": "It overrides whatever mode is selected when a failure occurs.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "ECAM / EICAS / FMS"}, {"q": "Automatic-reset circuit breakers:", "options": ["Must NOT be used as circuit-protection devices in aircraft", "Are the preferred protection for all aircraft circuits", "Are required on the battery bus", "Are the same as trip-free breakers"], "correct": 0, "short": "Prohibited as aircraft circuit protection.", "expl": "They reset themselves, which could re-energize a faulted circuit.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Power Conversion"}, {"q": "Which is one of the three common types of weather aids in a flight deck?", "options": ["On-board radar for detecting weather", "Transponder", "Marker beacon", "Altitude encoder"], "correct": 0, "short": "On-board weather radar.", "expl": "Also lightning detectors and uploaded satellite weather.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Which control unit handles AC generator functions such as voltage regulation and frequency control?", "options": ["GCU (Generator Control Unit)", "BPCU", "CSD", "TRU"], "correct": 0, "short": "GCU.", "expl": "The GCU controls the generator; the BPCU controls distribution.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "Aircraft wire insulation is selected primarily based on:", "options": ["The length of the run only", "The color of the wire", "Its dielectric strength/resistance and the environmental conditions", "The gauge number only"], "correct": 2, "short": "Dielectric strength/resistance and environment.", "expl": "Tefzel and similar insulations are chosen for the conditions.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The simplest single-axis autopilot found on small aircraft is a:", "options": ["Full three-axis digital AFCS", "Wing leveler that is vacuum-operated and controls only the ailerons", "Yaw damper controlling only the rudder", "Flight management computer"], "correct": 1, "short": "Vacuum-operated wing leveler (ailerons only).", "expl": "Its sensing element is the turn coordinator.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}, {"q": "ADS-B was made possible by the success of:", "options": ["Global navigation satellite systems (GNSS) such as GPS", "LORAN towers", "Vacuum-tube electronics", "Marker beacons"], "correct": 0, "short": "GNSS/GPS.", "expl": "ADS-B is built on satellite positioning.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "An exclusive-OR (XOR) gate differs from an OR gate in that its output is 0 when:", "options": ["Only one input is 1", "Both inputs are 1", "Any input is 1", "Both inputs are 0"], "correct": 1, "short": "Both inputs are 1.", "expl": "XOR = 1 only when inputs differ; 0 when both are 1 (or both 0).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Which DC generator produces a relatively constant voltage over a range of loads?", "options": ["Parallel (shunt)-wound", "Series-only", "Series-wound", "Reverse-wound"], "correct": 0, "short": "Parallel (shunt)-wound.", "expl": "A shunt field gives fairly steady voltage as load changes.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The state of charge of a lead-acid cell is measured with a hydrometer, which reads:", "options": ["Cell voltage", "Internal resistance", "Specific gravity of the electrolyte", "Cell temperature"], "correct": 2, "short": "Specific gravity of the electrolyte.", "expl": "Higher specific gravity = more charge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "AC alternators are found on aircraft that:", "options": ["Have no engine-driven power", "Use very little electrical power", "Use a large amount of electrical power (transport aircraft)", "Operate only on battery power"], "correct": 2, "short": "Use a large amount of electrical power.", "expl": "Transport aircraft like the 757 and A-380.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Alternators & AC Power"}, {"q": "An OR gate produces an output of Logic 1 when:", "options": ["The inputs are equal", "Both inputs are 0", "All inputs are 1", "At least one input is 1"], "correct": 3, "short": "At least one input is 1.", "expl": "Any high input makes an OR output high.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Avionics Fundamentals & Digital"}, {"q": "Bundle slack (sag) between supports should normally not exceed a maximum deflection of:", "options": ["1 inch", "2 inches", "1/2 inch", "1/8 inch"], "correct": 2, "short": "1/2 inch.", "expl": "Up to about 1/2 inch deflection under normal hand force.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "The two types of wire (both insulated) used on aircraft are:", "options": ["Solid and stranded", "AC and DC", "Bare and coated", "Copper and Tefzel"], "correct": 0, "short": "Solid and stranded.", "expl": "Stranded is preferred on aircraft for flexibility/vibration resistance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A VOR transmitter produces two signals: a reference signal and a:", "options": ["Variable signal (electronically rotated)", "406 MHz beacon signal", "150 Hz side signal", "Modulated glideslope signal"], "correct": 0, "short": "A variable (rotating) signal.", "expl": "The receiver compares reference vs. variable to find the bearing.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Frequency is defined as:", "options": ["The peak-to-peak voltage", "The distance between two waves", "The number of cycles in one second", "The time for one cycle"], "correct": 2, "short": "Number of cycles per second.", "expl": "Measured in hertz (Hz).", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Theory & AC"}, {"q": "The overriding rule for DC generator maintenance is to:", "options": ["Use any generic procedure", "Always follow the manufacturer's instructions", "Charge it like a battery", "Never inspect the brushes"], "correct": 1, "short": "Follow the manufacturer's instructions.", "expl": "Manufacturer data governs generator maintenance.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls"}, {"q": "The diode feeding a dual-feed bus:", "options": ["Allows current to flow in only one direction", "Converts AC to DC for the whole aircraft", "Blocks all current", "Allows current to flow both directions"], "correct": 0, "short": "Allows current one direction only.", "expl": "That's the defining property of a diode.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Within a three-unit regulator, the voltage regulator controls output voltage by:", "options": ["Changing engine RPM", "Opening the load circuit", "Rectifying the output", "Varying the generator field current"], "correct": 3, "short": "Varying the field current.", "expl": "The voltage-regulator unit adjusts field current to hold voltage.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "DC Generators & Controls", "fig": "img/slide110_1.jpeg"}, {"q": "Before servicing a strobe light system, a technician should allow it to sit for at least:", "options": ["5 minutes", "30 seconds", "1 minute", "1 hour"], "correct": 0, "short": "5 minutes.", "expl": "Time for the capacitors to discharge.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Lighting"}, {"q": "DME calculates the distance from the aircraft to the DME unit at the:", "options": ["Localizer antenna", "VORTAC ground station", "Nearest NDB", "Middle marker"], "correct": 1, "short": "VORTAC ground station.", "expl": "DME shows slant distance to the ground station.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Navigation"}, {"q": "Most autopilot systems consist of how many basic components?", "options": ["Four", "Three", "Six", "Two"], "correct": 0, "short": "Four.", "expl": "Sensing, computing, output, and command elements.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot & EFIS"}, {"q": "Auxiliary power for a large aircraft's electrical system on the ground is commonly provided by the:", "options": ["APU (Auxiliary Power Unit)", "CSD", "Inverter", "RAT"], "correct": 0, "short": "APU.", "expl": "The APU can drive a generator for ground and backup power.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Electrical Systems & Distribution"}, {"q": "Grounding is defined as connecting conductive material to the aircraft primary structure to:", "options": ["Establish a return path for current, completing the circuit", "Insulate the wire", "Store static charge", "Increase resistance"], "correct": 0, "short": "Establish the current return path.", "expl": "Ground completes the circuit back to the source.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "A circuit protection device should open the circuit:", "options": ["Only if the pilot commands it", "After the wiring emits smoke", "Never — it just warns", "Before the wiring or equipment overheats to the point of emitting smoke"], "correct": 3, "short": "Before it overheats to the point of emitting smoke.", "expl": "Protection must act before damage/fire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Termination, Bonding, Switches & Protection"}, {"q": "Radio waves that bounce off the ionosphere to travel long distances are:", "options": ["Ground waves", "Micro waves", "Sky waves", "Space waves"], "correct": 2, "short": "Sky waves.", "expl": "HF sky waves reflect off the ionosphere.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.11", "topic": "Radio Communication"}, {"q": "The only accurate way to determine the state of charge of a NiCd battery is:", "options": ["Reading specific gravity with a hydrometer", "Feeling the case temperature", "A measured discharge with a NiCd battery charger", "Measuring open-circuit voltage"], "correct": 2, "short": "A measured discharge with a NiCd charger.", "expl": "NiCd state of charge can't be read by hydrometer or voltage alone.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Batteries"}, {"q": "Indirect marking is accomplished by printing on a ____ and installing it on the wire's outer covering.", "options": ["heat-shrinkable sleeve", "metal band", "paper tag", "clamp"], "correct": 0, "short": "Heat-shrinkable sleeve.", "expl": "The printed sleeve is shrunk onto the wire.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.9", "topic": "Aircraft Wiring"}, {"q": "A rate-based autopilot moves the control surfaces based on:", "options": ["The memorized attitude only", "The radio frequency in use", "The cabin altitude", "The aircraft's rate of movement, sensed by a rate gyro"], "correct": 3, "short": "Rate of movement (rate gyro).", "expl": "It counters the rate of change rather than returning to a fixed position.", "cite": "FAA-H-8083-31B, Ch.10", "topic": "Autopilot Operation"}]}]}]};
